{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:25:49.867253+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_94a8becd0f1a0f22945505fb9a7caabb8ad8f8c7707dcd0c9691e3d33ed1cdf1","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm 1 of arXiv:2609.02040, '\Large Quantum Landau Damping with Coulomb Repulsion in the Whole Space'] Let $f_0$ be a solution of Equation~\eqref{eq:VP1} with initial data $f_0^\init \in L^1$ and $\op$ be a solution of Equation~\eqref{eq:Hartree_linear} with initial data $\op^\init$ a density operator with the same initial density $\rho_\h^\init := \rho_{\op^\init} = \rho_0^\init \in L^1$. Then, uniformly in $0 \le h \le 1$, the associated electric field defined in Equation~\eqref{eq:Electric_field} or~\eqref{eq:VP2} satisfies
		\begin{equation}
			\Nrm{E_\h(t,\cdot)}{L^\infty(\Rd)} \lesssim \weight{t}^{-3/2}
		\end{equation}
		and uniformly in $0 \le h \le 1$ and $t\in\R_+$, the position density satisfies
		\begin{equation}
			\Nrm{\rho_\h(t,\cdot) - \rho_0(t,\cdot)}{L^\infty} \lesssim h^2.
		\end{equation}
		If $f_0^\init \in L^1_xL^{6/5}_v$ and $\hd \tr\bigl(\sabs{\weight{x}^{3/2}\op^\init}^2+\sabs{\weight{x}^{3/2}\op^\init}^2\bigr)$ is bounded uniformly in $\hbar$, then the Wigner transform $f_\h$ of $\op$ satisfies

		\begin{equation}
			\Nrm{f_\h(t,\cdot) - f_0(t,\cdot)}{L^2} \leq 
			\sNrm{f_\h^\init - f_0^\init}{L^2} + C\, h^2 \weight{t} .
		\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:31:49.841326+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_94aae91d42651b4e8024310aecf23d6ae9368da15c9b8443fb9894c14e3e45a8","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Satz 3 of arXiv:2506.13661, 'Box-Covariances of Hyperuniform Point Processes'] Let $\eta$ be a hyperuniform point process satisfying the assumptions of Theorem \ref{thm:Cov_integrable} for $d\ge2$, Theorem \ref{thm:Cov_non_int} for $d=1$ and $a>0$, or that of Theorem \ref{thm:Cov_non_int_2} below. 
	If $\eta$ is Brillinger-mixing, then we have weak convergence in finite-dimensional distributions
	\begin{align}
		\Bigg(\frac{\eta(\Lambda_n(z))-n^d}{\sqrt{\mathrm{Var}\big(\eta(\Lambda_n)\big)}}\Bigg)_{z\in\mathbb{R}^d}{\underset{n\to\infty}\longrightarrow}\big(G(z)\big)_{z\in\mathbb{R}^d} 
	\end{align}
	towards a centered stationary Gaussian field $G$ with covariance function $\mathrm{cov}(z)$. In the case of $d=1$ with $a>0$, we obtain the increment process $G(z)=B_{a/2}(z+1)-B_{a/2}(z)$ of the fractional Brownian motion $B_{a/2}$ with Hurst index $a/2$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:07:48.565111+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_94ac7560fea400ccf2a4e751d800aafaf1105f7d8df1addb10e0583a5498d3bb","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 19 of arXiv:2609.01460, 'On cost-induced Santal\'o-type inequalities \\ in Polish measure spaces'] Let $m\in \N$ and let, for $i=1,\dots,m$, $(X_i,d_i,\mu_i)$ be Polish measure spaces and consider collections of analytic sets $\mathcal{A}_i\subset 2^{X_i}$. Let $-\infty \leq a<b \leq +\infty$ and let $\Phi:X_1\times\cdots \times X_m\to (a,b)$ be a continuous function.

    Then, the operator $\square f: (a,b) \to [0,\infty]$ given by
\begin{equation*}
\square f(r) = \sup_{\{x= (x_1,\dots,x_m) \colon \Phi(x) \geq r\} }\mathcal{M}_p^{(t)}(f), \quad -\infty < p \leq 1,
\end{equation*}
where $f\in \mathcal{F}\left(\mathcal{A}_1\right)\times \cdots\times \mathcal{F}\left(\mathcal{A}_m\right)$, is a generalized supremal convolution.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:30:14.222019+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_94ad35fedf3e9baffc62083ba22381f328e29f407321fb78af5e2f771a34108d","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:upper-heat of arXiv:2608.20537, 'Persistent anisotropy in multi-dimensional\\ branching Brownian motion'] Suppose $\beta$ satisfies (equation eq:beta-dot of the paper) and (equation eq:necessary of the paper).
