and introduce $\sigma(z)  =\sqrt{(z-\alpha_1)(z-\beta_1)(z-\alpha_2)(z-\beta_2)}$.  Assume that we have four functions $F(z)$, $A(z)$ (unknown), $E(z)$ and $s(z)$ (known) such that \eqref{eq_theeqa_intro} holds with $F(z) = O(\frac{1}{z^2})$ as $z \rightarrow \infty$, the functions $F(z),E(z)$ are holomorphic for $z \in \amsmathbb{C} \setminus \amsmathbb{A}$ while $s(z),A(z)$ are holomorphic in a neighborhood of $\amsmathbb{A}$ and $s(z)$ has no zeros in this neighborhood. Then, there is a unique solution $F(z) = \Op\big[\frac{E}{s}\big](z)$  satisfying the additional condition
