{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:36:16.061645+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_950085b45a3d1117c7bb074e3156242462ea5059fa3a8b16bfbd4161b45cccc2","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop: exact ruin brownian motion for collective model of arXiv:2606.28031, 'Insurance risk models in a heterogeneous time-dependent population: scaling limits and rui'] Let $X$ and $Y$ be two independent arithmetic Brownian motions. We assume the existence of $\rho$ and $\eta$ defined in Assumptions~(A:hyp LLN for V of the paper) and (A:hyp CLT for V of the paper). Set $\alpha_*:=2\frac{\rho {\mathrm c}_X+(1-\rho){\mathrm c}_Y}{\sigma_X^2\eta^2+\sigma_Y^2\left(\eta^2+1-2\rho\right)}$. 

Under the net profit condition $\rho{\mathrm c}_X+(1-\rho){\mathrm c}_Y>0$, for any $\alpha>\alpha_*$,
\begin{equation}
p(u)=\frac{{\mathrm e}^{-\alpha u}}{\mathbb{E}\left[{\mathrm e}^{-k_{\alpha}(\tau(u))}\,\big|\,\tau(u)<\infty\right]}.
