Consequently, whenever a strictly positive independent factor exists, its law is unique.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:17:13.075990+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_96d1d6844b2f88b38c3b16b4b5370532161c97d6c896dbeffc8b0ca7face3d0b","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop Laplacian-approximation-local of arXiv:2509.08928, 'The near critical random bond Ising model via embedding deformation'] Consider an s-embedding $\mathcal{S}^{(t)}(\Lambda_{n})$ constructed by Lemma (lem:SDE-embedding of the paper) and a real function $\phi$. Then, with $\mathbf{P}$-probability at least $1-O(n^{-4})$, there exist a constant $O=O(\omega,\alpha)>0$ such that for any $0\leq t \leq t^{(\alpha)}_n$,
