{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:03:39.776130+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9753cc1075ae12ea4c9a15b62731257ef290e2785993e0fbf9a9f3de793aeb9d","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defin of arXiv:2411.03065, 'Growing conditioned BGW trees with\\ log-concave offspring distributions'] An $\mathbb{X}$-\textit{shuffling rule} is a mapping $\psi\colon\trees^{\bullet}[\mathbb{X}]\to\mathfrak{S}$ such that for every $(T,{\boldsymbol{x}},\mathtt{u})\in\trees^{\bullet}[\mathbb{X}]$, its image $\psi(T,{\boldsymbol{x}},\mathtt{u})$ is an element of $\mathfrak{S}_{k_\mathtt{u}(T)}$.
