{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:17:13.072920+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_977b893c3eaa1d7f9c0aee8644c31387b01ac6d0b813809a889612190884a0d0","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:cQ-def of arXiv:2509.08928, 'The near critical random bond Ising model via embedding deformation'] Given $\mathcal{S} = \mathcal{S}_\mathcal{X}$, the \emph{origami} function, denoted by $\mathcal{Q} = \mathcal{Q}_\mathcal{X} : \Lambda(G) \to \mathbb{R}$, is defined (up to a global additive constant) as a real-valued function. Its increments between two neighboring vertices $v^{\bullet}(c)$ and $v^\circ(c)$ are given by
\begin{equation}

\mathcal{Q}(v^\bullet(c))-\mathcal{Q}(v^\circ(c))\ :=\ |\mathcal{X}(c)|^2\,=\,|\mathcal{S}(v^\bullet(c))-\mathcal{S}(v^\circ(c))|\,.
\end{equation}
We will often shorten $|\mathcal{X}(c)|^2=\delta_c$ the length of the edge of $\Lambda(G)$ attached to the corner $c$. The alternate sum of edge-lengths in a tangential quad vanishes, which ensures the consistent definition for $\mathcal{Q}$. One can see $\mathcal{Q}$ as a folding of the quadrilaterals along their diagonals (see e.g. [CLR1]), which makes $\mathcal{Q}$ a $1$-Lipschitz in the $\mathcal{S} $ plane.","status":"unverified"}

