{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:41:28.183178+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_99828e838ec0e459f980a1c12ec6908f9ecf78921f7e659d5ebd2d553bc07e42","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:nonrevup of arXiv:2511.10630, 'Cutoff for generalised Bernoulli--Laplace urn models'] Let $\varepsilon \in (0, 1]$. Suppose $\Phi_{\ast}^{(n)} \geqslant c_1$ for some constant $c_1 > 0$. For constant $\theta > 0$, let
    \begin{equation*}
        t_n = mn (t_{\textup{mix,\,single}}^{(n)}(1/\sqrt{n}) + \theta), \text{ and } t_n' = mn (t_{\textup{mix,\,single}}^{(n)}(1/\sqrt{n}) - \theta),
    \end{equation*}
    for $n \in \mathbb{N}$. Then there are constants $\theta, C > 0$ so that $\pi(\mathrm{Centre}(C)) \geqslant 1 - \varepsilon$, and the following hold.
    \begin{enumerate}[label=(\alph*)]
        \item For all $\mathbf{x} \in \Omega_n$ and $n$ large enough, we have
        \begin{equation*}
            \Ps{\mathbf{x}}{X^{(n)}_{t_n} \in {\mathrm{Centre}(C)}} > 1-\varepsilon.
        \end{equation*}
        \item We can find a sequence of starting states $(\mathbf{x}_n)_{n \geqslant 1}$ such that for $n$ large enough, we have
        \begin{equation*}
            \Ps{\mathbf{x}_n}{X^{(n)}_{t_n'} \in {\mathrm{Centre}(C)}} < \varepsilon.
        \end{equation*}
    \end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:50:30.568483+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_998f172352fe9bd0ffa3c5d733f9daf88f63828ed1b67cbca93edef7f3e375db","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Pro 6 of arXiv:2605.30779, '2605.30779'] Let \(\beta\in\{1,2\}\) and \(\{\xi_{\beta,j}\}_{j\ge1}\) be i.i.d.\ random variables, independent of \(\{a_{\beta,j}^{\Theta}\}_{j\ge1}\), with \(\xi_{\beta,j}^2\sim\chi_\beta^2\). Define the Airy-Green function
  \begin{equation}
    \mathcal G^{(\beta)}_{\Theta}(w)
    :=
    \lim_{x\to-\infty}
    \left[
      \sum_{j:\,a_{\beta,j}^{\Theta}>x}
      \frac{\beta^{-1}\xi_{\beta,j}^2}{w-a_{\beta,j}^{\Theta}}
      -\frac{2}{\pi}\sqrt{-x}
    \right].
  \end{equation}
  For every fixed finite vector \(\Theta\), the limit in \eqref{eq:defn_Airy_Green_multiple} exists almost surely for every
  \[
    w\in
    \mathbb C\setminus
    \bigl\{
    a_{\beta,j}^{\Theta}:j\ge1
    \bigr\},
  \]
  uniformly on compact subsets avoiding the poles. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:25:44.582721+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_99907ccf37e3eb8bddbfeffb62ebc71066bf7282da51379492ba328010a2816d","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm intro3 of arXiv:2509.08659, 'Gap metrics for stationary point processes and quantitative convexity of the free energy'] Let $\varphi(x)=|x|^{-s}$ for $0<s<1$ or $\varphi(x)=-\log|x|$.    Let $\mathsf{P}_0,\mathsf{P}_1$ be the laws of two stationary point processes with unit intensity with $\mathcal F^{elec}_{\beta}(\mathsf{P}_i)<\infty$, $i=0,1$. Then there exists a coupling $ \mathsf{Q}^0$ of $\mathsf{P}^0_0$ and $\mathsf{P}^0_1$ such that for $0\leq t\leq 1$ ($s=0$ corresponding to the $\log$ case)
    \begin{align*}
        \mathcal F^{elec}_{\beta}(\mathsf{P}_{t})&\leq  (1-t) \mathcal F^{elec}_{\beta}(\mathsf{P}_{0})+t\mathcal F^{elec}_{\beta}(\mathsf{P}_{1})\\
        &-\frac{(\mathbbm{1}_0(s)+s(s+1))(1-t)t\beta}{4}\int \sum_{(0,0)\neq (x,y)\in \bar\xi} (x-y)^2\inf_{z\in [x,y]}\left\lvertz\right\rvert^{-s-2} \mathsf{Q}^0(d\bar\xi).
    \end{align*}
In particular, $\mathcal F^{elec}_{\beta}$ has a unique minimizer. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:32:18.848625+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9994fc53647ed17c68dddedb31fcac5f86fab2b09695b8e30088a7da6df864a3","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop 8 (Maximum principle with one boundary for \eqref{moving frame-u}) of arXiv:2609.03869, '\bf Sharp asymptotics for a transport model with a nonlocal condition of the Fisher-KPP ty'] For any fixed $t_*\ge 0$ and for any  continuous  function $X(t,i)$ defined for $t\ge i+t_*$ and $i\in[0,i_\dagger)$  satisfying $X(t,i)$  for each $i\in[0,i_\dagger)$ is nondecreasing in $t\in(i+t_*,+\infty)$, and  $X(t,0)\ge \max_{s\in[0,i]}X(t-i+s,s)$, let $u(t,i,x)$ be a  bounded and continuous  function defined for $t\ge i+t_*$, $i\in[0,i_\dagger)$ and  $x\ge X(t,i)-R$,  such that
	\begin{equation}

		\begin{aligned}
			\left\{\begin{split}
				&	\partial_t u(t,i,x)  + \partial_i u(t,i,x) \le 0,~~~~~~~ ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~t\ge i+t_*, ~i\in(0,i_\dagger), ~x\ge X(t,i) -R, \\
				&	u(t,0,x)\le \S_0 \int_0^{i_\dagger} \omega(i) e^{-\alpha_*c_* i} \K_* *u(t,i,x)\md i,  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~t\ge  i_\dagger+t_*,   ~x\ge X(t,0).
			\end{split}\right.		
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation}
	Assume further that $u(t,0,x)\le0$ for $t\in [ t_*,i_\dagger+t_*]$ and $x\ge X(t,0)$, that\footnote{For this boundary assumption, it will be sufficient to assume: $u(t,i,x)\le 0$ for $t\ge i+t_*$, $i\in[0,i_\dagger)$ and $x\in[X(t,i)-R,X(t,i)]$.}  $u(t,0,x)\le 0$ for $t\ge t_*$ and $x\in[X(t,0)-R,X(t,0)]$, and $u(t,i,X(t,i))\le 0$ for $t\ge i+i_\dagger+t_*$ and $i\in[0,i_\dagger)$, then $u(t,i,x)\le 0$  for $t\ge i+i_\dagger+t_*$, $i\in[0,i_\dagger)$ and $x\ge X(t,i)$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:45:27.985228+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9998fe175bf2b3cd7e9504175e07505d72d180f9ce4108e9390477b2f28b6150","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:rec_map_prop of arXiv:2607.04514, 'Boundary-layer asymptotics for Gaussian-smoothed singular measures'] Assume (ass:standing of the paper)~(ass:standing:chart of the paper). Fix \(A>0\). There exists \(\sigma_0>0\) such that the map
