{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:58:40.873459+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9a78d2d12e93716a4ffb890f5978ac3a97e224978e11559f2fa12ae26c8fb504","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:basic-props of arXiv:2605.10617, 'Logarithmic scaling of selective sweep curves:\\ from tents to houses'] Let $Z=(Z(t), t \geq 0)$ be a continuous-time binary Galton--Watson process with birth rate $b$ and death rate $d$  being strictly positive such that $b \neq d$, and assume $Z(0)=1$. Then for $t\ge0 $
