{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:43:55.656995+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9a7e130a5d41922d2b194edf0d11883edce1b16bfa2b9a2577068d746a70390a","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm: transported Banach of arXiv:2604.05941, 'Banach spaces of continuous paths with finite $p$-th variation'] Fix $p>1$. For any path $x \in V^p_{\mathbb{T}}$ with linear dyadic $p$-th variation, consider its positive shift $\bar x$, given by (equation def: shifted x of the paper), satisfying (equation eq: linear variation x bar of the paper). Let $(B,\|\cdot\|_{B})$ be a Banach space such that $B \subset V^{p, 0}_{\mathbb{T}}$ in the notation of (equation def: tau p-th variation of the paper), i.e.,
    \begin{equation}    
        B \subset V^p_{\mathbb{T}} \qquad \text{and} \qquad [g]^{(p)}_{\mathbb{T}}\equiv 0 ~ \text{ for every } g\in B.
    \end{equation}
    Define $\mathcal L_{\bar x}(B) := \{ g \bar{x}: g\in B \} \subset C([0,1])$ and equip $\mathcal L_{\bar x}(B)$ with the norm
    \begin{equation}    
        \|y\|_{\mathcal L} := \|g\|_B = \Big\|\frac{y}{\bar x}\Big\|_{B}, \qquad y\in\mathcal L_{\bar x}(B).
    \end{equation}
    Then, the map $T : B \to \mathcal L_{\bar x}(B)$ defined by $T(g)=g\bar x$ is a linear isometric isomorphism. In particular, $\big(\mathcal L_{\bar x}(B),\|\cdot\|_{\mathcal L}\big)$ is a Banach space. Moreover, $\mathcal{L}_{\bar x}(B) \subset V^p_{\mathbb{T}}$ and for every $y=g\bar x\in\mathcal L_{\bar x}(B)$
    \begin{equation}    
        [y]^{(p)}_{\mathbb{T}}(t)=\int_0^t |g(u)|^p\,d[\bar x]^{(p)}_{\mathbb{T}}(u) = C_p\int_0^t |g(u)|^p\,du,\qquad \forall \, t\in[0,1].
    \end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:32:28.951913+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9a7ed7f180636f9b961ed808c53910b5f2227ba3d59c6c28c809a5273b18b4c5","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:Req of arXiv:2608.16915, 'Exact mean--covariance dynamics of the Weber field in the stochastic Lagrangian representa'] For a smooth solution of (equation eq:NS of the paper), the covariance field
