{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:09:43.711915+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9b7d83b90570a68d48bd5abccb1c4d271ae06901b2bb2511f8a3d4d6adc04d46","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma l.expdecay of arXiv:2605.28430, 'Limit theory for Lipschitz-localized statistics in random geometric models'] Let $Q_1, Q_2$ be two marking functions on finite marked point processes. Let $\tilde{\mathcal{P}}$ be a marked point process such that the ground process $\mathcal{P}$ satisfies the Palm correlation bound~(equation e:bd_palmcorr of the paper) for $m =1$.
 Suppose for $p\in\mathbb{N}$, $i=1,2$ that there are   fast decreasing functions $\varphi_{i,p}$ as in (equation varphibd of the paper) such that 
\begin{equation}
\sup_{1 \leq n < \infty} \sup_{x_1,\ldots,x_p \in W_n} \mathbb{P}_{\bk x1p}
\bigl(Q_i(\tilde{x}_1, \tilde{\mathcal{P}}_n)>r\bigr) \le \varphi_{i,p}(r),\quad r > 0.
\end{equation}
 Then 
for all $p \in \mathbb{N}$ there are fast decreasing functions ~$\tilde\varphi_p$ such that
$$
\sup_{1 \leq n < \infty} \sup_{x_1,\ldots,x_p\in W_n}\mathbb{P}_{\bk x1p}\left( \max_{x \in \mathcal{P}_n :  |x - x_1| \leq Q_1(\tilde{x}_1, \tilde{\mathcal{P}}_n)} Q_2(\tilde{x}, \tilde{\mathcal{P}}_n) > r \right) \leq   \tilde{\varphi}_p(r), \quad r > 0.
$$
If the assumption (equation e:Q12tails of the paper) holds with the supremum taken up to $n = \infty$, i.e., $\sup_{1 \le n \le \infty}$, then the conclusion  remains valid with  $\sup_{1 \le n \le \infty}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:17:10.011675+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9b8213c83c2f996be7059358a21572821aaeb1dfd9bf9840dc17f1868bcbb6ef","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 4 (Gaussian concentration) of arXiv:2602.14618, 'Finitary coding and Gaussian concentration\\ for random fields'] Let $d\geq 1$ and $Y=(Y_i)_{i\in\Zd}$ be a $B$-valued random field where $B$ is a standard Borel space. Then $Y$ satisfies a Gaussian concentration bound if there exists a constant $C>0$ such
that, for any local function $f:B^{\Zd}\to\R$ with the bounded-difference property, and for any $\lambda>0$,  one has
\begin{equation}
\log\E\big(\e^{\lambda (f(Y)-\E[f(Y)])}\big)\leq \frac{\sC}{2} \lambda^2 \|\delta f\|_2^2\,.    
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:15:16.643041+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9b82923914f6b0132eaf56ca0c926ffd85317caf8d0c18b7146b01e8bdcd5688","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 11 (Oscillation-only work) of arXiv:2608.30715, 'Endpoint Energy Atoms Force Local Pressure Concentration in Three-Dimensional Navier--Stok'] The relative pressure-work identity can be written as
\begin{equation}
 \frac12\norm{r_\tau(t)}_2^2
 +\nu\int_\tau^t\norm{\nabla r_\tau}_2^2\dd\rho
 =\int_\tau^t
 \ip{u}{\mathfrak h_\tau}_{\BMO,\Hh^1}\dd\rho.

\end{equation}
If \(\Aa_a[w]\ge m\), then
\begin{equation}
 \limsup_{t\uparrow T_*}
 \int_\tau^t
 \ip{u}{\mathfrak h_\tau}_{\BMO,\Hh^1}\dd\rho
