{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:01:54.290758+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9e8bd2505ca49ba30c1333425676612e53ee96af854991cda116d8493b6202fc","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:ergodicity of upper of arXiv:2411.02030, 'Finite ergodic components for upper probabilities'] Let $(\Omega,\mathcal{F},V,T)$ be an UPPS. Then the following two statements are equivalent:
	\begin{enumerate}
		\item [(i)]$V$ is of finite ergodic components;
		\medskip
		\item [(ii)] there exist  $Q_1,\ldots,Q_n\in\mathcal{ M}(T)\cap\operatorname{core}(V)$ and $A_1,\ldots,A_n\in\mathcal{I}$ satisfying that  $\Omega=\cup_{j=1}^nA_j$, $Q_j(A_j)=1$, and $A_j\cap A_k=\emptyset$ when $j\neq k$ such that for any $B,C\in \mathcal{F}$,
		\begin{equation}
			\lim_{N\to\infty}\int\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=0}^{N-1} 1_B\cdot(1_C\circ T^i)dV=\sum_{j=1}^nQ_j(C)(V(\cup_{k=0}^jA_k\cap B)-V(\cup_{k=0}^{j-1}A_k\cap B)),
		\end{equation}
		where $A_0=\emptyset$.
	\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:43:18.803238+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9e8cc49a43cdd2a5193f6c113b03edac8c576223a2ac70fe55d24a4fe73119de","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Conj index of arXiv:2609.02409, 'Membranes and Maps'] The constant-map contribution to the equivariant Gromov--Witten theory of $Z$ is given by
    \begin{equation}

        \exp\Big( 2 \, \GWargs{Z}{\mathsf{T}}{0} \Big)
        \sim
        \Exp\Big(
            \chi_{\mathsf{T}}\big(
                Z, T_{Z}^\vee - T_{Z}
            \big)
        \Big) \,.
    \end{equation}
    Here $\Exp$ denotes the plethystic exponential, and the symbol $\sim$ means that the logarithms of both sides have the same $\mathrm{o}(1)$ tail in their asymptotic expansions. (Stated as a conjecture by the paper; open unless the paper says otherwise.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T21:02:38.606531+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9e9b9a6b1f9c74eddd921c180179126392cb9be6d885ebe67dc6f4e5e95c5247","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 16 of arXiv:2607.08129, '2607.08129'] For $\rho\in(0,1/4)$, let $N_m=\lfloor(1+\rho)^m\rfloor$.  Then, on an event of probability one,
