where $C_G$ is as in Proposition~\ref{prop:exp-lln}.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:19:46.234240+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9fd70f858efac7874d7281f89a506334d8780dc41d75cf04bc832ea973933ffc","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm 70 of arXiv:2507.08909, 'Annealed almost periodic entropy'] Fix $\tau$ and $\phi$ as above.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[a.] For any $h > \log \Delta \phi_{\rm{ac}}$, there are a positive integer $d_0$, a neighbourhood $V$ of $\tau$, and a neighbourhood $O$ of $\phi$ such that the following holds:
\begin{quote}
If $\pi$ is a $d$-dimensional representation of $\A$ satisfying
\[d\ge d_0 \quad \hbox{and} \quad \tr_d\circ \pi \in V,\]
then
\[\frac{\vol_{2kd}\X(\pi,O)}{v(d)^k} \le e^{hd}.\]
\end{quote}
\item[b.] If $\log \Delta \phi_{\rm{ac}} > h > \infty$, and if $O$ is any neighbourhood of $\phi$, then there are a positive integer $d_0$, a neighbourhood $V$ of $\tau$, and another neighbourhood $O'$ of $\phi$ such that the following holds:
\begin{quote}
If $\pi$ is a $d$-dimensional representation of $\A$ satisfying
\[d\ge d_0, \quad  \tr_d\circ \pi \in V, \quad \hbox{and} \quad \X(\pi,O')\ne \emptyset,\]
then
\[\frac{\vol_{2kd}\X(\pi,O)}{v(d)^k} \ge e^{hd}.\]
\end{quote}
\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:51:15.027352+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9fd80aa4d0c594f08ddb3c787b841e1dd6ce968ef5f24eecefa588a6c5418ab0","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma drift_convergence of arXiv:2609.03858, 'High-Dimensional Learning Dynamics of \\ Attention-Indexed Models'] Fix $C_*>0$ and $\rho>C_*^2$. Let
\begin{equation}
\mathcal A_d(C_*):=\left\{
(S_1,\ldots,S_K):S_k=W_kW_k^T,\ \max_{1\le k\le K}\|W_k\|_{\mathrm{op}}\le C_*\right\}.
\end{equation}
For $\mathbf S\in\mathcal A_d(C_*)$, let $\mu(\mathbf S)$ denote its associated moment sequence. Then
\begin{equation}
\sup_{\mathbf S\in\mathcal A_d(C_*)}
\left\|V_d(\mu(\mathbf S))-V(\mu(\mathbf S))
\right\|_\rho\longrightarrow0
\qquad
\text{as }d\to\infty.
\end{equation}
Consequently, on the spectral-bound event $\mathcal{E}_d(T)$,
\begin{equation}
\sup_{t\in[0,T]}
\left\|V_d(\tilde\mu^{(d)}(t))-V(\tilde\mu^{(d)}(t))\right\|_\rho
\xrightarrow{\mathbb P}0.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:30:08.197775+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9fdc882a038a7d031893eb333f9c898652794979016b46a9225735c4f05aea2d","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:Wass_p_bnd_new of arXiv:2608.13452, 'Wasserstein stability of the zero cell of a Poisson hyperplane tessellation under directio'] Consider the setting of (lem:mainBound of the paper).  
Assume that $\phi_2$ is sufficiently close to $\phi_1$ such that
    \begin{align} 
    W_{\mathrm{geo}}(\phi_1,\phi_2) \leq \min_{u\in S^{d-1}} h(\Pi_1,u) \eqcolon C_1 .
    \end{align}
Then, for $p \in [1, \infty)$,
\begin{align}
         W_{\rho_p}\bigl(Z_0^{(1)} , Z_0^{(2)} \bigr) 
        \leq \frac{ 6\Gamma(p+1)^{1/p}(d\kappa_d)^{1/p} C_1^{-\frac{p+1}{p}} }{\gamma} W_{\mathrm{geo}}(\phi_1,\phi_2)^{\frac{1}{p}}.   
\end{align}
and
\begin{align} 
    W_{\Delta}\bigl(Z_0^{(1)},Z_0^{(2)}\bigr) 
    &\leq 
    \frac{ 6d\kappa_d\,d!\, \left(1+2^{-d}\right)^{\frac{d-1}{d}} C_1^{-d-\frac1d} }{\gamma^d} W_{\mathrm{geo}}(\phi_1,\phi_2)^{\frac1d}, 
\end{align}
where \(\kappa_d:= \lambda_d(B_d)\) is the volume of the unit ball in \(\mathbb{R}^d\). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:40:21.311270+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9fde3d8626fdc9146fbdc53fd91f2051daa927fee8e3efee1c9875f459410cfe","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 13 of arXiv:2604.08127, 'Asymptotics of Brownian occupation measures \\ with unusually large intersections'] Let $W_t$ be a Brownian motion in $\mathbb{R}^d$. For any $k < d$ and $x \in \mathbb{R}^d$, we have the following estimates. Here, $C$ is a constant that may depend on $d$ and $k$ but not on $t$, $x$, or $\epsilon$.
        \begin{align}

            \mathbb{E} \delta(W_t - x) &\le C\min\{ |t|^{-d/2}, |x|^{-d} \} \\

            \left| \mathbb{E} \Delta_{\epsilon}(W_t - x)\right| &\le C  \min\{ |t|^{-d/2}, |x|^{-d}, \epsilon|t|^{-d/2 - 1}, \epsilon|x|^{-d - 2} \} \\

            \mathbb{E}|W_t - x|^{-k} &\le C \min\{ t^{-k/2}, |x|^{-k} \} 
        \end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:25:44.583695+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9fdee65e5b5371cea274ecfb570ff1d146b31e78da1d1d33c1433b5ad98d4ed2","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm pointstationary of arXiv:2509.08659, 'Gap metrics for stationary point processes and quantitative convexity of the free energy'] Let $\mathsf{P}\in \mathcal P_{s,1}(\Gamma)$. Then its Palm measure $\mathsf{P}^0$ is stationary w.r.t.\ the shifts $\theta_{\tau_i}$, $i\in \IZ$, defined  for $\xi\in \Gamma_0$ by 
