{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:17:04.328791+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9fe74b977b4bfd599fe010341c3916d4c6ddb1c946eeed2653f30e55d3867174","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition defin:dirichletforms of arXiv:2606.01937, 'Stationary fluctuations for an exclusion process with mass and energy conservation'] Let $\mu$ be any probability measure on $\Omega$. For any $x,y\in\mathbb{Z}$ and any local function $f:\Omega\longrightarrow\mathbb{R}$ we define the Dirichlet forms with respect to the measure~$\mu$ by
    \begin{align}
        & \mathfrak{D}_{x\to y}^p (f;\mu ) \coloneq \frac12\int_{\Omega} c_{x\to y}^p(\eta )\big[ f(\eta^{x,y})-f(\eta )\big]^2\,\mathrm{d}\mu (\eta ), \\
        & \mathfrak{D}_{x\to y}^e(f;\mu )\coloneq \frac12\int_{\Omega}c_{x\to y}^e(\eta )\big[ f(\eta^{x\to y})-f(\eta )\big]^2\,\mathrm{d}\mu (\eta )
    \end{align}
    where the jump rates $c^p$ and $c^e$ are given respectively in (equation def:particlerate of the paper) and (equation def:energyrate of the paper), and the transformations of configurations are given by (equation eq:transformedconfig of the paper). Finally, we define the total Dirichlet form by
    \begin{equation*}
        \mathfrak{D}(f;\mu ) = \sum_{\substack {x,y\in\mathbb{Z}\\ |x-y|=1}} \big( \mathfrak{D}_{x\to y}^p(f;\mu ) + \mathfrak{D}_{x\to y}^e(f;\mu )\big).
    \end{equation*}
    By reversibility of the measure $\mu_{\rho ,\mathcal{E}}$ with respect to the generator $\mathcal{L}_S$ (\textit{cf.}~(prop:invariantmeasures of the paper)), we have the identity $\langle f,(-\mathcal{L}_S)f\rangle_{\rho ,\mathcal{E}} = \mathfrak{D}(f;\mu_{\rho ,\mathcal{E}})$ for any local function $f:\Omega\longrightarrow\mathbb{R}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:36:44.530961+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9fea638c28ae88d74033f28fb6702bc2004cb50cb2774302f70cbeaf0093a6d8","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 28 (The existence of super Brownian motion (SBM), see e.g. \cite{etheridge2000introduction}) of arXiv:2604.01852, 'The on/off Brownian snake'] Let $\mu \in \mathcal{M}_F(\mathbb{R}^d)$. Then there exists a measure-valued process $(\mathcal{X}_s)_{s \geq 0}$ that takes values in $\mathcal{M}_F(\mathbb{R}^d)$ with $\mathcal{X}_0=\mu$ which is the scaling limit of BBMs in the following sense. Let $\Tilde{\mathcal{Z}}^\varepsilon=(\Tilde{\mathcal{Z}}^\varepsilon_s)_{s \geq 0}$ be $\text{BBM}(\gamma/\varepsilon)$ with $\Tilde{\mathcal{Z}}_0^\varepsilon$ distributed as a Poisson random measure with intensity $\mu/\varepsilon$. Then we have 
\begin{equation*}
    (\varepsilon \Tilde{\mathcal{Z}}^\varepsilon_s)_{s \geq 0} \rightarrow (\mathcal{X}_s)_{s \geq 0}
\end{equation*}
as $\varepsilon$ goes to $0$, in distribution on  $\mathbb{D}([0,\infty), \mathcal{M}_F(\mathbb{R}^d))$. This process $(\mathcal{X}_s)_{s \geq 0}$ is called $\text{SBM}(\gamma)$ started at $\mu$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:38:36.832536+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9fec0d91cf8bab752d671c47c7df84b03f4ed8439b71d49b427b7e32861ae4e6","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:Feller of arXiv:2606.29352, 'Exponential Mixing for 2D Stochastic Damped Euler Equation Driven by Bounded Noise'] The operator $P_i$ is sequentially weak$\star$ Feller in $W^{1,\infty}$, that is 
