{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:52:57.732087+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a0108d1c858f4afcd81f8b41e6622333b7320b67e192f6b906f44bc3e0895778","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 5 of arXiv:2609.02755, 'The Sharp Rate of Probabilistically Strong Convergence to the KPZ Equation'] Suppose that $(\Pi, \Gamma)$ is a smooth model, that $|\tau|_\fs < - 2$, that $\Gamma \tau = \tau$ for all $\Gamma \in \mcG$. Suppose that we postulate that $\mcR \mathbf{1}_\pm \tau = \mathbf{1}_\pm \Pi_z \tau \in C^{|\tau|_\fs}$. Then we have that 
	\begin{align*}
		\mathbf{1}_+ \mcP_\gamma \mathbf{1}_+ \tau = \mathbf{1}_+ \mcI \tau +  \mcV_\tau
	\end{align*}
	where $\mcV_\tau \in \mcD^{\gamma, |\tau|_\fs + 2}$ is defined by
	\begin{align*}
		\mcV_\tau = \sum_{|k|_\fs < \gamma} \frac{X^k}{k!} \mathbf{1}_+ \left (  D^k R \ast \mathcal{R} \mathbf{1}_+ \tau -D^k K \ast \mathcal{R} \mathbf{1}_- \tau \right ).
	\end{align*}
	Furthermore, $\mcV_\tau$ depends continuously on $\mathbf{1}_\pm \Pi_z \tau \in C^{|\tau|_\fs}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:02:18.035994+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a010d6cdd6fd1575dbdfe514ff36b5da5363888cb37c6501b0aabe5d53cd5cb7","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:h of arXiv:2602.21315, 'The Instability of all Backoff Protocols\thanks{For the purpose of Open Access, the author'] Let $\overline{p}$ be a send sequence with $p_0=1$. Fix a positive integer~$j$, a tuple $\newsym{a-vect}{Tuple of non-negative integers, typically initial ball counts}{\overline{a}} \in (\mathbb{R}_{\ge 0})^j$, and a real number $\nu \ge 0$. We now define a function \newsym{h-nu-a}{function to track population means in a constant-escape process on $[j]$ with initial state~$\overline{a}$ (Definition~(def:h of the paper))}{\overline{h}^{\nu,\overline{a}}} from non-negative integers to tuples of non-negative reals indexed by $\{0,\dots,j\}$. $h_x^{\nu,\overline{a}}$ denotes the $x$'th component of $\overline{h}^{\nu,\overline{a}}$.
For all $x\in [j]$, let $h_x^{\nu,\overline{a}}(0) = a_x$.  For all $t \ge 0$, let $h_0^{\nu, \overline{a}}(t) = 0$. For all $t \ge 1$ and all $x \in [j]$, let
\begin{equation}
h_x^{\nu, \overline{a}}(t) = (1-p_x)h_x^{\nu, \overline{a}}(t-1) + p_{x-1}h_{x-1}^{\nu, \overline{a}}(t-1) + \nu.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:25:55.318511+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a0161bfd8c5e8d8768043ed3caa898590f08fb80741d7632a21ed9c80e656730","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 21 of arXiv:2607.27786, 'Gaussian free field in annulus: \\BPZ equations and crossing probabilities for level lines'] For $w_1,w_2\in \S_r$, we have
		\begin{align} 
			\begin{split}	
				& \Im H_1(r;w_1)\Im H_1(r;w_2) \\
				=& \Re\Big(
				-\frac12\left(\partial_zH_1(r;w_1-w_2)-\partial_zH_1(r;w_1-\overline{w_2})\right)
				-\frac14\left(H_1(r;w_1-w_2)^2-H_1(r;w_1-\overline{w_2})^2\right)\\
				& \qquad
				+\frac12H_1(r;w_1-w_2)\left(H_1(r;w_1)-H_1(r;w_2)\right)
				-\frac12H_1(r;w_1-\overline{w_2})\left(H_1(r;w_1)-\overline{H_1(r;w_2)}\right)
				\Big).	
			\end{split}
		\end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:30:14.227510+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a01a057a252e666072fac40f1ab6d6843f3a9537a98d2934c50286ccdc07f631","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma Formula-for-Laplace-of-theta_t of arXiv:2608.23202, 'Locally finite Fixed points of Branching Brownian Motion'] Let $\theta$ be a point process and  $\theta_t^\lambda$ as in (equation theta_t-def of the paper). Then, for all $f\in C_c^+(\mathbb R)$ and $\phi(x):=1-e^{-f(-x)},$
