    $$","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:17:04.330282+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a13098794cd978f670b88edf872bfd3181df20b499e6f7ca3219c94c5b4e1281","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition eq:martdecomp of arXiv:2606.01937, 'Stationary fluctuations for an exclusion process with mass and energy conservation'] For any $i\in \{1,2\}$, any test function $\varphi\in \mathscr{S}(\mathbb{R} )$ and any $t\in [0,T]$, the martingale $\mathcal{M}^{N,i}$ decomposes as
    \begin{equation}
        \mathcal{M}_t^{N,i}(\varphi ) = \mathcal{Z}_t^{N,i}(\varphi )-\mathcal{Z}_0^{N,i}(\varphi ) - \mathcal{I}_t^{N,i}(\varphi ) - \Lambda_t^{N,i} (\varphi ) + E^{N,i}_t(\varphi)
    \end{equation}
    where
    \begin{align}
        & \mathcal{I}_t^{N,i}(\varphi ) = (\kappa -1)N^{a-2}\int_0^t \mathcal{Z}_s^{N,i}(\Delta_N\varphi )\,\mathrm{d} s, \\
        & \Lambda_t^{N,i} (\varphi ) = N^{a-\gamma -\frac32} \int_0^t \sum_{x\in\mathbb{Z}} \big( \mathfrak{c}_i\tilde{h}_x^p(s)+\tilde{h}_x^e(s)\big) \nabla_NT_{v_iN^{a-1}s}\varphi\Big( \frac xN\Big)\,\mathrm{d} s, 
    \end{align}
    and $E_t^{N,i}(\varphi )$ is the remainder term coming from the replacement of the discrete gradient by its continuous counterpart, whose $L^2$-norm is of order $O(N^{2a-2\gamma -4})$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:43:43.363998+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a1314a455b98255ecd73e462e1629e12a0a14a01170992ef00ff9bb4b5e12226","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop p.gmc.coupling of arXiv:2507.16056, 'Conditional GMC within the stochastic heat flow'] There exists a coupling of the family $(M^{\theta}_{[s,t]})_{ \theta\in\mathbb{R}}$ such that, with $\mathscr{H}_{\theta}:=\sigma(M^{\phi}_{[s,t]}\,|\,\phi\leq\theta)$ denoting its canonical filtration,
\begin{align}

\begin{split}
    \text{the law of } &(M^{\theta}_{[s,t]})_{\theta\geq\theta_0}
    \text{ conditioned on } \mathscr{H}_{\theta_0}
\\
    &\text{ is equal to the law of }
\big(\mathcal{G}\big[M^{\theta}_{[s,t]},Y_{\star\,[s,t]}^{\theta},\xi(\theta_0,\theta)\big]\big)_{\theta\geq\theta_0}\ .
\end{split}
\end{align}
In particular, $M^{\theta}_{[s,t]}$ is a Markovian martingale in $\theta$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:25:44.969844+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a1329ea52708181df364fdfa673646c214c229368e81f817e077cefc0c930553","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 18 (Small-time expansion of the coordinate profile) of arXiv:2605.11451, '2605.11451'] Let $1\le p<2$ and $n\ge2$, and let $Z\sim N(0,1)$. As $t\downarrow0$,
\begin{equation}
 h_{p,n,t,e_1}(0)
 =
 C_{p,n}\left(1-\frac{n-1}{p}\,\E|Z|^p\,t^{p/2}+O_{p,n}(t)\right),
\end{equation}
and therefore
\begin{equation}
 \Phi_{p,n}(t)
 =
 C_{p,n}\sqrt{v_{p,n}}\left(1-\frac{n-1}{p}\,\E|Z|^p\,t^{p/2}+O_{p,n}(t)\right).
\end{equation}
Moreover,
\begin{equation}
 \Phi_{p,n}'(t)
 =
 -C_{p,n}\sqrt{v_{p,n}}\,\frac{n-1}{2}\,\E|Z|^p\,t^{p/2-1}
 +O_{p,n}(1).
\end{equation}
In particular, $\Phi_{p,n}'(t)<0$ for all sufficiently small $t>0$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:11:09.319949+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a1341f00ae797767f86f3f0d56703efc221b89ef686b98a11142076336d31b29","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma le:integrate-one-spin of arXiv:2411.06195, 'Restrictions of some reinforced processes to subgraphs'] Let $1\in\Lambda$ and set $1^c=\Lambda\setminus\{1\}$.
  One has
  \begin{align}

    \int_{H^{2|2}}\mathcal{D}\sigma_1\, e^{\sum_{i\in 1^c}W_{1i}\left\langle{\sigma_1,\sigma_i}\right\rangle}
    =e^{-\|\sum_{i\in 1^c}W_{1i}\sigma_i\|},
  \end{align}
  and consequently, 
\begin{align}

  \int_{H^{2|2}}\mathcal{D}\sigma_1\,
    e^{\frac12\sum_{i,j\in\Lambda}W_{ij}(1+\left\langle{\sigma_i,\sigma_j}\right\rangle)}
      =& e^{\frac12\sum_{i,j\in 1^c}W_{ij}(1+\left\langle{\sigma_i,\sigma_j}\right\rangle)
    +\sum_{i\in 1^c}W_{1i}-\|\sum_{i\in 1^c}W_{1i}\sigma_i\|}.
\end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:47:01.616049+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a1386157ae3bad80a00530f39d675c17626558a5d719630b35ac5da9db0b30a5","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 9 of arXiv:2604.22644, '2604.22644'] The tail distribution of the maximum penetration depth $\displaystyle M = \max_{0 \le t \le \theta_1} X_t$ is given by:
