{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:56:11.143508+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a1fa624385f1ca11a3323b11979f865a5d14a929b70e4413085a3246f7710038","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 3 of arXiv:2608.11071, 'Scaling Laws for Majority-based Opinion Dynamics in the Presence of Stubborn Agents'] The parametric form of the boundary $\Gamma_{\text{crit}}^k$ is $$\{(\gamma_0(w),\gamma_1(w)), w\in (w_{\min}, w_{\max})\},$$ where
    \begin{align}
        &\nonumber \gamma_1(w)=w - \frac{P_k(w)(P_k(w)+P_{k}(1-w))}{P'_{k}(1-w)P_k(w)+P_k'(w)P_k(1-w)},
    \end{align}
    $\gamma_0(w)=\gamma_1(1-w)$, $w_{\min}$  solves $\gamma_0(w_{\min})=0$, and $w_{\max} = 1 - w_{\min}$. Here, $P'_k$ denotes differentiation with respect to the argument of $P_k$. 
    Furthermore, we have $\gamma_0(1/2)=\gamma_1(1/2)=\gamma_k^*$, where
    \begin{align}
        \gamma^*_{k} = \frac{1}{2}+\frac{1}{4k}-\frac{2^{2k}}{4k\binom{2k}{k}},
    \end{align}
    is the point at which  $\Gamma_{\text{crit}}^k$ intersects the line $\gamma_0=\gamma_1$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:16:49.565046+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a1fdcd9c93ecd2371d01d55af7cab23e737984220c183c5fe4236b1fe476d324","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 22 (Panter--Dite optimal point density and the high-rate law \citep{panter1951quantization}) of arXiv:2608.25188, 'Transforms for LLM Quantization:\\ The Great Inversion and Format Co-Design'] Assume the high-resolution regime of \Cref{lem:bennett}. For a \emph{fixed-length} (uncoded) quantizer of $R$ bits, with $p$ smooth and light-tailed enough that $\int p^{1/3}$ converges and overload negligible at the rates considered, ($L=2^R$ points, $\int\lambda=L$), minimizing Bennett's integral over $\lambda$ under the count constraint gives the optimal density $\lambda^\star(x)\propto p(x)^{1/3}$ and distortion
\begin{equation}
D_{\mathrm{fl}} \;\approx\; \frac{1}{12}\Big(\int p(x)^{1/3}dx\Big)^{3}\,2^{-2R}.
\end{equation}
For an \emph{entropy-constrained} (variable-length) quantizer, minimizing $D$ at fixed \emph{index entropy} $H$ instead makes the optimal density \emph{uniform} ($\lambda^\star$ constant) \citep{gish1968asymptotically}, giving $D_{\mathrm{ec}}\approx \tfrac{1}{12}2^{2h(X)}2^{-2H}$, where $h(X)$ is the differential entropy (\Cref{sec:rate}).","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:33:43.751194+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a2006cfc269e1fe5bddbb2a38c0562149fff8a6401e256b71ca365dbdc26ff30","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:sde_kin_dens of arXiv:2608.30493, '\bf McKean-Vlasov Differential Equations:\\ {An introduction and a focus on some kinetic m'] Let Assumptions (assump:hor of the paper) and (assum:sigma_0 of the paper) be in force. 
Setting
\begin{equation}
\beta(t,x) : = B x +  \left( 
\begin{matrix}
\beta_0(t,x)  \\
{\bf 0}_{(d - d_0) \times 1}
\end{matrix}
\right)  , \quad \sigma(t,x) : = \left( 
\begin{matrix}
\sigma_0 (t,x)  \\
{\bf 0}_{(d - d_0) \times 1}
\end{matrix}
\right), \qquad (t,x)\in(0,T)\times\mathbb{R}^d,
\end{equation}
with $\beta_0\in L^{\infty}((0,T)\times \mathbb{R}^d)$, we have:
\begin{itemize}
\item[(i)] SDE$(\beta,\sigma)$ is weakly well-posed (see Definition (def:well_posed_lin of the paper)). For any $(s,x)\in [0,T)$, the law of the (unique in law) solution to SDE$(\beta,\sigma;s,\delta_x)$, at time $t\in (s,T)$, has a density $p(s,x; t, \cdot)$.

\item[(ii)] 
There exists $C>0$, depending only on $\| \sigma_0 \|_{L^{\infty}_{T}\mathcal{C}^{\alpha}_B(\mathbb{R}^d)}$, $\| \beta_0 \|_{L^{\infty}((0,T)\times \mathbb{R}^d)}$, $\lambda$, $T$ and $B$, such that
\begin{equation}
 |  \nabla^{m}_{x^0} p(s,x;t,y)  |  \leq C (t-s)^{-\frac{m}{2}} \, p_{\mathscr{K}_{2\lambda}}(t-s, x  , y).

\end{equation}

for any $0\leq s < t < T$, any $x,y\in\mathbb{R}^d$ and $m=0,1,2$. Here, $p_{\mathscr{K},\cdot}$ is the Gaussian density in (equation eq:kol_dens of the paper).

\item[(iii)] Furthermore, for any $s\in[0,T)$ and $\nu\in\mathcal{P}(\mathbb{R}^d)$, the flow of time-marginal densities of the solution to SDE$(\beta,\sigma;s,\nu)$ is the unique (weak) solution to FP$(\beta,\sigma;s, \nu)$ (see Definition (def:sol_lin_fp of the paper)). In particular, for any $x\in\mathbb{R}^d$, $(p(s,x; t, \cdot))_{t\in(s,T)}$ is a density solution to FP$(\beta,\sigma;s, \delta_x)$.
\end{itemize}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:43:45.392711+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a207848b47ed0a81f55c63c7662704b5c2c87ba70ad2a3c7b7f13f1d92a83a25","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 23 of arXiv:2609.04139, 'The conformally invariant metric on CLE$_4$ II:\\ existence of geodesics'] There is a universal constant $C > 0$ such that the following is true: Let $K_1, K_2 \subset \overline\BD$ be two disjoint, connected, simply connected, and compact subsets such that $\partial \BD \not\subset K_1 \cup K_2$. Then the extremal distance in $\BD$ between $K_1$ and $K_2$ is at most 
    \begin{equation}
        \left.C\middle/\log\left(\frac{\diam(K_1) \wedge \diam(K_2)}{\dist(K_1, K_2)} \vee 1\right)\right., 
    \end{equation}
    where $\diam$ and $\dist$ denote Euclidean diameter and distance, respectively, with the convention that the bound is $+\infty$ when the logarithm vanishes. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:48:45.312392+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a209d36952ace56913084d06c055c7b340c9b48dadbb707618d62f263f36069c","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:cone1 of arXiv:2411.14921, 'A boundary Harnack principle and its application to analyticity of 3D Brownian intersectio'] For any $u_1>1$ and $0<u_2<1$, there exists 
$n_0(u_1,u_2,k)>0$ such that if $n>n_0$ and $|x_i|\geq 2^{-n}$ for all $1\le i\le k$, then 
\begin{equation}
\mathbb{P}_{\vec{x}}\left(\mathcal{W}\cap\left(\mathsf{C}(v,u_1^{-n})\cap B(0,2)\right)=\varnothing \text{ for some unit vector $v$}\right)>1-u_2^n.
