{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T21:02:38.597491+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a20cf1cd559a93c01612a9d2fe17dbaf3c21ac8cdad053a8fd6422311a79222c","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 16 of arXiv:2607.07283, 'Multi-time Markov renewal chains and stratified renewal theorems'] The regenerative increment is called full-rank if $\mathcal L$ has rank $d$. If $\mathcal L$ is full-rank, its covolume is

\begin{equation*}
h_{\mathcal L}=|\Z^d/\mathcal L|.
\end{equation*}

The renewal lattice generated by complete cycles is

\begin{equation*}
\mathcal L_* = \mathcal L+\Z y_0,
\end{equation*}

and, when $\mathcal L$ is full-rank, its covolume is

\begin{equation*}
h_* = |\Z^d/\mathcal L_*|.
\end{equation*}

The regenerative increment is called renewal-aperiodic if $\mathcal L_*=\Z^d$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:30:36.773623+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a21c100bf1a1b27eaa9521d6499070d09ced064b945de6708d094bbafe0d13cb","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 5 of arXiv:2607.10356, '2607.10356'] Let $\{\CW_n:n\ge 1\}$ be a sequence of independent $\Bb$-valued random variables with mean zero and is \emph{tight} $($\emph{i.e.} satisfies \eqref{m1}, \eqref{m2}$)$.
Suppose $\{\CW_n:n\ge 1\}$ is uniformly integrable, \emph{i.e.}
\begin{equation}
\textstyle\lim_{t\rightarrow\infty}\Bigl[ \sup_{n\ge 1}\E\bigl[\nrm{\CW_{n}}\Ind_{\{\nrm{\CW_{n}}\ge t\}}\bigr]\Bigr]=0.
 \end{equation}
Then for all $\epsilon>0$ there exists $t\ge 1$, $\{\xa_j: 1\le j\le t\}\subseteq\Bb$, a sequence $\{ \CY_{n}:n\ge 1\}$ of $\Bb$-valued independent random variables, a constant $C<\infty$ and for $1\le j\le t$, a sequence $\{\xi_{n,j}: n\ge 1\}$ of $[-1,1]$-valued independent random variables such that for all $n\ge 1$
\begin{align}
\CW_n=\CY_n+&\textstyle\sum_{j=1}^t \xa_j\xi_{n,j},\\

\E[\xi_{n,j}]&=0,\;\;\;\;\forall\,1\le j\le t,\\
\E[\nrm{\CY_n}]&\le \epsilon\\

\nrm{\CY_n-\CW_n}&\le C.
\end{align}
Further, if $\{\CW_n:n\ge 1\}$ are i.i.d. then $\{\CY_n:n\ge 1\}$ are i.i.d. and for each $1\le j\le t$, $\{\xi_{n,j}: n\ge 1\}$ are also i.i.d.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:09:59.306747+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a21c6e90c2a86499f4b167c242d9334e2275816de70102d2a7608fe18c0d6ad4","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:pushforward of arXiv:2608.07842, 'Resolution of a Conjecture on Shifted R\'enyi Divergence'] For $\beta\in[0,1)$ with $\mu_D(\beta)$ as above, $\mu'_\beta$ is Gaussian with
$$\langle u,\cdot\rangle\text{-marginal }=N\big(m-\mu_D(\beta),\,(1-\beta)^2\sigma^2\big),$$
and with all marginals orthogonal to $u$ equal to those of $\mu$, namely $N(0,\sigma^2)$. Hence, writing $\sigma_q^2(\beta):=q\sigma^2-(q-1)(1-\beta)^2\sigma^2$ and $\mu_1(\beta):=m-\mu_D(\beta)$,
\begin{equation}
J(\beta):=R_q(\mu'_\beta\|\nu)=\frac{q\,\mu_1(\beta)^2}{2\sigma_q^2(\beta)}+\frac{1}{1-q}\log\frac{\sigma_q(\beta)}{\big((1-\beta)\sigma\big)^{1-q}\sigma^{q}}.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T01:16:46.696222+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a21d9e009fb8a00fc2f3023586a0c231f1e3d06fd2158ab8af7d3c072e94d87e","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Cor SDdegbound of arXiv:2411.18563, 'Lower tails for triangles inside the critical window'] Let $\lambda > 0$, $\zeta \in [0,1]$, and $p = \frac{\lambda}{1 + \lambda}$. For any $\kappa > 0 $ we have
\begin{align*}
&\mathbb{P}_{\lambda,\zeta}\left(\Delta(G) > np(1 + \kappa)\right) \leq n \cdot \exp\left(-\frac{\kappa^2 np}{2 + \kappa}\right),~\text{and} \\
& \mathbb{P}_{H,\lambda,\zeta}\left(|S| > np(1 + \kappa)\right) \leq n \cdot \exp\left(-\frac{\kappa^2 np}{2 + \kappa}\right) \,.
\end{align*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:25:44.955306+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a220538ac01897e31b5d360dc93e18f3b6f9f7bbbadaf66b528d6f7a5719c38f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Teo 1 of arXiv:2605.11264, 'Uniform sampling of multitype continuous-time Bienaym\'e-Galton-Watson trees'] For any $k\geq 1$, $T\in \re_+$,  $r\in [d]$, we have
\begin{equation}
	\begin{split}
\mathbb{P}^{(k)}_{unif,T,r}&\paren{\Delta_T(n)}\\
&= \frac{1}{(k-1)!}\frac{1}{\mathbb{P}_{r}\paren{\ N_T\geq k}}\int_0^\infty(e^\phi-1)^{k-1}\Q^{(k),\phi\vec{1}}_{T,r}(\Delta_T(n))\E_{r}\left[N^{\floor{k}}_Te^{-\phi\vec{1}\cdot \bo Z_T}\right]{\rm d}\phi,
\end{split}
\end{equation}
where
\[
\begin{split}
\Q^{(k),\phi\vec{1}}_{T,r}(\Delta_T(n))&= \prod_{h=1}^n\prod_{m=1}^d\E_{m}\left[e^{-\phi\vec{1} \cdot \bo Z_{T-t_h}}\right]^{\ell_{h,m} -g_{h,m}}p_{i_h}(\bm \ell_{h})\bm \ell_{h}^{\floor{\bo g_h}}\\
		&\hspace{-1.5cm}  \times 
		\prod_{h=0}^{n-1}\prod_{m=1}^d\prod_{\substack{ q\leq g_{h,m}:\\ k_{v(h,m,q)}\geq 2}}\E_{m}\left[Z^{(c(v(h,m,q)))}_{t_{v(h,m,q)}-t_h} \prod_{j=1}^d\E_{j}\left[e^{-\phi\vec{1}\cdot \bo Z_{T-t_{v(h,m,q)}}}\right]^{ Z^{(j)}_{t_{v(h,m,q)}-t_h}-\delta_{c(v(h,m,q)),j}}\right]\\
	& \hspace{1.5cm} \times
	\frac{\prod_{h=1}^{n}\prod_{m=1}^{d}\E_{m}\left[N_{T-t_{h}}e^{-\phi\vec{1} \cdot \bo Z_{T-t_{h}}}\right]
		^{\#\{ q\leq g_{h,m}:k_{v(h,m,q)}=1\}}
	}{\E_{r}\left[N_T(N_T-1)\cdots(N_T-k+1)e^{-\phi\vec{1}\cdot \bo Z_{T}}\right]} \prod_{h=1}^n\alpha_{i_{h}} {\rm d}t_h,
	\end{split}
\]
 $t_{v(h,m,q)}$ and $c(v(h,m,q))$ are the spine splitting times  and type associated to the vertex $v(h,m,q)$.  When  $h=0$, we observe that $v(0)=\emptyset$, $g_{0,m}=\delta_{r,m}$ and $v(0, r, 1)=v(1)$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:28:49.117484+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a226e2a3e91a24a70743430195a55d19bda3daf8d04638e6b306d0d98b90c729","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:green of arXiv:2608.17022, 'Sharp hypocoercive convergence estimates for underdamped Langevin dynamics with specular r'] Let $\Omega\subset\mathbb{R}^d$ be a bounded Lipschitz domain and $U\in C^1(\overline\Omega)$, and write $Z_x:=\int_\Omega e^{-U}\mathrm{d} x$. For all $a\in H^1(\Omega)$ and $b\in H^2(\Omega)$,
