{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:53:30.674206+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a4b252aca4c872455d9763e94e288738492ef16b72acb57cab82a8cc4753e5ed","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop existence_r>2 of arXiv:2506.01746, '\bf Optimal Bregman quantization: existence and uniqueness of optimal quantizers revisited'] Let $r\!\in (0, +\infty)$.

\smallskip
\noindent $(a)$ Case  $r\ge2$.  Assume that the distribution $P$ of $X$ satisfies the  moment assumption~(equation eq:Integrabilite of the paper), that $F$ is $C^2$ on $U$ with $\nabla^2F$ bounded on $U$ and that $\nabla^2F(x)$ is (symmetric) positive definite for  every $x\!\in U$.

Assume that at every $\bar x \!\in\partial\,U$, 

\smallskip
\noindent either
\begin{equation} 
 \nabla^2F \mbox{ can  be continuously extended at } \bar x \mbox{ and } \nabla^2F(\bar x) \mbox{ is (symmetric) positive definite}
\end{equation}
or 

\smallskip
 \quad the l.s.c. extensions $\phi_{_F}(\xi, \cdot)$  (on the closure $ \bar U^{\widehat{\mathbb{R}^d}}$ of $U$) satisfy 

\begin{equation}

 \forall\, \xi \!\in U, \qquad \phi_{_F}(\xi, \bar x) =\displaystyle  \sup_{x\in U}\phi_{_F}(\xi,x)
\end{equation}
and, when $U$ is unbounded, $\bar x = \infty$ satisfies this second condition~(equation eq:Cond-rb of the paper).

\smallskip
Then $G_{r,1}:  \bar U^{\widehat{\mathbb{R}^d}}\to \mathbb{R}_+$  attains a unique strict minimum  at  a point $x^{(r)}\!\in U$  \textcolor{black}{which is the unique solution of the stationarity equation
\[
x^{(r)}= \int_U \xi \,{\rm I\hspace{-1.50ex}Q} _{x^{(r)}}(\mathrm{d}\xi) \!\in U\quad \mbox{ where }\quad {\rm I\hspace{-1.50ex}Q} _{x} (\mathrm{d}\xi)= \frac{\phi_{_F}(\xi,x)^{\frac r2}\cdot P(\mathrm{d}\xi)}{\int_U\phi_{_F}(\xi,x)^{\frac r2}P(\mathrm{d}\xi)}, \quad x\!\in U.
\]
In particular, $x^{(r)}$ is the  unique  critical point of $G_{r,1}$.}

\noindent \textcolor{black}{$(b)$ Case  $r\!\in (0, 2)$. Assume   all the assumption in $(a)$ are in force and, furthermore, that 
\begin{equation}
\exists\, \delta>0,\quad \mathbb{E}\,\phi_{_F}(X,x)^{\frac r2-1-\delta}<+\infty.
\end{equation}

Then,  $G_{r,1} $ attains a minimum on $U$.} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:05:09.626919+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a4b2bcc7803cd756388760f84cc1b7b92019f6847597de304df24950dbebdc21","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:ergodic theorem for capacity of arXiv:2411.03512, 'Ergodicity and Mixing of Sublinear Expectation System and Applications'] Let $(\Omega,\mathcal{H},U_f,\hat{\mathbb E})$ be a regular sublinear expectation system, where $U_f$ is induced by a measurable transform $f:\Omega\to\Omega$. Let $C$ be the capacity induced by $\hat{\mathbb E}$ on $(\Omega,\sigma(\mathcal{H}))$. Then the following two statements are equivalent:
     \begin{enumerate}[(i)]
         \item $C$ is ergodic;
         \item there exists an ergodic probability $P\in\mathcal{P}$ such that for any $X\in L^1(\Omega,\mathcal{\sigma(\mathcal{H})},P)$, 
 \[C\bigg(\Big\{\omega\in\Omega:\lim_{n\to\infty}\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=0}^{n-1}X(f^i\omega)=\int XdP\Big\}^c\bigg)=0.\]
     \end{enumerate}
  When the equivalent statements (i) and (ii) hold, one has $\mathcal{P}\cap \mathcal{M}(T)=\mathcal{P}\cap \mathcal{M}^e(T)=\{P\}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:52:05.656866+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a4bbe182681388025a6ef7c548fdb3a47894cc4e1f891ea8cb7a7b2c5f003e3f","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop sign-permute of arXiv:2602.06011, 'Excursion decomposition of the XOR-Ising model'] Let $(\xi_k)_{k\geq1}$ be a sequence of i.i.d. symmetric signs. Let $(X_k)_{k\geq1}$ be any sequence of random variables independent of $(\xi_k)_{k\geq 1}$ such that 
