{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:19:46.234405+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a7f78c4888c5b13c90284eadb4c8a2144ea28f58a240b4b751be1e91d80116f1","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Cor 72 of arXiv:2507.08909, 'Annealed almost periodic entropy'] Assume that $\tr_{d_n}\circ \pi \to\tau$, and let $\phi \in \B(\A,\rmM_k)_+$ and $\psi \in \B(\A,\rmM_\ell)_+$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[a.] If $\rm{diag}(\phi,\psi)$ is asymptotically associated to $\bspi$, then
\[\rmh_{\bspi}(\rm{diag}(\phi,\psi)) = \rmh_{\bspi}(\phi) + \rmh_{\bspi}(\psi).\]
\item[b.] If $k = \ell$, $t \in [0,1]$, and $t\phi + (1-t)\psi$ is asymptotically associated to $\bspi$, then
\[\rmh_{\bspi}(t\phi + (1-t)\psi) \ge t\rmh_{\bspi}(\phi) + (1-t)\rmh_{\bspi}(\psi).\]
\end{enumerate}
\qed (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:59:45.008731+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a7f88421a54b16142ef9984dc2c3822f1e4a60df90ae2f71a38190855e3637a1","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition of arXiv:2605.12777, 'Mesoscopic Rates of Convergence for Complex Wishart Matrices at the Leftmost Spectrum Edge'] Consider a matrix $X\in M^{n\times m}(\mathbb{C})$ with all i.i.d.\ complex Gaussian entries such that
    \begin{equation*}
        \mathbb{E} X_{j,k}=\mathbb{E}X_{j,k}^2=0,~\mathbb{E}|X_{j,k}|^2=1
    \end{equation*}
    for any $j,~k$. Then the Hermitian matrix $W_{n,m}:=X^*X\in M^{m}(\mathbb{C})$ is called a \textbf{complex Wishart matrix}, or a \textbf{Laguerre Ensemble}.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:13:22.537068+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a7f8b6b169bb74b292d7a4676948359cb451d7b69788232f135d3c0a0bb9b1cc","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary cor.expansion_trunc of arXiv:2506.07158, 'The diffusivity of supercritical Bernoulli percolation is infinitely differentiable'] For any $\delta \in [0, 1-\mathfrak{p}]$, the conductivity perturbation $\widetilde\Delta^{\delta}_m$ defined in (equation eq.defDeltaTrunc of the paper) satisfies 
			\begin{equation}
				\mathbb{E} [\widetilde\Delta^{\delta}_m] = \sum_{l=0}^{\vert E_d({\scaleobj{1}{\square}}_m) \vert} \widetilde c_{l,m} \left(\frac{\delta}{1-\mathfrak{p}}\right)^l,
			\end{equation}
			with the coefficient $\widetilde c_{l,m} $  defined as 
			\begin{equation}
				\widetilde c_{l,m} := \frac{1}{|{\scaleobj{1}{\square}}_m|}\sum_{e\in E_d({\scaleobj{1}{\square}}_m)}\mathbb{E}
				\left \langle \nabla v_m(\mathbf{a}),(\mathbf{a}^e-\mathbf{a})\sum_{\substack{G \subseteq E_d({\scaleobj{1}{\square}}_m) \\ |G|=l-1}}D_{G}\big(\nabla v_m(\mathbf{a}^e)\big)\right \rangle_{{\scaleobj{1}{\square}}_m}.
			\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:55:18.064107+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a7fbb3cd1348fd8a8cfa0855c2ea7c7ccce0b92c98c1b4663fe2550952dfa336","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 17 (Doob's optional stopping version II, general supermartingale) of arXiv:2606.17452, 'Optional Stopping for Superhedging Supermartingales'] Let $\{f_j\}_{j \geq 0}$ be a given  real-valued supermartingale on $\mathcal{S}$. Consider two stopping times satisfying  
$\rho\le \tau$ a.e. and assume that $(L)-a.e$ holds. Then, if $\tau \leq C ~~a.e.$, where $C$ is a non negative integer:
\begin{equation}
\overline{\sigma}_{\rho} f_{\tau} \leq f_{\rho}\;\;\mbox{a.e. on}~~\mathcal{S}.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:55:18.063945+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a7fca362bd7b64beea454fcfd125185f2c36460cd4ae0821879b9cfe0c3c4622","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 15 (Stopped Sequence) of arXiv:2606.17452, 'Optional Stopping for Superhedging Supermartingales'] Given a sequence $\{f_j\}_{j \geq 0},~f_j \in Q$  and a stopping time $\tau$, we define the stopped sequence $\{f_{ j}^{\tau}\}_{j \geq 0 }$ by
\begin{equation} \nonumber
f_j^{\tau}(S):=f_{\tau(S)\wedge j}(S).
\end{equation}
Furthermore, if $\tau< \infty$ a.e. then  $f_{\tau}(S):=
f_{\tau(S)}(S)$ is defined a.e.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:06:21.667190+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a80111fd98548db3b8e930e1f72a1c7d880804ac09f878e965aa740d2f8e8c80","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 6 (Stationary moment identities) of arXiv:2607.20823, 'Fluctuation impossibility results for stochastic burst networks'] For every $i\in\{1,\dots,N\}$,
\begin{equation}
        \frac{\E_\pi[X_i]}{\tau_i}
        =
        \kappa_i\mu_i
        =
        \kappa_i\E_\pi[f_i(X_{-i})].

\end{equation}
Moreover, defining
\begin{equation}
        B_i
        :=
        \frac{\E[K_i^2]+\E[K_i]}{2\E[K_i]}
        =
        \frac{\E[K_i^2]+\kappa_i}{2\kappa_i},

\end{equation}
we have
\begin{equation}
        F_{X_i}-B_i
        =
        \frac{\Cov_\pi(f_i(X_{-i}),X_i)}{\mu_i}.

\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:14:37.618627+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a807efcaeb59cfe27eac49abdac11ff0ac3ba97aa2c48f394564f891334caf1d","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 19 of arXiv:2608.19065, 'Boundedly exchangeable graphs'] Let $\xi$ be a boundedly exchangeable graph process on $S$ directed by $\nu$. For every $B \in \widehat{\Bcal}(S)$ where $\pbb(\nu(B) = 0) < 1$ there exists an infinite jointly exchangeable matrix $A^B \in \rbb^{\nbb \times \nbb}$ such that the adjacency matrix $(A, N)$ of any i.i.d.\ representation of $\xi\vert_B$ satisfies for all $n \geq 1$:
