{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:51:33.321850+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a85c6afa03f1b2b6616146a91c15f8e7a1f2d860de55bd119fa0627a7283a1cc","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Cor global-smash-well-based of arXiv:2608.28504, 'Global $\bm\infty$-categories and global Thom spectra'] If $X\in\textbf{\textup{$\bm G$-Top}}_*$ is $\mathchoice{\hbox{\hfuzz=20pt\hbox{$\mathcal A\kern-.3pt$}\setbox0=\hbox{$\ell\kern-.75pt\ell$}\hbox to 0pt{\hfuzz=8pt\kern-.075pt\unhcopy0}\hbox to 0pt{\hfuzz=8pt\kern.075pt\unhcopy0}\box0\kern.05pt}}{\hbox{\hfuzz=20pt\hbox{$\mathcal A\kern-.3pt$}\setbox0=\hbox{$\ell\kern-.75pt\ell$}\hbox to 0pt{\hfuzz=8pt\kern-.075pt\unhcopy0}\hbox to 0pt{\hfuzz=8pt\kern.075pt\unhcopy0}\box0\kern.05pt}}{{\mathcal{A}}\kern-.3pt\ell\kern-.6pt\ell}{{\mathcal{A}}\kern-.3pt\ell\kern-.6pt\ell}$-cofibrant, then $X\smashp{-}\colon\cat{$\bm G$-$\textbf{\textup{L}}$-Top}_*\to \cat{$\bm G$-$\textbf{\textup{L}}$-Top}_*$ preserves $G$-global weak equivalences between well-based orthogonal $G$-spaces.
    \begin{proof}
        By the previous lemma, $X\smashp{-}$ preserves $G$-global weak equivalences between \emph{cofibrant} objects. Picking functorial cofibrant replacements in the $G$-global level model structure, it will therefore suffice to show that $X\smashp{-}$ preserves $G$-global \emph{level} weak equivalence between well-based objects. This follows at once by applying Proposition~(prop:smash-well-based of the paper) levelwise.
    \end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:48:35.816776+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a8615c913f9ebb341571d715e123cdf57d209fdc7a0bdca8b22a331b40ed77bc","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:mp-mbi of arXiv:2411.09516, 'Sharp Matrix Empirical Bernstein Inequalities\footnote{NeurIPS 2025 Poster}'] Let $n \geqslant 2$ be even and $ \mathbf{X} _1,\dots,  \mathbf{X} _n$ be $\mathcal{S}_d^{[0,1]}$-valued independent random matrices with common mean $ \mathbf{M} $ and variance $ \mathbf{V} $. We denote by $\overline{ \mathbf{X} }_n$ their sample average and by $    \mathbf{V} ^*_n$ their paired variance estimator defined in (equation eqn:paired of the paper).
    Then, for any $\alpha \in (0,1)$,
    \begin{equation} 
     \mathbb P \left( \lambda_{\max} \left( \overline \mathbf{X} _n - \mathbf{M}  \right) \geqslant   D_n^{\mathsf{meb1}} \right) \leqslant \alpha,
\end{equation}
where
\begin{equation}
    D_n^{\mathsf{meb1}} = \frac{ \log\frac{nd}{(n-1)\alpha}   }{3n} + \sqrt{  \frac{2  \|  \mathbf{V} ^*_n \| \log\frac{nd}{(n-1)\alpha}  }{n}} + \left( \sqrt{\frac{5}{3}} + 1 \right) \frac{\sqrt{\left(\log\frac{nd}{(n-1)\alpha}\right)\left( \log \frac{2nd}{\alpha}\right) } }{n}.
\end{equation}
Further, if $ \mathbf{X} _1,\dots,  \mathbf{X} _n$ are i.i.d.,
\begin{equation}
    \lim_{n\to\infty} \sqrt{n}   D_n^{\mathsf{meb1}} = \sqrt{2\log(d/\alpha) \| \mathbf{V} \| }, \quad \text{almost surely}.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

