{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:03:59.344534+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a8b99d3198e1399f24b6bb4577f5b5a4f35d15e5069f9af35303c8defc9bc2f2","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Satz th:BBLM of arXiv:2507.08692, 'Some notes on higher order concentration of measure'] If $X_1, \ldots, X_n$ are independent random variables and $f = f(X) \in L^\infty({\mathbb P})$, with the constant $\kappa = \frac{\sqrt{e}}{2\,(\sqrt{e} - 1)}$, we have for any $r \ge 2$,
	\begin{equation*} 
