{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:28:49.118866+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a97bc48873653e4ce36fdd85d99683d0592426cd74dca56af3e30d9a6d9f4b19","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:Ergodic:ErgodicStrong of arXiv:2608.17031, 'A shape theorem for periodic branching diffusion'] Let $\nu\in C^{2,\alpha}_p(\mathbb{R}^d)$ be the density of the unique invariant measure of $(\dot{Z}_t)_{t\geq 0}$ on $\mathbb{T}^d$ and $f\in C^{0,\alpha}_p(\mathbb{R}^d)$ be such that $\nu(f)=0$. Fix $\epsilon>0$. There exist positive constants $C_1,C_2$ depending on $d$, $\|\sigma\|_{0,\alpha}$ and $\|b\|_{0,\alpha}$ such that, for all $t$ sufficiently large (depending on $d$, $\|\sigma\|_{0,\alpha}$, $\|b\|_{0,\alpha}$, $\|f\|_{0,\alpha}$ and $\epsilon$),
