{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:05:37.774505+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a984728f2d88722fb813e40a35efb5996ed8ded91f9bd5136860210bb90d2661","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma L_k asympto of arXiv:2608.00399, 'The Heavy-tailed Frog Model'] Let $\zeta \in (0, 1)$. Then for any $k \in \{0, \ldots, k_1 \}$, we have
    \begin{align}
        c_\zeta 2^{\zeta^{-k}} \le m_k \le 2^{\zeta^{-k}},
    \end{align}
where $c_\zeta := 1 - 2^{\zeta - 1}$. Moreover, for any $k \in \{ k_1 + 1, \ldots, k_2 - 1 \}$, we have
    \begin{align}
        c_{\gamma} 2^{\zeta^{-k_1}\gamma^{-(k-k_1)}} \le m_k \le 2^{\zeta^{-k_1}\gamma^{-(k-k_1)}},
    \end{align}
where $c_{\gamma} := 1 - 2^{\gamma - 1}$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:07:48.553586+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a98dd1fef65ff833289909d69af13f8f0884a3ebd0f6c87e08001b70ccf854ea","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop 11 of arXiv:2609.01413, 'Sharp Threshold for Universality of Rational Canonical Forms over a Finite Field'] We have
\begin{equation}
\Aut\left(\bigoplus_{i=1}^k\bigoplus_{j\ge 1}(\F_p[t]/(f_i^{\l^{(f_i)}_j}))\right)=\prod_{i=1}^k\Aut_{f_i}(\lambda^{(f_i)}),
\end{equation}
where for all $1\le i\le k$, $\Aut_{f_i}(\lambda^{(f_i)})$ is the group of automorphisms of $\bigoplus_{j\ge 1}(\F_p[t]/(f_i^{\l^{(f_i)}_j}))$, and
\begin{equation}
\#\Aut_{f_i}(\lambda^{(f_i)})=p^{\deg f_i\cdot(|\l^{(f_i)}|+2n(\l^{(f_i)}))}\prod_{j\ge 1}(p^{-\deg f_i};p^{-\deg f_i})_{m_j(\l^{(f_i)})}.
\end{equation}
Here, the notation $(a;q)_m:=(1-a)(1-aq)\cdots(1-aq^{m-1}),m\ge 0$ refers to the $q$-Pochhammer symbol with the convention that $(a;q)_0=1$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:01:54.383902+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a98e1fddcfe32823052cf069f9cac492d7a8302e4f7b4e291973f64d106052dc","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm_new_moderate_deviations of arXiv:2411.02046, 'Speed of Convergence and Moderate Deviations of FPP on Random Geometric Graphs'] Consider first-passage percolation as in Theorem~(thm:speed.FPP of the paper), under the same assumptions as in that theorem. There exist~$C,C', c > 0$ such that for any~$x \in \mathbb R^d$ with~$\|x\|$ large enough, we have
	\begin{equation*}
		\p\left(\frac{|T(x) - \E[T(x)]|}{\sqrt{\|x\|}} > \ell \right) \le Ce^{-c \ell} \quad \text{for any } ~\ell \in \left[C'\log\big(\|x\|\big),\sqrt{\|x\|}\right].
	\end{equation*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:52:08.659204+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a98e34e0205cb7badaa936d556f7baec7895c37c737660652bf6b42aaa7eac81","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 8 of arXiv:2608.29984, 'On Cauchy Problems for Parabolic Equations with Rough Coefficients'] Assume that $K\geq 0$ is a continuous even function, and $K(x)\geq K(y)$ whenever $|x|\leq |y|$, then
\begin{equation}
\sup_{\mu>0}\left(\int_{\R}\frac{1}{\mu}K\left(\frac{y}{\mu}\right)|f(x-y)|dy\right)\lesssim \|K\|_{L^{1}(\R)}\mathcal{M}(f)(x).
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:32:56.101713+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a98e7ed81e2f6e55c6c50416cc0b199ec78cccddac0a55f12257c73cfe917921","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma Lem:ftc of arXiv:2411.13030, 'Classification of the limit shape for 1+1-dimensional FPP'] Let $f(x)$ be a Riemann-integrable function on $[0,1]$ and set $L=\int_0^1 f(x) \,dx$. For $\varepsilon>0$, define the sets
\[
D=\{x\in [0,1]:\, f(x)<0 \} , \quad L_\varepsilon=\{ x\in [0,1]: f(x)\in [0,L+\varepsilon] \}. 
\]
Then one of the following holds:
\begin{equation}

\left| \int_D  f(x) \, dx \right| \ge \frac \varepsilon 2.
\end{equation}
or
\begin{equation}

\mu(L_\varepsilon \cup D) \ge \frac \varepsilon {2(L+\varepsilon)}. 
\end{equation}
where $\mu(\cdot)$ denotes the Lebesgue measure.","status":"unverified"}

