{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:13:49.971463+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a9ea55889654c535ea20f0c8a3b52201079d6b451a14a2ad73aac4c7fc27f925","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:grafting0 of arXiv:2411.07157, 'Rough differential equations in the flow approach'] Given $\ell \in\N$ and a labelled tree $\underline{\sigma}\in\boldsymbol{\mathfrak{T}}$, we define a new labelled tree ${\underline{\sigma}}+\ell \in\boldsymbol{\mathfrak{T}}$ to be the labeled tree with node set ${N}(\underline{\sigma}+\ell) \eqdef \{ j + \ell : j \in N(\underline{\sigma})\}$ and such that the \textit{shift by $\ell$} defined as $ N(\underline{\sigma})\ni j \mapsto j+ \ell \in{N}(\underline{\sigma}+\ell)$ belongs to $\mathrm{Iso}(\underline{\sigma},\underline{\sigma}+\ell)$ -- in particular, we have $ r(\underline{\sigma}+\ell)= r(\underline{\sigma})+\ell$. 
Note that $\underline{\sigma} + 0 = \underline{\sigma}$. 

Finally, given two labelled trees $\underline{\tau},\underline{\sigma}\in\boldsymbol{\mathfrak{T}}$, we set 
\[
\ell_{\underline{\tau},\underline{\sigma}}
\eqdef 
 \begin{cases}  
    \max N(\underline{\tau}) + 1& \text{if} \;N(\underline{\tau}) \cap N(\underline{\sigma}) \not = \emptyset\,, \\
    0 & \text{otherwise} \,. \\
\end{cases}
\]
We overload notation, identifying $\ell_{\underline{\tau},\underline{\sigma}}$ with the shift-isomorphism and write $\ell_{\underline{\tau},\underline{\sigma}} \in \mathrm{Iso}(\underline{\sigma}, \underline{\sigma} + \ell_{\underline{\tau},\underline{\sigma}})$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:32:58.364356+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a9f011d821d285250ba878598629cbe866b31293154e4fe02716988d184f5207","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem 6 of arXiv:2506.20146, 'Parabolic Anderson Model in the Hyperbolic Space. Part I: Annealed Asymptotics'] The function $\{v^{t}(s,x):s\geqslant0,x=(\rho,\sigma)\in\Sigma_{R}\}$
is the solution to the following rescaled PAM:
\begin{equation}
\begin{cases}
\partial_{s}v^{t}={\cal L}^{t}v^{t}+\xi_{t}v^{t}, & (s,(\rho,\sigma))\in(0,\infty)\times\Sigma_{R};\\
v^{t}(s,\cdot)=0 & \text{on }\{\rho=R\};\\
v^{t}(0,\cdot)\equiv1,
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
where
\[
{\cal L}^{t}\triangleq\partial_{\rho}^{2}+\alpha(t)\coth(\alpha(t)\rho)\partial_{\rho}+\alpha^{2}(t)\sinh^{-2}(\alpha(t)\rho)\Delta_{\sigma}
\]
is the Laplacian under curvature $\kappa_{t}\equiv-\alpha^{2}(t)$
with $(\rho,\sigma)$ being viewed as the corresponding geodesic polar
coordinates. In particular, $v^{t}(s,x)$ admits the following Feynman-Kac
representation:
\begin{equation}
v^{t}(s,x)=\mathbb{E}\big[e^{\int_{0}^{s}\xi_{t}(W_{r}^{t,x})dr};W^{t,x}([0,s])\subseteq\Sigma_{R}\big],\ \ \ (s,x)\in[0,\infty)\times\Sigma_{R},
\end{equation}
where $\{W_{s}^{t,x}\}$ is a Brownian motion on $(\Sigma,g^{t})$
starting at $x$ that is independent of $\xi.$","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:00:15.217696+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a9f8fb29b7a968e79d28bed655a8fbdf41ff5b3cdb040bfbb3fad570a93c7cb3","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 3 of arXiv:2607.18186, 'Small Counterexamples to the Gaussian Moments Conjecture'] For every $A\in\C[z]$ and $m\geq1$,
