{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:20:33.749712+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ab4bcd6c0cf0117b9b218431e558da4df48e8bd2373e68c180a482f6c7c8fb0b","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 6 of arXiv:2608.18834, 'The Parabolic Anderson Model's Total Mass at Small Times: Geometry, Fluctuations, and Reno'] Let $B$ be a standard Brownian motion on $\mbb R^d$ (which is started at
the origin). For every $x\in\mbb R^d$, we define
\begin{align}

B^x=x+B.
\end{align}
For any stochastic process $Z$ (e.g., $B^x$), we denote the hitting time
\[\tau_D(Z)=\inf\{s\geq0:Z(s)\not\in D\}.\]
Then, we define the total mass of $u_{\ka,\eps}$ (which we denote $\msf M_{\ka,\eps}(t)$) as
\begin{align}

\int_Du_{\ka,\eps}(t,x)\d x:=\msf M_{\ka,\eps}(t)=\int_D\mbf E_B\left[\mbf 1_{\{\tau_D(B^x)>t\}}\mr e^{\ka\int_0^t\xi_\eps(t-u,B^x(u))\d u}\right]\d x
\end{align}
for every $\eps,t>0$, where we assume that $\xi$ and $B$ are independent,
and $\mbf E_B$ means that we are only taking the expectation with respect to $B$---i.e., conditional on $\xi$.
(This is clearly well-defined since $\xi_\eps$ is continuous.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:24:20.643981+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ab4e8d832488dac4e9e2becbfa1f69d13fc542f336d9eb44c36e307bb83d4325","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem of arXiv:2608.10415, 'Continuous random variables'] The basic random matrices $Z\in M_N(L^\infty(X))$ are as follows:
