{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:56:49.288396+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ab59b39a10dc16778f3083e0b900262aac1fe3978420df86a20229ac8debbbd5","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 35 (Young--L\'{o}eve's inequality) of arXiv:2606.22964, 'Signature McKean--Vlasov stochastic differential equations'] Given $x\in C^{\textup{$p$-var}}([0,T],\mathbb{R}^d)$ and $y\in C^{\textup{$q$-var}}([0,T];L(\mathbb{R}^d,\mathbb{R}^e))$, where $L(\mathbb{R}^d,\mathbb{R}^e)$ denotes the space of linear functional from $\mathbb{R}^d$ to $\mathbb{R}^e$, there exists a unique (indefinite) Young integral of $y$ against $x$ denoted by $\int_0^\cdot ydx$. Moreover, the indefinite Young integral has finite $p$-variation and
    \begin{equation*}
        \Bigg|\int_0^\cdot ydx\Bigg|_{p-\textup{var};[s,t]} \leq C|x|_{p-\textup{var};[s,t]}\left(|y|_{p-\textup{var};[s,t]} + |y_0|\right),
