{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:05:37.773432+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_acc72015cb78b9e48909bf89deb076b098d09dbc727bad19073a3a4f1e46d5d7","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem MainThm: LowerBound_Smaller_d of arXiv:2608.00399, 'The Heavy-tailed Frog Model'] Let $d \ge 1$. Assume $Q$ is a symmetric, translation-invariant, and heavy-tailed transition matrix on $\mathbb Z^d$ with exponent $\alpha \in (0, d)$. Consider the frog model on \( \mathbb Z^d \) equipped with $Q$. Fix any particle lifespan \( \ell \in \mathbb{N} \) and particle density \( \lambda > 0 \). Then, there exists a positive constant $c = c(Q, \lambda, \ell)$ such that $\mathbb P$-almost surely $\mathcal{A}(c(\log L)^\Delta) \subseteq B(L)$ for all large $L$ and 
