{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:56:03.897355+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_afa60ec8173e32a2e9f782fc648be3116d700c160ea1dc569423aa11852e3e9c","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 36 of arXiv:2606.19115, 'Finite free perpetuities'] Assume the sequence $(A_n,B_n)_n$ is admissible with
\begin{itemize}
\item[(i)] $p_{n}^{[i]}\in\pols_n(\R_{\geq 0})$ for all $i=1,\ldots,\kappa$, and $|\E[A_n^m]|<1$ for all $1\leq m\leq n$, 
\item[(ii)]   $\limsup_{n\to\infty}\E[A_n]<1$ and  $\limsup_{n\to\infty}\E[B_n]<\infty$,
\item[(iii)] the sequence $(\meas{p_{n}^{[i]}})_n$ converges weakly to some $\mu_i\in\MM(\R_{\geq 0})$, $i=1,\ldots,\kappa$. 
\end{itemize}
Then, for every \(n\in\N\), the finite free perpetuity \(p_{X_n}\) of order \(n\)
generated by \((A_n,B_n)\) exists, is unique, and has only nonnegative roots.

Moreover, the sequence $(\meas{p_{X_n}})_n$ converges weakly to some $\mu_{\X}\in\mathcal{M}(\R_{\ge0})$, which is uniquely determined by the free perpetuity fixed-point equation
\begin{align}
\mu_\X =  \mu_\kappa\boxast^{(\kappa)} \left( \mu_{\kappa-1}\boxast^{(\kappa-1)}\left(\cdots \mu_2 \boxast^{(2)}\left( \mu_1\boxast^{(1)} \mu_\X\right)\cdots \right)\right),
\end{align}
where $\boxast^{(i)} = \boxtimes$ if $i\in\mathcal{I}$ and $\boxast^{(i)} = \boxplus$ otherwise.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:21:52.420508+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_afaaa55b9ff70377349bab84ac401532ddb5d20b418304593d57640d6bc3a955","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 129 of arXiv:2601.16377, '2601.16377'] We introduce
