{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:55:18.064984+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b0f7a0378cfa57bd5d46cb0f941dd2529efe90677b797006a6125bfd7947dae4","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 28 (Types of nodes) of arXiv:2606.17452, 'Optional Stopping for Superhedging Supermartingales'] Given a trajectory space $\Se$ and a node $(S,j)$:
	\begin{itemize}
		\item $(S,j)$ is called an \emph{up-down node} if
		\begin{equation} 
			\sup_{\tilde{S} \in \Se_{(S, j)}}~~ (\tilde{S}_{j+1} - S_{j}) >    0\quad \mbox{and}\quad
			\inf_{\tilde{S} \in \Se_{(S, j)}} ~~(\tilde{S}_{j+1} - S_{j}) <    0.
		\end{equation}
		\item $(S,j)$ is called a \emph{flat node} if
		\begin{equation} 
			\sup_{\tilde{S} \in \Se_{(S, j)}}~~ (\tilde{S}_{j+1} - S_{j}) =    0 =
			\inf_{\tilde{S} \in \Se_{(S, j)}} ~~(\tilde{S}_{j+1} - S_{j}).
		\end{equation}
	\end{itemize}
	\noindent $(S, j)$ is called an arbitrage-free node if (\ref{upDownProperty}) or (\ref{flat}) hold, otherwise it
	is called an \emph{arbitrage node}. An arbitrage node $(S, j)$ is said to be of \emph{type I}, if there exists $\hat{S}\in\Se_{(S, j)}$	such that $\hat{S}_{j+1} = S_j$; otherwise it is said to be of \emph{type II}.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:36:49.820275+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b0fc7f51bd6cc24ccd57bfc146155db525f436ecf25f1a854392b15bf96e26d4","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:brownian-ball-localization of arXiv:2609.02829, 'Quantitative explosion and percolation of the divisible sandpile'] Assume $d<4$. For every $0<T<\infty$ there are $C=C(d)>0$ and $c=c(d)>0$
