{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:41:11.634722+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b15f6b7df7fd14f8f9bcb816cf03e15dcd04a7dab598dfe5c3dd08aed7f67b26","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition eul_id_projection of arXiv:2411.13707, 'High-degree cubature on Wiener space \\ through unshuffle expansions'] The Eulerian idempotent is a projection onto $\mathcal L(V)$, and $x \in T(V)$ can be expressed as a symmetric product of Lie elements by
\begin{equation}
x = \sum_{n \geq 0} \frac{1}{n!} {(\mathrm{e}(x^{(1)}),\dots ,\mathrm{e}(x^{(n)}))} .
\end{equation}
In other words, this provides an isomorphism
\begin{equation}
\bigoplus_{n = 0}^\infty \frac{\mathrm{e}^{\star n}}{n!} \colon T(V) \xrightarrow{\cong} \bigoplus_{n = 0}^\infty \mathcal L(V)^{\odot n}.
\end{equation}
\begin{proof}
Writing $\mathbbm 1 = \exp_\star (\log_\star(\mathbbm 1))$ yields the identity
\[
x = \exp_\star (\mathrm{e})(x) = \sum_{n \geq 0} \frac{\mathrm{e}^{\star n}(x)}{n!} ,
\]
which coincides with the expression in the first statement, thanks to (equation eq:star of the paper).
\end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

