  Moreover, assume that the memory size of the $N$-th system satisfies $\frac{C_N}{N} \mathop{\rightarrow}_N c$, with $0 < c \leqslant 1$. \\ Then, if $1-c$ is a continuity point of the quantile function $Q_\infty = G^{-1}_\infty$, the random threshold $\widehat{\theta}_N$ defined by eq. (equation eq:threshold_definition of the paper) converges ${\rm P}-$almost surely to $\theta^*= Q_\infty(1-c)$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:04:39.223932+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b20bc7f1d95577b10eebc45de04499432378b31ff538a6d9db037979c792060b","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 7 (Mod-$\phi$ convergence for descending plane partitions) of arXiv:2608.14143, 'Mod-$\phi$ convergence for random variables with cyclotomic generating functions'] For each $N\in\N$ let $X_N$ be a random variable with rescaled moment generating function of the form \eqref{eq:mgfDPP}. Then the sequence of random variables $X_N/N^2$ converges mod-$\phi$ with parameter $N$, and normalization function and limiting function given by
