{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:53:00.814480+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b3c1882c84e616d2a1680abab08b4a5e55fb99576a80cbd6824a36bd965be20f","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:type_path_induced of arXiv:2607.16911, 'Multipartite random graphs with given degrees: local limit, revisiting the giant, distance'] Fix $s_0,s_1 \in S$. Consider any rooted tree $\mathbb T$ with vertex types in $S$. For any vertex $v \in \mathbb T$ with $T_{p(v)}=s_0, T_v=s_1$, and a type path/ray $\pi=(s_0\rightarrow s_0s_1 \rightarrow s_0s_1s_2\rightarrow \dots)$, we define the subtree $\mathbb T(v;\pi)$ of $\mathbb T$ to be spanned by the vertices
    \begin{align*}
        \{v\}\cup \left(\cup_{i=2}^{|\pi|}C(e;\pi_{(i)})  \right),
    \end{align*}
    where $e$ is the directed edge $(p(v),v)$. We naturally think of $\mathbb T(v;\pi)$ to be a rooted tree, rooted at $v$. We call $\mathbb T(v;\pi)$ the subtree of $v$ in $\mathbb T$ induced by $\pi$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:12:20.553054+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b3cc1710de72168605723ca25d9a4b3451e60f61842d2d037257777c478bea05","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:prob_ball_given_tree of arXiv:2603.28936, 'Composition of random functions and word reconstruction'] Let $R$ be chosen uniformly at random in $[n]$ and $T\in \mathfrak{L}$. We have
\begin{align}
\mathbb P(B_{\omega}(R)\stackrel{lab}{=} T) = 
\mathbb P_{\scriptscriptstyle BGW}\bigl(\tau_\mathbf{Z}^{(lab)}= T\bigr) \left(1-\frac{c_{\textrm{approx}}^T(\omega)}{n}+O\Big(\frac{|T|^3}{n^2}\Big)\right),
\end{align}
where 
\begin{align}
c_{\textrm{approx}}^T(\omega)&:=  \frac{1}{2}\bigl[|T|(|T|-1)+t_a^-(\omega)(t_a^-(\omega)-2t_a^+(\omega)) + t_b^-(\omega)(t_b^-(\omega)-2t_b^+(\omega))\bigr].
\end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:36:31.240104+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b3d0a7f39964791233e061afe4581b13ce13d67c271dbb0e0049a9096ab3708c","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 65 of arXiv:2609.00567, 'Hodge--Tate splitting and Akizuki--Nakano vanishing in positive characteristic'] Let $k$ be a perfect field and let $X$ be a smooth projective scheme over $W(k)$.
If the closed fiber $X_{\F_p}$ is HT-split, then for every ample line bundle $L$ on $X$, the following vanishings hold;
\begin{align*}
    H^i_{(p)}(X,\Omega^j_{X/W(k)} \otimes L^{-1})=0 & \quad (i+j<\dim X), \\
    H^i(X_{\F_p}, \Omega^j_{X_{\F_p}} \otimes L_{\F_p}^{-1}) = 0 & \quad (i + j < \dim X - 1), \\
