{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:59:45.008099+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b42bc443fd452a67e5d68194d9bf7f77e7fd6fe4ad805c4fbb5f5f19ed3357bc","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition bound for Airy function of arXiv:2605.12777, 'Mesoscopic Rates of Convergence for Complex Wishart Matrices at the Leftmost Spectrum Edge'] [olver1997asymptotics] The Airy function $Ai(x)$ given in Definition (def4 classic DPPs of the paper) is the unique solution of the {Airy equation}: $y''(x)=xy(x)$ vanishing at $+\infty$. On $\mathbb{R}^+$, $Ai(x)$ is a decreasing function such that
