{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:56:36.588004+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b5489f51fead9c6ddf72bb87c467798240d8199fd8b6ecf7053ba1f448bae8b7","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn def:fsopwtp of arXiv:2608.30548, 'Finite speed of propagation and waiting time phenomena for stochastic porous media equatio'] Let $u$ be a non-negative, continuous, and $\mathscr{F}$-adapted,  $L^1(\mathbb T^d)$-valued process defined on $[0,T]$. For a point $x_0\in \mathbb T^d\setminus \mathrm{supp}(u_0)$ we define $R_0(x_0):= \mathrm{dist}_{\mathbb{T}^d}(x_0,\mathrm{supp}(u_0)) $. Then, for any $r\in (0,R_0]$, we say that $B_r(x_0)$ has \emph{waiting time $\tau_r(x_0)$}, where  
    \begin{equation}

        \tau_r(x_0) := \inf\left\{ t>0, \quad \int_0^t\int_{B_r(x_0)} u >0 \right\} \, \wedge\, T.
    \end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:44:27.992867+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b55a86448040994cd9ba422fb978c15f4410b4c224187a7ef813de5175ec6a1e","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 1 of arXiv:2607.13308, 'No infinite clusters at criticality for two-dimensional dependent percolation under abstra'] Given a percolation model satisfying~\eqref{h1},~\eqref{h2},~\eqref{h3},~\eqref{h4},~\eqref{h5}, and such that $\lambda_{c} \in (0,1)$, we have
    \begin{equation}
        \theta(\lambda_{c}) = \P_{\lambda_{c}} \big( 0 \leftrightarrow \infty \big) = 0.
    \end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:54:18.240632+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b55b2fa23d99a45c51af9244a9128a87f005b6d45d307ce4ea8c08141d5c4f35","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:positive-cells of arXiv:2608.29719, 'Persistence Pairing Graphs: Tracking Changes of Selected Homology Bases'] A cell $\sigma_i$ is called \emph{positive} if the $i$-th reduced column satisfies $R_i=0$, and \emph{negative} if $R_i\neq0$. If $\sigma_i$ is a positive $q$-cell, its \emph{selected birth cycle} is the $q$-chain
\begin{equation}

z_i := \sum_{\substack{\ell\leq i\\dim\sigma_\ell=q}}V_{\ell i}\sigma_\ell.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:28:49.117261+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b55b919ce449da833d37f4918b6a9b8e4cf785e7a7d1c2678145ce6a10541041","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:specular of arXiv:2608.17022, 'Sharp hypocoercive convergence estimates for underdamped Langevin dynamics with specular r'] Let $\mathscr C$ denote the set of functions $f\in C^2(\overline\Omega\times\mathbb{R}^d)$ such that
\begin{equation}
|\partial^\alpha f(x,v)|\le C_\alpha(1+|v|)^{N}
\quad\text{for all }(x,v)\in\overline\Omega\times\mathbb{R}^d\text{ and all multi-indices }\alpha\text{ with }|\alpha|\le2,
\end{equation}
where $\partial^\alpha$ ranges over all mixed derivatives in $(x,v)$, for some $N\in(0,\infty)$ and constants $C_\alpha<\infty$ depending on $f$, and
\begin{equation}
f(x,v)=f(x,R_xv)\qquad\text{for all }x\in\partial\Omega,\ v\in\mathbb{R}^d.
\end{equation}
We write $\mathscr C_0:=\mathscr C\cap\Lmuz$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:52:47.249400+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b55be378888a79c7a49f31080061eabc6f33316ebfeb9a6485a95b79dd720f20","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 10 (Volume doubling) of arXiv:2609.01098, 'Gaussian upper bounds for averaged heat semigroups on graphs'] Let $x\in X$,   {$R_2\geq R_1\geq 4S$}, $n>2$ and $\phi\geq 1$. If for all $r\in[R_1,R_2]$ {and $u\in\cC_{c}(B_x(r))$}, we have
\begin{equation*}
\frac{m(B_x(r))^{\frac{2}{n}}}{\phi r^2}
\Vert u\Vert_{\frac{2n}{n-2}}^2
\leq \cE(u)+\frac{1}{r^2}\Vert u\Vert_2^2,
\end{equation*}
then we have
{\[
\frac{m(B_x(R))}{R^{n}}
\leq {A(n)} \Phi_x(n,r,R)\ \frac{m(B_x(r))}{r^{n}}, \quad R_1\leq r\leq R\leq R_2,
\]
}
where
{
\[
\Phi_x(n,r,R)=\left[1\vee  r^n
{\frac{m(B_x(R))}{m(B_x(R_1))R^n}}
\right]^{ 4\sqrt[n]{S/r}},\quad\mbox{and}\quad A(n)=2^{ 8n^2}\phi^{\frac{n}{2}}(1\vee S)^{4n}.
\]
}
In particular, if $R_2= 2R_1$, we obtain 
\begin{align*}
	m(B_x(R_2))
\leq {A'(n)} \Phi_x(n,R_2) m(B_x(R_2/2)),\qquad A'(n)=2^{ 9n^2}\phi^{\frac{n}{2}}(1\vee S)^{4n}
\end{align*}
where $\Phi_x(n,R_2)=\left[
{\frac{m(B_x(R_2))}{m(B_x(R_2/2))}}
\right]^{ 6\sqrt[n]{S/R_2}}\geq \Phi_x(n,R_2/2,R_2)$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:51:12.739126+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b56118357133bd72893edce550e5e9086f3a9eb219051fe3e5c20fb39d2dd47a","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop 6 of arXiv:2605.02420, '\textbf{Large time behavior of critical marked Hawkes processes with heavy tailed marks an'] a) For any $m\in\N$ and $\theta_1,\ldots, \theta_m, t_1,\ldots, t_m\in (0,\infty)$ we have
\begin{equation}
 E\exp\left\{-\sum_{k=1}^m\theta_k Y_{T}(t_k)\right\}=\exp\left\{-\mu\int_0^\infty g_T(t)dt\right\},

\end{equation}
where
\begin{equation}
  g_{T}(t):=1-Ee^{-\<N^t, f_T\>},

 \end{equation}
 with $f_T$ given by \eqref{e:f_sum} and \eqref{e:fT} and recalling that $N^t$ denotes the empirical measure of a branching particle system starting from a single particle at point $t$ (with no immigration). Moreover, the function $g_T$ satisfies
\begin{align}
  g_{ T}(t)=&1-e^{-f_T(t)}
G\left(1-\int_0^\infty g_{T}(t+s)\varphi(s)ds\right)
 \\
 =& 1-e^{-f_T(t)}+e^{-f_T(t)}\int_0^\infty g_T(t+s)\varphi (s)ds -e^{-f_T(t)}H \left(\int_0^\infty g_T(t+s)\varphi(s) ds\right)
 .
\end{align}
b) Let the function $h_T$ be given by
\begin{equation}h_T(t):=E\<N^t,f_T\>

\end{equation}
Then $h_T$ 
satisfies the equation
\begin{equation}

 h_{T} (t) =  f_T(t)+\int_0^\infty  h_{T}(t+s)\varphi (s)ds.
\end{equation}
Moreover, $h_T$ may be expressed explicitly as
\begin{equation}
 h_T(t)=f_T(t)+\int_0^\infty f_T(t+s) R(s)ds,

\end{equation}
where $R$ is the resolvent defined in \eqref{e:R}.\\
c) The functions $g_T$ and $h_T$ satisfy
\begin{equation}
 0\le g_T(t)\le h_T(t)\qquad t\ge 0.

\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:20:07.902659+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b56a8ab9eeef23405b476698fa5429d07d4239131e7008cc5aba3703998c1a47","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:optimalweightoperator of arXiv:2608.23765, '\texorpdfstring{Tight Majorizations and Convergence Rates\\ of Nuclear Norm Minimization I'] Let $\varepsilon > 0$ and $\mathbf{X} \in \R^{d_1 \times d_2}$ be a matrix with the full singular value decomposition 
$\mathbf{X}= \mathbf{U}_{\mathbf{X}} \diag (\boldsymbol{\sigma}) \mathbf{V}_{\mathbf{X}}^\top$, 
where $\mathbf{U}_{\mathbf{X}}  \in \R^{d_1 \times d_1}$ and $\mathbf{V}_{\mathbf{X}} \in \R^{d_2 \times d_2}$ are orthogonal
and $\diag (\boldsymbol{\sigma}) \in \R^{d_1 \times d_2}$ is the rectangular diagonal matrix with the extended vector of singular values $\boldsymbol{\sigma} \in \R^{D}$ of $\mathbf{X}$ on the diagonal.
Then the  \emph{weight operator}  $\W{\mathbf{X}}{\varepsilon}: \R^{d_1 \times d_2} \to \R^{d_1 \times d_2}$ 
is defined by
\begin{equation} 
	\W{\mathbf{X}}{\varepsilon}(\mathbf{Z}) = \mathbf{U}_{\mathbf{X}} \left[\mathbf{H}_{\boldsymbol{\sigma},\varepsilon} \circ (\mathbf{U}_{\mathbf{X}}^{\top} \mathbf{Z} \mathbf{V}_{\mathbf{X}})\right] \mathbf{V}_{\mathbf{X}}^{\top}
\end{equation}
for each $\mathbf{Z} \in \R^{d_1 \times d_2}$.
Here $\mathbf{H}_{\boldsymbol{\sigma},\varepsilon}$ is the 
weight operator core matrix of (def:weightcore of the paper).","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:37:51.456292+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b56b58b5dbc35d337f410f3ef22826797b0d58016a42cdad60758169a94ef453","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem 15 of arXiv:2604.03777, 'Equilibrium fluctuations for a multi-species particle system with long jumps'] Assume Hypothesis \ref{hyplin}. Then, there exist some $\delta_1 \in (0, \;1]$ and some function $g: \mathbb{N} \mapsto \mathbb{R}$ satisfying $\lim_{n \rightarrow \infty} g(n)=0$, such that
	\begin{align} 
\forall \alpha,\beta \in S, \; \forall n \in \mathbb{N}_+, \; \forall	0 \leq s \leq t \leq T, \quad	\mathbb{E}_{\nu_{\rho}} \big[ \big\{ \mcb{B}_t^{n, \alpha , \beta}(H) - \mcb{B}_s^{n, \alpha , \beta}(H) \big\}^{2} \big] \leq  C (t-s)^{1 + \delta_1} g(n). 
