{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:09:59.304101+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b57360140013341c6c30908f245f0098d865545930214b4ccf75c1b4725d0eba","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition bdry_cov of arXiv:2608.07236, 'Negative association of Busemann functions \\in exponential last-passage percolation'] For any coordinate-wise monotone functions $f$ and $g$ that are monotone in the same direction (that is, either both are increasing or both are decreasing),
it holds that 
\begin{align} 
\textup{$\mathbb{C}$ov}\Big(f\big(G(\mathcal{B})\big), g\big( (G(\mathcal{R}^j_i))_{i,j=1}^{2k} ,(G(\mathcal{G}^j_i))_{i,j=1}^{2k}\big) \Big) \leq 0.
\end{align}
In addition, if we restrict the range of the index $j$, then we have
\begin{align} 
\textup{$\mathbb{C}$ov}\Big(f\big(G(\mathcal{B})\big), g\big( (G(\mathcal{G}^j_i))_{i=1,\dots,2k}^{j=1,\dots,k} , (G(\mathcal{R}^j_i))_{i=1,\dots,2k}^{j=k,\dots,2k} \big) \Big) = 0.
