{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:42:55.322188+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b6b1a0496fc8065321d2af6e9d02c9108c608ca3b0076f30f056c7387c26b106","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop 40 (Finite-window Kearns--Saul inequality) of arXiv:2609.02398, '\textbf{Convex Order Comparisons for Sub-Gamma Random Variables}'] {Let $p\in(0,1/2]$ and $a>0$. The inequality}
\begin{align}
    \E\left[e^{\lambda(\berv-p)}\right]
    \leq e^{A\lambda^2}
    \qquad\text{for all }|\lambda|\leq\frac1a

\end{align}
{holds with $A=A_p(a)$ and with no smaller constant, where}
\begin{align}
    A_p(a)
    &=
    \begin{cases}
        \displaystyle
        \frac{1-2p}
        {\displaystyle4\log\left(\frac{1-p}{p}\right)},
        & \displaystyle2\log\left(\frac{1-p}{p}\right)\leq\frac{1}{a},\\[8mm]
        \displaystyle
        a^2\log\left(
            p\,e^{(1-p)/a}
            +(1-p)\,e^{-p/a}
        \right),
        & \displaystyle 2\log\left(\frac{1-p}{p}\right)>\frac{1}{a}.
    \end{cases}

\end{align}
{For $p<1/2$, \eqref{eqn:kswindow} at $A=A_p(a)$ is an equality at exactly one nonzero $\lambda$ of the window: $\lambda=2\log\left((1-p)/p\right)$ on the first branch, $\lambda=1/a$ on the second. At $p=1/2$ the first branch is read by \eqref{eqn:lhopital} as $A_{1/2}(a)=1/8$.} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:23:37.802779+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b6b49e9c7eb962cdc37babf9065dbbffa88df4e975bb959cc7e509c6801af05e","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 9 (Strichartz estimates) of arXiv:2609.03558, 'Local well-posedness of the higher-order nonlinear Schr\"{o}dinger equation on the half-li'] Let $s\ge 0$, $(\mu,r)$ be higher-order Schrödinger admissible, and $\psi_0\in H_t^{\frac{s+1}{3}}(\mathbb{R})$, $\psi_1\in H_t^{\frac{s}{3}}(\mathbb{R})$ satisfy \eqref{supp-cond}.  Then, the solution of \eqref{HNLSreduced} represented by the formula \eqref{sol_for_reduced} satisfies 
