{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:12:20.554310+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b8967dfa9cd85473082906f92793030079bb03ba40d3d5629461ecb6514c22f0","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition Induced events of arXiv:2603.28936, 'Composition of random functions and word reconstruction'] For all $V_A^-,V_B^-,V_A^+,V_B^+\subseteq [n]$ and functions $\psi_a: V_A^+ \to V_A^-$ and $\psi_b: V_B^+ \to V_B^-$, we let $F(V_A^-,V_B^-,V_A^+,V_B^+,\psi_a,\psi_b)$ be the intersection of the following events (seen as events under the probability measure $\mathbb P$):
\begin{itemize}
\item[-] $\mathbf{a}(x)=\psi_a(x)$, for all $x\in V_A^+$;

\item[-] $\mathbf{b}(x)=\psi_b(x)$, for all $x\in V_B^+$;

\item[-]  $\mathbf{a}^{-1}(y)=\psi^{-1}_a(y)$, for all $y\in V_A^-$; and

\item[-]  $\mathbf{b}^{-1}(y)=\psi^{-1}_b(y)$, for all $y\in V_B^-$.
\end{itemize}
We denote $f_a^-=|V_A^-|$, $f_b^-=|V_B^-|$, $f_a^+=|V_A^+|$ and $f_b^+=|V_B^+|$. We call such an event an \emph{induced} event.  If the role of $V_A^-,V_B^-,V_A^+,V_B^+,\psi_a,\psi_b$ is irrelevant in the context, we write $F:= F(V_A^-,V_B^-,V_A^+,V_B^+,\psi_a,\psi_b)$ to keep the notation light. We also write $\textrm{Supp}(F):= V_A^-\cup V_B^-\cup V_A^+\cup V_B^+$ and $|F|:= |\textrm{Supp}(F)|$.","status":"unverified"}

