{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:55:27.265880+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b979b77764a13092ed804254d7c219d3c3b08c44f9f705ea38fff071f35bad1e","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma S31L of arXiv:2410.23899, 'Tightness for interlacing geometric random walk bridges'] Fix $p, \eta, M \in (0, \infty)$ and $\delta_1 \in (0,1/2)$. There exist $N_1 \in \mathbb{N}$ and $\delta > 0$, depending on $p, \eta, M, \delta_1$, such that the following holds. If $n \geq N_1$, $x, y \in \mathbb{Z}$ are such that $x \leq y$ and $|x| \leq M n^{1/2}$, $|y - pn| \leq M n^{1/2}$, $t \in [\delta_1, 1-\delta_1]$, and $a \in \mathbb{R}$, we have
