	where $\alpha_+$ and $\alpha_-$ are diffuse random measures, $\kappa_+$ and $\kappa_-$ are random variables in $\mathbb{N}\cup\{0\}\cup\{\infty\}$, $\beta_{+,1},\beta_{-,1},\beta_{+,2},\beta_{-,2},...$ are positive random variables, and $\sigma_{+,1},\sigma_{-,1},\sigma_{+,2},\sigma_{-,2},...$ are almost surely distinct random points in $S$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:53:00.844637+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b9d7e0e20a37dbda3d556c2264d8cc774997ae2a1934e0774d629275206fa39c","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition Prop: recursive form remainder of arXiv:2607.18635, 'Remainders of generalised Taylor expansions and a priori bounds for rough differential equ'] For any forest $w=\tau_1\cdots\tau_k\in{\mathcal F}$ and any rooted tree $\tau_0\in{\mathcal T}$ we have
		\begin{equation}
			\sum_{i=1}^k\frac{1}{\pi(\tau_i; w)}H_{z_1z_2}(w\setminus\tau_i;\tau_0) \, \Upsilon_{\tau_i}(z_1)-\frac{1}{\pi(w)}\Upsilon_{w\curvearrowright\tau_0}(z_1)=H_{z_1z_2}(w;\tau_0)\cdot z^{[1]}\,,
