{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:25:05.622849+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_bce9fa122a9b62f2d7c9c609e1751e2f564f0922d87dc7e896c274f472d754cc","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 5 of arXiv:2608.22112, '2608.22112'] \textbf{(Control of standard deviation under bounded trajectories).}
Suppose that the true and predicted trajectories satisfy a uniform bound
\[
\|\x(j)\|_2 \le M, \quad \|\tilde{\x}(j)\|_2 \le \tilde{M}
\quad \text{for all } j=1,\dots,m.
\]
Then the difference between the variances satisfies
\begin{equation}
\|\bm \sigma^2 - \tilde{\bm \sigma}^2\|_2 \le 2(M+\tilde{M}) \, \mathrm{RMSE}.
\end{equation}
Moreover, the difference between the standard deviations satisfies
\begin{equation}
\|\bm \sigma - \tilde{\bm \sigma}\|_2^2
\le
C \, \mathrm{RMSE},
\end{equation}
where $C = 2\sqrt{n}(M+\tilde{M}) >0$ depends only on $M$ and $n$ (and not on $m$).","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:17:10.022119+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_bcf0c2ab1b677b8e07c5c50d573bb40363de1260e01d7d0115fce345b7976610","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 68 of arXiv:2602.14618, 'Finitary coding and Gaussian concentration\\ for random fields'] Let $Y = (Y_n)_{n \in \Z}$ be a renewal process with $\gcd\{k\ge1:f_k>0\}=1$ (non-lattice inter-arrival distribution). The following are pairwise equivalent:
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\arabic*)]
\item $Y$ satisfies a Gaussian concentration bound;
\item $\sum_{k\ge1}s^kf_k<\infty$ for some $s>1$;
\item $Y$ is a finitary process with exponential tail coding radius; 
\item $Y$ is a finitary coding of an i.i.d. process.
\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:48:35.819042+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_bcf8fa7eb8d784f7740c69091caac43bc0bfdc03002924ccddd0956c306b64bf","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition Matrix Bennett-Bernstein inequality \eqref{eqn:mbi} of arXiv:2411.09516, 'Sharp Matrix Empirical Bernstein Inequalities\footnote{NeurIPS 2025 Poster}'] Let $\overline \mathbf{X} _n$ be the sample average of independent $d\times d$ symmetric matrices $ \mathbf{X} _1,\dots,  \mathbf{X} _n$ with common mean $ \mathbb E   \mathbf{X} _i =  \mathbf{M} $, common eigenvalue upper bound $\lambda_{\max}( \mathbf{X} _i) \leqslant B$, and $ \sum_{i=1}^n \mathbb E   \mathbf{X} _i^2 \preceq n  \mathbf{V} $. For any $\alpha > 0$
