{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:32:28.996731+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c0c8cf4fd8f609eb7098b148b2189e0836c24f517eee165f7f3398d1c7682848","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem hk est of arXiv:2608.25471, 'Exact Hausdorff measures and fine geometry of collisions of independent Markov processes'] Fix $x_0\in M$, $r\in(0, 1 \wedge r_*)$, and $s\in(0, 1 \wedge t_*)$.
We then have
\begin{equation}
  \sup_{(x_1, \ldots, x_k) \in M^k}
  \int_{B(x_0,r)}
  \prod_{i=1}^k p^i_s(x_i,y)\,\mu(dy)
  \leq
  \tilde{c}_1\!
  \left(
    s^{-\hat{d}_{\mathrm{s}}^*/2}
    \wedge
    r^{d_{\mathrm{f}}}s^{-\hat{d}_{\mathrm{s}}/2}
  \right).
\end{equation}
Moreover, if we set $c_1 \coloneqq \max_i (2 c_{\mathrm{LE}}^{-1})^{d_{\mathrm{w}}^{(i)}} \vee 1$, then, for all $s \geq c_1 r^{d^{\mathrm{min}}_\mathrm{w}}$,
\begin{equation}
  \inf_{(x_1,\ldots,x_k) \in \hat{B}_*(x_0,r)}
  \int_{B(x_0,r)}
  \prod_{i=1}^k p^i_s(x_i,y)\,\mu(dy)
  \ge
  \tilde{c}_2\, r^{d_{\mathrm{f}}}s^{-\hat{d}_{\mathrm{s}}/2}.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:39:18.537756+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c0cab04d7ff07275c5c8e780c693809b2670493bffe4b9fa252dcfa2d1189161","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm 2 of arXiv:2609.02327, 'On the counting function of square-full numbers'] We assume the strong Riemann hypothesis. For any $x\geqslant x_0$, where $x_0$ is sufficiently large, there are effective complex constant $c_{\{\gamma /6 \}}$ such that the asymptotic formula
			\begin{equation}
				\sum_{n\leqslant x} f_2(n)= \frac {\zeta (\frac {3}{2})}{\zeta (3)} x^{\frac {1}{2}} + \frac {\zeta (\frac {2}{3})}{\zeta (2)}  x^{\frac {1}{3}}+	
				\sum_{\substack{\rho=\frac{1}{12}+i\frac{\gamma}{6},\\|\gamma |< c_1 x^{\frac {1}{2}},\\\zeta(6\rho)=0}}c_{\frac{\gamma}{6}} \ x^{\frac{1}{12}+i\frac{\gamma}{6}}
				+O_{\epsilon} \left( x^{10 \epsilon} \right)
			\end{equation}
			holds. We also have
			\begin{equation}
				\sum_{n\leqslant x} f_2(n)= \frac {\zeta (\frac {3}{2})}{\zeta (3)} x^{\frac {1}{2}} + \frac {\zeta (\frac {2}{3})}{\zeta (2)}  x^{\frac {1}{3}}+	
				\sum_{\substack{\rho=\frac{1}{12}+i\frac{\gamma}{6},\\|\gamma |< c_2 x^{\frac {5}{12}},\\\zeta(6\rho)=0}}c_{\frac{\gamma}{6}} \ x^{\frac{1}{12}+i\frac{\gamma}{6}}
				+O_{\epsilon} \left( x^{\frac {1}{12} + 10 \epsilon} \right).
			\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:26:40.357769+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c0cf16ae6466abc66477afa8a7ad7d63837b00c1b3fca78a6cbe14c70c89023e","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 19 of arXiv:2511.09280, 'Random Walk conditioned to stay above a non-flat floor: curvature effects'] For any \(1\leq k\leq n/2\), \(a> 0\),
