{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:46:00.369328+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c4526c8a98054c5834598720e55ff1ac5e6168863dda843cdbfbecd8ab757f5f","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition kpoint of arXiv:2607.21443, 'The critical KPZ scale for the averaging process'] For $k\in \Bbb N$, define the \textit{$k$-point motion} to be the continuous-time Markov process $\mathbf R=(R^1,\ldots,R^k)$ on $\Bbb Z^k$ with transition probabilities  given, for every $t\ge0$ and $\mathbf x = (x_1,\ldots,x_k), \mathbf y=(y_1,\ldots,y_k)\in \mathbb{Z}^k$, by
    \begin{equation}\mathsf p^{(k)}_t(\mathbf x, \mathbf y)\coloneqq \Bbb E \bigg[ \prod_{j=1}^k K_t (x_j,y_j)\bigg]\; \text{.}
    \end{equation} 
     Let $\ensuremath{\mathcal L}^{(k)} (\mathbf x,\mathbf y):= \frac{{\rm d}}{{\rm d} t}\big|_{t=0}\, \mathsf p^{(k)}_t(\mathbf x,\mathbf y) $, $\mathbf x, \mathbf y \in \mathbb{Z}^k$, denote the corresponding generator, while
    $\mathbf P_\mathbf x^{(k)}$ and $\mathbf E_\mathbf x^{(k)}$ stand for the path law and corresponding expectation when starting from $\mathbf x\in \mathbb{Z}^k$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:38:58.695285+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c45521ebf11b2711abe78544e0f450160d87b6b38267bcbe7901810242564145","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:marginals of arXiv:2510.22778, 'Free denoising diffusion models'] The unique adapted solution of (equation eq:forward of the paper) is
\begin{equation}
  X_{t}=\sqrt{\alpha_{t}}\,X_{0}
  +\int_{0}^{t}\sqrt{\tfrac{\alpha_{t}}{\alpha_{s}}}\,\sqrt{\beta(s)}\;\mathrm{d} S_{s},
\end{equation}
and its spectral law is
\begin{equation}
  \mu_{t}:=\mu_{X_{t}}=\big(D_{\sqrt{\alpha_{t}}}\mu_{0}\big)\boxplus\gamma_{1-\alpha_{t}},
\end{equation}
where $D_{c}\mu$ denotes the pushforward of $\mu$ under $x\mapsto cx$. In particular
$\int x^{2}\mathrm{d}\mu_{t}=\alpha_{t}\int x^{2}\mathrm{d}\mu_{0}+(1-\alpha_{t})$ and
$\mu_{t}\to\gamma$ in $W_{2}$ as $\Lambda(t)\to\infty$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:11:44.380036+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c455b24305ef02fb43e68d372b6e9cc2f7253dc5306ce013e5c07e0f121938d9","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 6 of arXiv:2609.03112, '2609.03112'] Let $\Gamma_H<H$ be commensurable with
$\operatorname{SL}(2,\Z)\ltimes\Z^2$, let $\mu_Y$ be Haar probability
measure on $Y=\Gamma_H\backslash H$, and let $\alpha\notin\Q$.
For every bounded continuous $F:Y\to\C$ and every $h\in C_c(\R)$,
\begin{equation}
 \lim_{v\to0}\int_\R F(\Gamma_Hg_\alpha n(u)a(v))h(u)\,du
 =\left(\int_YF\,d\mu_Y\right)\left(\int_\R h(u)\,du\right).
\end{equation}
Suppose in addition that $\alpha$ is Diophantine of type $\kappa$ in the
sense of \eqref{eq:diophantine}.  If $h_0\in C_c(\R)$ is nonnegative,
$0<\epsilon<1$, and $0<\epsilon'<1/(\kappa-1)$, then the function
\eqref{eq:cusp-majorant} satisfies
\begin{equation}
 \limsup_{v\to0}\int_{|u|>v^{1-\epsilon}}
 F_R(g_\alpha n(u)a(v))h_0(u)\,du
 \ll_{\alpha,f_0,h_0,\epsilon,\epsilon'}R^{-\epsilon'/2}.
\end{equation}
Consequently, if $F$ is continuous and dominated by $F_R$, then
\begin{equation}
 \lim_{v\to0}\int_{|u|>v^{1-\epsilon}}
 F(g_\alpha n(u)a(v))h(u)\,du
 =\left(\int_YF\,d\mu_Y\right)\left(\int_\R h(u)\,du\right).
\end{equation}
The same statements hold on a finite cover of $Y$ and on a finite union of
rational translates of such covers. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:24:59.498146+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c455fedcec149bab8bed71bb0241947143237ddde117da97fb1182660594d07e","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 18 of arXiv:2605.09706, 'A dyadic construction of a three-dimensional attractive point interaction Markov family'] Fix $t, \beta>0$ and $x\in\R^3\setminus\{0\}$. The function $Q_t^\beta$ admits the representation
\begin{align}
Q_t^\beta(x)
\, = \,
\frac{1}{4\pi\beta\,|x|\,\sqrt{\pi t}}
\int_{0}^{\infty} \,
\big(e^{4\pi\beta w}\,-\, 1\big)\,
e^{-\frac{(|x|+w)^2}{4t}}\,dw\,.
\end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:47:12.025226+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c4576cf814ab5f9abe1cc8f8c0b850374885fdf7bebe682fb3a83f4639efb754","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theo thm:frostman-intro of arXiv:2512.06984, 'Infinite Dimensional Multifractal Analysis of the Wiener measure'] Let $ (X,d) $ be a $\sigma$-compact metric space and $ A \subset X$ a Borel subset. Then for every Hausdorff function $h$  such that $\mathcal{H}^h(A) > 0 $, there exist a compact set $K\subset A$,
a  probability Borel measure $\mu$ supported by $K$, and a constant $C\in(0,\infty)$
such that 
\begin{equation*} 
\mu\bigl(B(x,r)\bigr)\ \le\ C\,h( 6 r) , \qquad\forall\,x\in X,\ \forall\,r>0.
\end{equation*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:56:14.240414+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c4578883dd92ed9d16d3a6760188775021d4fdb7f894fc809a8228078d7ac262","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop connection of arXiv:2412.09471, 'On the moderate deviation principles in the sparse multi-type Erd\H{o}s R\'enyi random gra'] For all $(\gamma_k)\in{\mathbb N}^{{\mathbb S}_n}$ such that $\sum_{k\in {\mathbb S}_n}k\gamma_k=\mu^n n$, 
    \begin{multline*}
        {\mathbb P}\left(t_n(k)=\gamma_k, \forall k\in{\mathbb S}_{n}\right)
={\mathbb P}\left(\sum_{i=1}^{N(nZ_n)}\ind{X_i^{n}=k}=\gamma_k,\forall k\in{\mathbb S}_n\bigg|\sum_{i=1}^{N(nZ_n)}X_i^{n}=\mu^n n\right).
    \end{multline*}
    Moreover,
    \begin{multline}
         \mathbb{P}\left(\sum_{i=1}^{N(nZ_n)}X_i^n=\mu^n n\right)=
         \frac{e^{-Z_n n}
         n^n
         \prod_{r,s}{(1-\frac{\kappa(r,s)}{n})^{\frac{n^2\mu^n_s\mu^n_r}{2}}\prod_s{(\mu^n_s)^{n\mu^n_s}}}}

         {\prod_{s\in\mathcal{S}}(n\mu^n_s)!}.
    \end{multline}
    For any $k\in {\mathbb S}_n$,
    \[{\mathbb P}\left(\mathcal{C}^n_{\max}=k\right)={\mathbb P}\left(\max_{1\leq i\leq N(nZ_n)}X_i^n=k\bigg|\sum_{i=1}^{N(nZ_n)}X_i^{n}=\mu^n n\right).
    \]
    For all $l\in{\mathbb N}^+$,
    \[
    {\mathbb P}\left(C_n=l\right)={\mathbb P}\left(N(nZ_n)=l\bigg|\sum_{i=1}^{N(nZ_n)}X_i^n=\mu^n n\right).
    \]","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:28:53.657239+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c45a434c9540218d1df233199d0735f7487a2f5cd16c64e6be4ce1169c16c38d","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 2 (diffusion local time) of arXiv:2605.13648, 'Sticky CIR process with potential: invariant measure and exact sampling'] \label[definition]{diffusion-local-time}
  For a diffusion $X$ with speed measure $m$, the \emph{diffusion local time} $\ell_t^a$ at level $a$ is defined by
\[
\ell^a_t(X) = \lim_{\varepsilon \downarrow 0} \frac{1}{m((a-\varepsilon, a+\varepsilon))} \int_0^t \mathbf{1}_{\{|X_s - a| < \varepsilon\}}\,ds.
\]
The following occupation-time formula holds: 
for measurable $f \geq 0$,
\begin{equation}
\int_0^t f(X_s)\,ds = \int f(a)\,\ell^a_t(X)\,m(da).
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:05:09.526121+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c45e7c748f9e06289dff534ad956b947c1e01679e5b1f3d7fc796423d47b8a95","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary of arXiv:2411.03512, 'Ergodicity and Mixing of Sublinear Expectation System and Applications'] Suppose that $\{X_k\}_{k\geq 1}$ is a d-dimensional $\alpha$-mixing stationary sequence on a regular sublinear expectation space $(\Omega,\mathcal{H},\hat{\mathbb{E}})$ for some $\alpha>0$ with $\hat{\mathbb{E}}[|X_1|^{2}]<\infty$. If $X_1$ has no mean-uncertainty, then 
        \begin{equation*}
         \lim_{n\to\infty}\frac{1}{n}\sum_{k=1}^nX_k=\hat{\mathbb E}[X_1], \ \hat{\mathbb{E}}\text{-q.s.}
     \end{equation*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:31:14.333557+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c4625c44351cf88904e95feae4c222682a82ec91de6c781fcee8ac865bff65c4","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma of arXiv:2606.16609, 'On stability of outliers from the circular law'] Set $Q_N(z):=1+b_N^*\mathbf{R}_{N}(z)\mathbf{R}_{N}(z)^*b_N$ for $z \notin Sp (D_N)$ and denote by $\mu_1,\ldots,\mu_{N-1}$ the eigenvalues of $D_N$. Then 
   \begin{equation*}
