{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:59:53.163466+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c4d390e3192e9a3889c0fcd3e3da1eda1306b7a0175f525b7bc8cdec4c64ddd4","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 15 of arXiv:2607.02489, '2607.02489'] Let $\fS\in\upGamma_0(\HS)$ be such that $\Argmin\fS\neq\emp$, let
$(\upgamma_{\nS})_{\nnn}$ be a decreasing sequence in $\RPP$ 
such that $\sum_{\nnn}\upgamma_{\nS}=\pinf$, let
$(\mu_{\nS})_{\nnn}$ and $(\rho_{\nS})_{\nnn}$ be sequences of 
$\RP$-valued random variables, and let $x_0\in
L^2(\upOmega,\FE,\PP;\HS)$. Iterate
\begin{equation}

\begin{array}{l}
\textup{for}\;\nS=0,1,\ldots\\
\left\lfloor
\begin{array}{l}
x_{\nS+1}\underset{\mu_{\nS},\rho_{\nS}}
{\overset{\FE_{\nS}}{\approx}}
\prox_{\upgamma_{\nS}\fS}x_{\nS}
\end{array}
\right.\\
\end{array}
\end{equation}
and suppose that 
$\sum_{\nnn}\EC{\mu_{\nS}}{\FE_{\nS}}<\pinf\;\Pas$ and 
$\sum_{\nnn}\upgamma_{\nS}^{-1}\EC{\rho_{\nS}}{\FE_{\nS}}
<\pinf\;\Pas$
Then the following hold:
\begin{enumerate}
\item

$(x_{\nS})_{\nnn}$ converges weakly $\Pas$ 
to an $(\Argmin\fS)$-valued random variable.
\item

Suppose that $\sum_{\nnn}\EE\mu_{\nS}<\pinf$ and 
$\sum_{\nnn}\EE\rho_{\nS}<\pinf$. Then
$(x_{\nS})_{\nnn}$ converges weakly in $L^2(\upOmega,\FE,\PP;\HS)$
to an $(\Argmin\fS)$-valued random variable.
\item

Set $(\forall\nnn)$ 
$\uptheta_{\nS}=\sum_{\kS=0}^{\nS}\upgamma_{\kS}$. Suppose that 
$\sum_{\nnn}\uptheta_{\nS}\EC1{\mu_{\nS}}{\FE_{\nS}}<\pinf\;\Pas$ 
and that $\sum_{\nnn}\upgamma_{\nS}^{-1}\uptheta_{\nS}
\EC1{\rho_{\nS}}{\FE_{\nS}}<\pinf\;\Pas$
Then $\fS(x_{\nS})-\inf\fS(\HS)
=\mathrm{o}(1/\uptheta_{\nS})\;\Pas$
\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:25:06.181320+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c4d444fab1d2a63a5b381d9411083eda912bbf863b924cbf31766926dd2a6194","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 3 (Regularity of the coefficient fields) of arXiv:2608.22636, 'Q-Learning with Stable Infinite-Dimensional \\Linear Function Approximation'] Suppose Assumptions~\ref{assump:mdp}, \ref{assump:stable_kernels}, and \ref{assump:stepsizes} hold. Then, for every $n\ge0$, $\theta_n^\mrm U,\theta_n^\mrm N\in\Theta$. Moreover, the coefficient fields are also Lipschitz with
