{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:41:03.290750+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c9620eca02c2a97ec96650e730522a96d023c662186cb5680ce9ee43eb17fef6","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm_Portenko of arXiv:2411.08846, 'On the Number of Crossings and Bouncings\\ of a Diffusion at a Sticky Threshold'] [Efimenko-Portenko (1989), see~[Port94]]

	We consider the setting of Lemma~(thm_Altmeyer of the paper). 
	For all $t>0 $, 

	$\sum_{i=1}^{[nt]} \indicb{\hfprocess{X}{}{i-1}\hfprocess{X}{}{i}<0}$
	converges in law to some discrete random variable $Z_t $
	with values in $\IN_0 $, i.e., there exists $(b_k(t);\; k\in \IN_0,\, t\ge 0) $ such that $\Prob_0 \left( Z_t = k \right) = b_k(t) $, for all $k\in \IN_0 $.
	The probabilities $(b_k(t);\; k\in \IN_0,\, t\ge 0) $ are defined via their Laplace transforms 
	\begin{equation}
		\begin{aligned}
			\int_{0}^{\infty} \lambda e^{-\lambda t} b_k(t) \,\mathrm{d} t &= 
			\frac{A_{\lambda}}{1/\rho^{2} + A_{\lambda}}
			\left( \frac{1/\rho^{2}}{1/\rho^{2}+A_{\lambda}} \right)^{k},
			& \forall &k\in \IN_0,
			& \forall & t\ge 0,  
		\end{aligned}
	\end{equation}
	with $A_{\lambda}= \sqrt{\lambda} \left( \sqrt \lambda + \sqrt{2}/\rho \right) $. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:33:11.957713+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c964de8b8c1829309a9355dfa3ae8ac3115f5270365c996246e74421af9513e1","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma eq:zero-first-moment of arXiv:2607.14087, 'Stochastic Domination of Gaussian Maxima: A Resolution of the Weak Simplex Conjecture'] \label[lemma]{thm:centered-product}
Let $R$ be an $m\times m$ correlation matrix and let $X\sim\mathcal N(0,R)$.  For
each $i$, let $f_i:\mathbb R\to[0,\infty)$ be bounded and log-concave, with positive
Gaussian integral, and suppose that
\begin{equation}
  \int_{\mathbb R}z f_i(z)\,d\gamma_1(z)=0.
\end{equation}
Then
\begin{equation}
  \mathbb E\prod_{i=1}^m f_i(X_i)
  \geq \prod_{i=1}^m \mathbb E f_i(X_i)
  =\prod_{i=1}^m\int_{\mathbb R}f_i\,d\gamma_1.
\end{equation}
The covariance matrix $R$ may be singular. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:20:07.902280+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c96df0056b4a1ceb8d456437c7ae63a77704ce1495370eecc2dcdb5b482befec","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma epslowerbound of arXiv:2608.23765, '\texorpdfstring{Tight Majorizations and Convergence Rates\\ of Nuclear Norm Minimization I'] Assume that the linear measurement operator $\mathcal{A}: \mathbb{R}^{d_1 \times d_2} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}^m$ satisfies the NSP of order~$r$ with constant $ \eta_r < 1 $.
	Moreover, assume that $\mathbf{X}_\star$ has rank~$r$.
    Let $\left(  \mathbf{X}^{(k)}\right)_{k\geq 0}$ and $ \left(\varepsilon_{k} \right)_{k \geq 0}$ be the iterates and smoothing parameters of $\texttt{MatrixIRLS}$ with input $\mathbf{y} = \mathcal{A}(\mathbf{X}_\star)$, arbitrary initial weight operator $\mathbf{W}^{(0)}$, $\widetilde{r} = r$, based on $q$-power mean weight operators with $q \in [-1,\infty]$ or on one-sided weight operators $\W{\mathbf{X}^{(k)}}{\varepsilon_k}(\cdot)$.
	Then, for all natural numbers $k \ge 1$, it holds that 
	\begin{equation*}
		\varepsilon_k \ge  \vartheta \frac{\besterrNuc{\mathbf{X}^{(k)}}{r}}{d},
	\end{equation*}
	where
	\begin{equation*}
	\vartheta
	:=
	\frac{1-\eta_r}{\left(1+\eta_r\right)\left(\frac{3}{2}+\eta_r\right)}.
	\end{equation*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:58:09.039802+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c975d1ee0d34b5bf322fbdfe539cc819234189b07f334e4a1a9909a2fdb7900f","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 2 of arXiv:2606.26619, 'Ergodicity of stochastic reaction-diffusion equations on unbounded domains driven by space'] Let $u$ be the solution to the following equation driven by space-time white noise $W$:
\begin{align}
\begin{cases}
    d u(t,x) = \frac{1}{2}\partial_{xx} u(t,x) dt  + W(dt,dx), \quad x\in\mathbb{R},\\
    u(0,x) = f(x), \quad x\in\mathbb{R},
\end{cases}
\end{align}
where $f\in L^p_{\lambda}$ for some $p\geq 1$. 
