{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:59:58.439962+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ca83de13f13125dd5643ce7a2768fc1a1c0e24c04c6584c305cc664856613e2c","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def.finite-volHamiltonian of arXiv:2604.24743, 'The impact of disorder and non-convex interactions on delocalisation of height functions'] Let $\Lambda \subseteq \ensuremath{\mathbb{Z}^d}_n$ be a finite set, and let $(J_{xy})_{ \{ x, y \} \in E(\Lambda)} \in [0, \infty)^{E(\Lambda)} $ be a collection of non-negative conductances. We define the Wells' disorder to be the probability distribution on the space of site percolation configurations $\{0 , 1\}^{\Lambda \cap \ensuremath{\mathbb{Z}^d}}$ given by the identity: for any $r \in \{0 , 1\}^{\Lambda \cap \ensuremath{\mathbb{Z}^d}}$
    \begin{equation} 
