{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:42:39.096385+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ccb0c64e6236cca6041dd048cb0b8a46ef957bcedc74a14d99154db8f4dc98cd","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defi defi of arXiv:2412.08497, 'Time discretization of Quadratic Forward-Backward SDEs with singular drift'] A functional $\Phi: \mathbb{D}^d \rightarrow \mathbb{R}$ is called $L^{\infty}\text{-Lipschitz}$, if there exists a constant $\Lambda_{\Phi}> 0$ such that 
				\begin{align}
					|\Phi(\bold{x}_1)-\Phi(\bold{x}_2)| \leq \Lambda_{\Phi} \sup_{ 0\leq t\leq T}|\bold{x}_1(t) -\bold{x}_2(t)|, \quad  \forall \bold{x}_1,\bold{x}_2 \in \mathbb{D}^d; 
				\end{align}
				and $\Phi: \mathbb{D}^d \rightarrow \mathbb{R}$ is called $L^{1}\text{-Lipschitz}$, if it satisfies
				\begin{align}
					|\Phi(\bold{x}_1)-\Phi(\bold{x}_2)| \leq \Lambda_{\Phi} \int_{ 0}^{T}|\bold{x}_1(t) -\bold{x}_2(t)|\mathrm{d}t, \quad  \forall \bold{x}_1,\bold{x}_2 \in \mathbb{D}^d.
				\end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:41:11.631739+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ccba57f3f171955230c381d835cb502788da11ea155c850c6979b9fbcbcc7c98","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:lerw of arXiv:2411.13505, 'Capacity of loop-erased random walk'] Let $\omega\in\Omega$ be a one-sided nearest-neighbour, transient path in $\mathbb{Z}^d$. The loop-erasure $\LE(\omega)$ of $\omega$ is the path obtained by erasing loops from $\omega$ chronologically. To be precise, $\LE(\omega)_i=\omega_{\ell_i}$, where the times $(\ell_i)_{i\geq0}$ are defined inductively as $\ell_0=0$ and $\ell_{i+1}=1+\max\{k\colon\omega_k=\omega_{\ell_i}\}$. When $S$ is a simple random walk on $\mathbb{Z}^d$ for $d\geq3$, we write
\begin{equation}
	\eta:=\LE(S[0,\infty)).
\end{equation}
This is well-defined, since simple random walk is almost surely transient for $d\geq3$.","status":"unverified"}

