{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:05:09.624628+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ce659606f11c771f43f7dfedadd1ed59f6a607284fcc14c05aed251817aab158","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def: alpha-mixing of arXiv:2411.03512, 'Ergodicity and Mixing of Sublinear Expectation System and Applications'] A process $\{X_t\}_{t\geq 0}$ of $d$-dimensional random vectors on a sublinear expectation space $(\Omega, \mathcal{H}, \hat{\mathbb{E}})$ is said to be $\alpha$-mixing if there exist $c_X>0,\alpha>0$ such that for any non-empty finite subset $\Lambda^1,\Lambda^2\subset \mathbb{R}_+$ with $\Lambda^1\leq \Lambda^2$ and $\phi\in C_{lip}(\mathbb{R}^{2d})$,
		\begin{equation}
			\begin{split}
				|\hat{\mathbb{E}}[\phi(\bar{X}_{\Lambda^1}, \bar{X}_{\Lambda^2})] - \hat{\mathbb{E}}[\hat{\mathbb{E}}[\phi(x, \bar{X}_{\Lambda^2})]|_{x=\bar{X}_{\Lambda^1}}]| \leq c_X l_{\phi} e^{-\alpha |\Lambda^2_{min}-\Lambda^1_{max}|},
			\end{split}
		\end{equation}
		where $\bar{X}_{\Lambda^i}=\frac{1}{|\Lambda^i|}\sum_{s\in \Lambda^i}X_s$, $i=1,2$, $l_{\phi}$ is the Lipschitz constant of $\phi$.

   Similarly, a sequence $\{X_k\}_{k\geq 0}$ of $d$-dimensional random vectors on a sublinear expectation space $(\Omega, \mathcal{H}, \hat{\mathbb{E}})$ is called $\alpha$-mixing if (equation equ: alpha-mixing of the paper) holds for non-empty finite subsets $\Lambda^1,\Lambda^2\subset \mathbb{N}$ with $\Lambda^1\leq \Lambda^2$.","status":"unverified"}

