{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:26:00.619550+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cebe3a3b110b6b0f91d1a899cf89363ef4f6b241e5d78afcd0444b5b919e5bc9","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop 6 of arXiv:2511.00679, 'Stochastic solutions to abstract telegraph-type equations involving fractional dynamics'] The solution of the fractional Cauchy problem involving the Bessel-Riesz operator
\begin{align}
&\bigg[(D_t^{\alpha})^2+2\lambda D_t^{\alpha}+(-\Delta)^{\beta/2}(I-\Delta)^{\gamma/2}\bigg] u =0,\quad t> 0, \ \alpha\in (0,1], \beta \in(0,2], \gamma \geq 0, \\
&u(0)=f,\ \alpha \in(0,1],\\
& D_t^{\alpha}u(0)=0,\ \alpha \in(1/2,1],
\end{align}
is given by the convolution product $u_\alpha(t,x)=(f\ast \Phi_\alpha(t))(x),$ for each $x\in\mathbb R^d,$where
\begin{equation}
\Phi_\alpha(t,x)=\frac{1}{(2\pi)^{\frac d2}||x||^{\frac d2-1}}\int_0^\infty r^{\frac d2}J_{\frac d2-1}(r||x||)\widehat\Phi_\alpha(t,r)\de r,\quad t>0
\end{equation}
and 
\begin{align*}
\widehat \Phi_\alpha(t,\xi) &=\frac{1}{2}\left[
\bigg(1+\frac{\lambda}{\sqrt{\lambda-||\xi||^\beta(1+||\xi||^2)^{\gamma/2}}}\bigg)E_{\alpha,1}(r_1t^{\alpha})\right.\\
&\quad\left.+\bigg(1-\frac{\lambda}{\sqrt{\lambda-||\xi||^\beta(1+||\xi||^2)^{\gamma/2}}}\bigg)E_{\alpha,1}(r_2t^{\alpha})\right],
\end{align*}
where 
\begin{align}
\nonumber & r_1 := -\lambda+\sqrt{\lambda^2-||\xi||^\beta(1+||\xi||^2)^{\gamma/2}},\\
\nonumber & r_2 := -\lambda-\sqrt{\lambda^2-||\xi||^\beta(1+||\xi||^2)^{\gamma/2}}.
\end{align}
Moreover, for $\alpha \in (0,\frac{1}{2}]$ and $\beta+\gamma\in(0,2],$ the solution to the problem \eqref{es2eq:fractelc}-\eqref{es2eq:fractelc2} has the following stochastic representation 
\begin{equation*}
u_\alpha(t,x)=\mathbb E_x\left[f(Y_d^{\beta,\gamma}(\mathcal{L}^\alpha(t) ))\right] ,\quad t>0,x\in\mathbb R^d. 
\end{equation*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:26:52.666474+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cebf340f792774a5ce8d2289ea2fcfd78ad26a84edad7d081cbed961dd0e1ece","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 18 (Keyl--Werner) of arXiv:2609.03736, '2609.03736'] Let $\tau$ be a density matrix on $\CC^m$ with decreasing eigenvalue list
$s$, and let $\Pi_\lambda^{(N)}$ be the $\mathfrak S_N$-isotypic projection
of shape $\lambda$ in $(\CC^m)^{\otimes N}$.  The numbers
\begin{equation}
 \mathbb P_{\tau,N}(\lambda)
 =\tr\bigl(\tau^{\otimes N}\Pi_\lambda^{(N)}\bigr)

\end{equation}
form a probability measure on partitions of $N$ with at most $m$ parts.
View $\lambda/N$ as an $m$-vector by appending zero parts.  For every
$\varepsilon>0$,
\begin{equation}
 \sum_{\substack{\lambda\vdash N\\
        \|\lambda/N-s\|_1>\varepsilon}}
 \tr\bigl(\tau^{\otimes N}\Pi_\lambda^{(N)}\bigr)
 \longrightarrow0.

\end{equation}
Equivalently, the normalized row-length vector $\lambda/N$ converges in
probability to the spectrum $s$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:41:11.636151+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cec1854404378bd94069776b446522d1405a05fad4e6d03975e61d3f62a83ca1","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma terms_in_signature_third of arXiv:2411.13707, 'High-degree cubature on Wiener space \\ through unshuffle expansions'] \[
\sum_{1\leq i\leq d}(\epsilon_0,\epsilon_0,\epsilon_i^2)=\sum_{1\leq i\leq d}((\xi_{0},\xi_{0ii})+\frac{2}{3}(\xi_{0i},\xi_{0i})+(\xi_{0},\xi_{0},\xi_{i},\xi_{i})\Big ) .
\]
\begin{proof}
Representation (equation eq:expansion of the paper) combined with (cor:parity of the paper) gives,
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
3\sum_{1\leq i\leq d}(\epsilon_0,\epsilon_0,\epsilon_i^2)&=\mathrm{e}^{\star 2}\Big (3\sum_{1\leq i\leq d}(\epsilon_0,\epsilon_0,\epsilon_i^2)\Big )+\mathrm{e}^{\star 4}\Big (3\sum_{1\leq i\leq d}(\epsilon_0,\epsilon_0,\epsilon_i^2)\Big )\\
&=\mathrm{e}^{\star 2}\Big (\sum_{1\leq i\leq d}(\epsilon_0^2\epsilon_i^2+\epsilon_0\epsilon_i^2\epsilon_0+\epsilon_i^2\epsilon_0^2)\Big )+\mathrm{e}^{\star 4}\Big (\sum_{1\leq i\leq d}(\epsilon_0^2\epsilon_i^2+\epsilon_0\epsilon_i^2\epsilon_0+\epsilon_i^2\epsilon_0^2)\Big ) .
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
The first term simplifies by substituting \(\xi_{0i}=-\xi_{i0}\), and \(\xi_{0ii}=\xi_{ii0}\) (reversal property),
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\mathrm{e}^{\star 2}\Big (\sum_{1\leq i\leq d}(\epsilon_0^2\epsilon_i^2+\epsilon_0\epsilon_i^2\epsilon_0+\epsilon_i^2\epsilon_0^2)\Big )=\sum_{1\leq i\leq d}&\Big (3(\xi_{0},\xi_{0ii})+3(\xi_{0},\xi_{ii0})+2(\xi_{0i},\xi_{0i})\\
&+2(\xi_{0i},\xi_{i0})+2(\xi_{i0},\xi_{i0})\Big )\\
=\sum_{1\leq i\leq d}&\Big (6(\xi_{0},\xi_{0ii})+2(\xi_{0i},\xi_{0i})\Big ) .
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
The second term is evaluated as,
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{e}^{\star 4}\Big (\sum_{1\leq i\leq d}(\epsilon_0^2\epsilon_i^2+\epsilon_0\epsilon_i^2\epsilon_0+\epsilon_i^2\epsilon_0^2)\Big )=\sum_{1\leq i\leq d}3(\xi_{0},\xi_{0},\xi_{i},\xi_{i}).
\end{equation}
Substituting (equation eq:k2_0_0_ii of the paper) and (equation eq:k4_0_0_ii of the paper) into (equation eq:0_0_ii of the paper) we obtain the required result.
\end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

