{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:00:22.779688+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cf7aac9abb8b90f4df66e3325bde5f09ece12152351a0c9d080d9ab399ff2682","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn Ornstein-Uhlenbeck Process of arXiv:2411.01633, 'On the Limit of the Tridiagonal Model \\for $\beta$-Dyson Brownian Motion'] The normalized stationary scalar Ornstein--Uhlenbeck process $\mathrm{OU}(c)$, is the unique solution to
    \begin{equation}
    \begin{split}
        &dx(t)=-m x(t)\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}{t} +\sqrt{2m} \mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}{W(t)} \\
        &x(0) \sim N(0,1)
    \end{split}
\end{equation}
for a standard one dimensional Brownian motion $W(t)$. Or equivalently, $\mathrm{OU}(m)$ is a stationary Gaussian process for all time with covariances given by $\mathbb{E}[x(t)x(s)]=e^{-m|t-s|}$. Furthermore if $m=(m_1,...,m_n)$ denote by $\mathbf{OU}_n(m)$ the vector valued Ornstein--Uhlenbeck process that has independent $\mathrm{OU}(m_i)$ entries. If $c$ is scalar, the notation $\mathbf{OU}_n(m)$ will be short for $\mathbf{OU}_n((m,...,m))$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:18:19.200974+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cf7f9b7e8ac143c5767025bf7e609259ca046cc977e1388ba4b36bda3dd9f7d0","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 16 of arXiv:2608.28964, '2608.28964'] For every $T>0$, there exists a constant $C>0$ such that for any $u_0\in \mathcal H^1$ and $f\in L^2(0,T;H^{1}(D;\R^2))$, the solution $u$ of \eqref{eq NS det} satisfies
	\begin{align}
	\|u\|_{L^2(0,T;\mathcal H^2)}&\leq C\left(\|u_0\|_{\mathcal H^1}+\|f\|_{L^2(0,T;H^{1}(D))}\right).\\
	\|u\|_{L^\infty((T/2,T)\times D)}&\leq C\left(\|u_0\|_{\mathcal H^1}+\|f\|_{L^2(0,T;H^{1}(D))}\right),\\
	\|\partial_tu\|_{L^2(T/2,T;L^4(D))}&\leq C\left(1+\|u_0\|_{\mathcal H^1}^2+\|f\|_{L^2(0,T;H^{1}(D))}^2\right).
	\end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:02:11.269572+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cf8ee3a86d5ba0de50dc649488d2b4a78b1cc876725fda91da6366c95b00f69d","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:clt-rw2mc of arXiv:2509.10225, 'Phase Transitions for Elephant Random Walks \\ with Two memory Channels'] The fluctuations of the walk around the almost sure limit exhibit the following central limit behavior based on the value of $p$. 
\begin{enumerate}
\item[a)] For $p \in \left(0, \frac{11}{16}\right)\cup\left(\frac{113+\sqrt{97}}{128}, 1\right)$,
\begin{align}
    \sqrt{n}\left(\frac{{S}_n}{{n}} - \Lambda_p\right) \stackrel{d}{\to} N\left(0,\Sigma^{(1)}_p\right),
\end{align}
where
\begin{align}
   \Sigma^{(1)}_p = &{\begin{cases}{\frac{2}{11-16p}}, &{p \in \left(0, \frac{11}{16}\right)}, \\[.1cm] \Sigma^{(1)}_{\alpha, \beta, p}, &{p \in \left(\frac{113+\sqrt{97}}{128}, 1\right)},\end{cases}}
\end{align}
with $\alpha, \beta$ given by (equation eq:al-be of the paper) and the random variable $\Sigma^{(1)}_{\alpha, \beta, p}$ given by (equation eq:sig1 of the paper).
\item[b)] For $p \in \left\{\frac{11}{16}, \frac{113+\sqrt{97}}{128}\right\}$,
\begin{align}
    \sqrt{\frac{n}{\log n}}\left(\frac{{S}_n}{{n}} - \Lambda_p\right) \stackrel{d}{\to} N\left(0,\Sigma^{(2)}_p\right),
\end{align}
where
\begin{align}
   \Sigma^{(2)}_p = &{\begin{cases}{\frac{2}{3}}, &{p = \frac{11}{16}}, \\[.1cm] \Sigma^{(2)}_{\alpha, \beta, p}, &{p = \frac{113+\sqrt{97}}{128}}.\end{cases}}
\end{align}
with $\alpha, \beta$ given by (equation eq:al-be of the paper) and the random variable $\Sigma^{(2)}_{\alpha, \beta, p}$ given by (equation eq:sig2 of the paper).
\item[c)] For $p \in \left(\frac{11}{16}, \frac{7}{8}\right)\cup\left(\frac{7}{8}, \frac{113+\sqrt{97}}{128}\right)$,
\begin{align}
    n^{y_p}\left(\frac{{S}_n}{{n}} - \Lambda_p\right) \stackrel{a.s.}{\to} L_{p},
\end{align}
where
${{L_p}}$ is a finite random variable and 
\begin{align*}
    y_p = \begin{cases}\hspace{1.7cm}{\frac{7-8p}{3}}\hspace{2.05cm},
    &{p \in \left(\frac{11}{16}, \frac{7}{8}\right)}, \smallskip \\{\frac{5p-4-\sqrt{-64p^3+161p^2-134p+37}}{2p-1}}, &{p \in \left(\frac{7}{8}, \frac{113+\sqrt{97}}{128}\right)}.\end{cases}
\end{align*}
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:52:51.763065+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cf93c5dbb2e54f35e713b5fb20bb82094953bae2663b0c13eae98af33f03fdca","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition Def:ms-dissipative-schurz of arXiv:2604.28068, 'Mean-square Stability and Bifurcations for Dissipative SDEs'] Let $\left\{X(t):t\in[0,\infty)] \subseteq D \subseteq \mathbb{R}^d \right\}$ be a solution of the SDE (equation eq:main_d of the paper) corresponding to initial data $X(0)\in D$ which is measurable with respect to $\mathcal{F}_0$. The SDE is called 
    \begin{enumerate}
    \item mean-square dissipative if  there exist $r,R>0$ such that     $$\limsup_{t\to\infty}\mathbb{E}\left[\|X(t)\|^2\right]<R^2$$
    whenever $\mathbb{E}\left[\|X(0)\|^2\right]<r^2$;

    \item uniformly mean-square dissipative if  there exist $r,R>0$ such that $$\lim_{t\to\infty}\expect{\sup_{0\leq s \leq t}\|X(s)\|^2} <R^2$$
    whenever $\mathbb{E}\left[\|X(0)\|^2\right] < r^2$.
    \end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:48:47.285665+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cf9b4d14b32b875e8e602a05497339f8d78af0e6f5b7f3b71f69382aff19afbd","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:SUSY1eps of arXiv:2604.20660, 'The Legendre structure of the TAP complexity\\ for the Ising spin glass'] Let $\zeta\in\mathrm{Prefix}_2(u,q)$ for some $u,q\in(0,1)$.
		We define $\mathrm{SUSY}_1(\zeta,\varepsilon)$ as the set of $\mathbf{m}\in[-1+\delta_\varepsilon,1-\delta_\varepsilon]^N$ such that
		\begin{equation*}
			\left|\frac{1}{N}\|\mathbf{m}\|^2-q\right|\leq\varepsilon,
			\qquad
			\left|\frac{1}{N}\langle\partial_m h_\zeta(q,\mathbf{m}),\mathbf{m}\rangle
			-\xi'(q)\int_0^1\zeta([0,t])\,\mathrm{d} t\right|\leq \varepsilon,
		\end{equation*}
		and
		\begin{equation*}
			\mathrm{dist}\!\bigl(\zeta_\mathbf{m},\,\zeta|_{(q,1]}+\zeta([0,q])\,\delta_q\bigr)\leq\varepsilon,
		\end{equation*}
		where we recall that $\mathrm{dist}$ is as in (equation def:dist of the paper).","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:38:25.224212+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cf9e9a9a83a417da92c23a4b82dc92aa730bbc4376003b7a7d4d95b429ce3c56","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition AS of arXiv:2609.00324, 'Quantitative Stability for Fractional Yamabe minimizers'] Let $(M,[\hat{h}])$ be the conformal infinity of an asymptotically hyperbolic manifold $(X^{n+1},g^+)$, and $p\geq 3$. Let $u_0$ be a non-integrable minimizer of the fractional Yamabe energy and suppose that it satisfies the $\text{AS}_p$ condition. Then, there exists a sequence $(u_k)_{k\in \mathbb{N}} \subset H^\gamma (M)$ with $u_k \to u_0$ in $H^\gamma (M)$ such that 
\begin{align*}
    \lim_{k\to \infty}\frac{I_\gamma [u_k,\hat{h}] -\Lambda_\gamma (M,[\hat{h}])}{\|u_k - u_0\|_{H^\gamma (M)}^{p-\alpha} } = 0 \quad \forall \alpha>0.
\end{align*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:09:49.861751+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cfa97543e2ef279fa8e4cd5e795ffbada34b1c464209acf0caf557573d89e91a","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 16 of arXiv:2609.03084, 'Convergence rates to traveling waves for an FKPP model with nonlinear advection'] For $x \geq 2t$, let
        \begin{equation}
            \phi(t, x) = \frac{K(x-2t)}{t^{3/2}}e^{-(x-2t)}e^{\frac{-(x-2t)^2}{4t}},
        \end{equation}
        where $K$ is a positive constant.
        Then there exists a $T > 0$ such that
        \begin{equation}
            \bar \phi(t, x) = \left(1- \frac{1}{t^{1/4}}\right) \phi(t, x) 
        \end{equation}
        is a nonnegative supersolution of \eqref{main_eq} for $t > T$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:51:52.062695+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cfaa37f7a792550cc4cce06911affbf4b38a4ac82b381c50e36987b7e1c933fe","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition D:main-nondegenerate-arc of arXiv:2607.15966, 'Canonical Mandelbrot Cascades on Curves Are Rajchman'] A map \(\gamma:[0,1]\to\mathbb{R}^2\) is a fixed nondegenerate \(C^2\) embedded arc if it is a \(C^2\) embedding and
