{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:32:35.569726+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d2278c7f38071c26fb9682ccf4466bb311db44fbc1df4a5db6450ba36693c8fd","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem.velocity-decomp of arXiv:2605.26082, 'Quantitative Einstein relation for reversible diffusions in a random environment'] Let $\alpha\in(0,\delta/2)$, let $\zeta\in(0,\zeta_H]$, let $\theta\in(0,1/4)$, and let $\varepsilon>0$ satisfy $\varepsilon(1-\alpha)<\alpha$.
				There exist deterministic constants $C,C'<\infty$ and $c>0$, a deterministic exponent $\sigma\in(0,1)$, and a random scale $\lambda_*^H\colon\Omega\to(0,1]$ such that
				\begin{equation}
						\mathbb{Q}\bigl(\lambda_*^H\le\lambda\bigr)\le C'\exp\bigl(-c (\log(1/\lambda))^\sigma\bigr)\qquad\text{for every }\lambda\in(0,1]\, .
					\end{equation}
					The constants $C,C',c,\sigma$ depend only on the displayed parameters and on $(d,\Lambda,r_0,\beta_h)$. For $\mathbb{Q}$-a.e.\ $\omega$ and every $0<\lambda\le\lambda_*^H(\omega)$,
