{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:21:10.900835+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d36d57fd807f46091074cb836a91de96297a9bd09c11d5618afb5fb39dcb7951","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 39 (Discrete Gaussian for extended filling fractions) of arXiv:2601.16377, '2601.16377'] Suppose that Assumptions~\ref{Assumptions_Theta}, \ref{Assumptions_basic}, \ref{Assumptions_offcrit}, \ref{Assumptions_analyticity} hold and that the equilibrium measure has at least one saturation. There exists $\eps > 0$ and random $K$-tuples of integers $\boldsymbol{Q}^{\circ},\boldsymbol{N}^{\circ}$ and $K$-tuple of real numbers $\hat{\boldsymbol{a}}^{\circ},\hat{\boldsymbol{b}}^{\circ}$ such that the following properties hold with probability exponentially close to $1$ as $\N \rightarrow \infty$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item $\boldsymbol{N}^{\circ},\hat{\boldsymbol{a}}^{\circ}$, $\hat{\boldsymbol{b}}^{\circ}$ are deterministic affine functions of $\boldsymbol{Q}^{\circ}$, with coefficients of the linear part depending only on the diagonal of $\boldsymbol{\Theta}$;
\item the segment $[\hat{a}_k^{\circ},\hat{b}_k^{\circ}]$ contains the $k$-th band and is contained in its $\eps$-neighborhood;
\item the segment $[\N\hat{a}_k^{\circ},\N\hat{b}_k^{\circ}]$ contains exactly $N_k^{\circ}$ particles, the leftmost and rightmost ones being at integer distance of its endpoints.
\end{enumerate}
Besides, the distribution of $\boldsymbol{Q}^{\circ}$ is asymptotically equal to a discrete Gaussian distribution $\textnormal{\textsf{\textbf{Gau\ss{}}}}_{\amsmathbb{Z}}$ as $\N \rightarrow \infty$, whose quadratic form $\boldsymbol{B}$ and centering vector $\boldsymbol{u}$ depend on the parameters of the ensemble. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:16:53.605616+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d370316f70bdf0da0dd2e4335ad395b4cdbb2cf889ebd59f514e76c5fbd39491","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:SSL of arXiv:2412.05142, 'Strong convergence of the Euler scheme for singular kinetic SDEs driven by $\alpha$-stable'] [BDG-Levy] 
Let $(S,T)\in[0,1]^2$. Let $p\in[2,\infty)$.   Let
$(A_{s,t})_{(s,t)\in \Delta_{[S,T]}}$ be a family of ${\mathbb R}^d$-valued random variables in $L_p(\Omega)$
such that $A_{s,t}$ is $\mathcal{F}_t$-measurable for all $(s,t)\in \Delta_{[S,T]}$.   Let ${\mathcal G}\subset{\mathcal F}_S$ be an $\sigma$-algebra. Suppose that there exist constants $\epsilon_1,\epsilon_2>0$ and constants $K_1, K_2\in[0,\infty)$,  such that the following hold:
\begin{enumerate}[(1)]
   \item $\|A_{s,t}\|_{L_p(\Omega)|{\mathcal G}}\leqslant K_1|t-s|^{\frac{1}{2}+\epsilon_1}$, $(s,t)\in\Delta_{[S,T]}$,
   \item $\|{\mathbb E}^{s-(t-s)}\delta A_{s,u,t}\|_{L_p(\Omega)|{\mathcal G}}\leqslant K_2|t-s|^{1+\epsilon_2}$, $(s,t)\in\Delta_{[S,T]}$ and $u=\frac{s+t}{2}$,
   \item there exists a  process $\mathcal{A}:[S,T]\mapsto L^p(\Omega;{\mathbb R}^d)$  such that  for any $(s,t)\in [S,T]_\leqslant^2$ one has
   \begin{align}

       {\mathcal A}_t-{\mathcal A}_s=\lim_{m\rightarrow\infty}\sum_{i=1}^{m-1}A_{s+i\frac{t-s}{m},s+(i+1)\frac{t-s}{m}} \quad\text{ in probability}.
   \end{align}
\end{enumerate}
 Then there exists constants $\hat K_1,\hat K_2>0$ which depend only on $\epsilon_1,\epsilon_2,\epsilon_3, d,p$ so that for any $(s,t)\in[S,T]_{\leqslant}^2$,
  \begin{align}

       \|{\mathcal A}_{t}-{\mathcal A}_{s}\|_{L_p(\Omega)|{\mathcal G}}\leqslant K_1\hat K_1|t-s|^{\frac{1}{2}+\epsilon_1}+K_2\hat K_2|t-s|^{1+\epsilon_2}.
   \end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:35:10.789048+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d3720543bddfc048987e8d6d7c8e533befd0bfc54914a6ed3d4849483019060c","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 30 of arXiv:2604.00630, 'The contact process on a bipartite spatial network'] Let~$\pi=(\pi_0,\ldots,\pi_\ell)$ be a combinatorial path of length~$\ell$ with~$k$ distinct vertices; assume that~$\pi_\ell \notin\{\pi_0,\ldots,\pi_{\ell-1}\}$. Let~$T$ be the discovery tree of~$\pi$. Let~$\mathcal Q(\pi)$ be the probability that there exists an infection path in~$\mathcal G$ started at the root and having an ordered trace~$\Gamma$ satisfying the following properties:
\begin{itemize}
\item the combinatorial path associated to~$\Gamma$ is~$\pi$;
\item all vertices of~$\Gamma$ are blue.
\end{itemize}
Then,
	\[
		\mathcal Q(\pi) \le 
			(2\lambda^{a \wedge 1})^{(\ell-2k)\vee 0} \cdot F(T,\pi_0,\pi_\ell). 
	\] (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:37:20.365943+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d37723ae6e9f92d3ed279817507cf3c9c7ac7f7595bf15b7a3a23232ed9cb0b2","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm -1 of arXiv:2411.13403, 'Path weighting method for spot sensitivities and its variance reduction'] If $F(S)$ has generalised derivatives with at most
polynomial growth a.e., then the random variables 
\begin{equation}
\xi=\frac{1}{\sqrt{\Delta t_{1}}}\Big(F(S)\Theta^{(1)}(s,Z_{1},\sqrt{\Delta t_{1}}Z_{1})-F(\hat{S})\Theta^{(1)}(s,Z_{1},\sqrt{\Delta t_{1}}\hat{Z}_{1})\Big),
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
\eta_{1}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{\Delta t_{1}}}\Big(F(S)\Lambda^{(1)}(s,Z_{1},\sqrt{\Delta t_{1}}Z_{1})-F(\hat{S})\Lambda^{(1)}(s,Z_{1},\sqrt{\Delta t_{1}}\hat{Z}_{1})\Big),
\end{equation}
have variance of order
\begin{align}
\mathrm{Var}(\xi) & =\mathrm{O}(1),\;(\Delta t_{1}\to0),\\
\mathrm{Var}(\eta_{1}) & =\mathrm{O}(1),\;(\Delta t_{1}\to0).
\end{align}
If, in addition, $F(S)$ has second order generalised derivatives
with at most polynomial growth a.e., then the random variable
\begin{equation}
\eta_{2}=\frac{1}{\Delta t_{1}}\Big(F(S)\Lambda^{(2)}(s,Z_{1},\sqrt{\Delta t_{1}}Z_{1})-2F(\bar{S})\Lambda^{(2)}(s,Z_{1},\sqrt{\Delta t_{1}}\bar{Z}_{1})+F(\hat{S})\Lambda^{(2)}(s,Z_{1},\sqrt{\Delta t_{1}}\hat{Z}_{1})\Big)
\end{equation}
has variance of order
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{Var}(\eta_{1})=\mathrm{O}(1),\;(\Delta t_{1}\to0),
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:49:05.498768+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d37f121dcc17a2ddcab8c57da7bac64068edec1b5452a3b1a483b443805b419c","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm bigthm of arXiv:2601.19145, 'Stochastic Persistence in Infinite Dimensions'] Let $r$ be large enough. Under (sass-A of the paper)-(SPDE-G-sass of the paper), for every $R \geq 0$, $\theta \in \Theta$, and initial condition $\tilde{x}_0 \in C(\overline D)_+$ there is a unique mild solution in $L^r$ (see (def:mild of the paper)), denoted by $\tilde{x}_t = (\tilde{u}_t, \tilde{z}_t)$, to
    \begin{equation}
        \tilde{x}_t =S(t) \tilde{x}_0 + (S * \tilde F(R,\tilde{x},\theta))_t + (S \diamond \tilde G(R, \tilde{x},\theta))_t
   \end{equation}
    Furthermore, the following properties hold:
    \begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
        \item  $\tilde{x}_t \geq 0$.
        \item  $\tilde{u}_t = 0 \iff \tilde{u}_0 = 0$.
        \item  $t \mapsto \tilde{x}_t$ is almost surely a continuous map from $[0,\infty)$ to $B$.
        \item  If $(R_n,\tilde{x}_0^{(n)},\theta_n) \to (R,\tilde{x}_0,\theta)$ in $[0,\infty) \times C(\overline D)_+ \times \Theta$, then $\tilde{x}^{(n)}$, the solutions to (equation eq:mild-solution of the paper) with initial condition $\tilde{x}_0^{(n)}$ and $R = R_n$, $\theta = \theta_n$, converge to $\tilde{x}$ in $L^p(\Omega;C([0,T];B))$ for all $p,T > 0$.
        \item  If $\tilde{u}_0 \neq 0$ then $(r_t,v_t) \coloneqq (R\|\tilde{u}_t\|_{L^1}, \tilde{u}_t/\|\tilde{u}_t\|_{L^1})$ satisfies
        \begin{equation}
            \begin{aligned}
            dr &= r[\ip{v}{\tilde e_u} + \ip{\tilde F_u(r,v,\tilde{z})}{1_u}]dt + r\ip{\tilde G_u(r,v,\tilde{z})^*1_u}{dW_t} \\
            dv &= A_uvdt + v[-\ip{v}{\tilde e_u} - \ip{\tilde F_u(r,v,\tilde{z})}{1_u} + \|\tilde G_u(r,v,\tilde{z})^*1_u\|_\mathcal{U}^2]dt \\
            &+ \tilde F_u(r,v,\tilde{z})dt - \tilde G_u(r,v,\tilde{z})\tilde G_u(r,v,\tilde{z})^*1_udt \\
            &+ \tilde G_u(r,v,\tilde{z})dW_t- \ip{\tilde G_u(r,v,\tilde{z})^*1_u}{dW_t}v \,,
        \end{aligned}
    \end{equation}
    where the equation for $v$ holds in the mild sense of (def:mild of the paper). We omitted the dependence on $\theta$ for brevity.

    \item  Let $\bar{x}_t \coloneqq (R\tilde{u}_t, \tilde{z}_t) = (r_tv_t,\tilde{z}_t)$, where $(r_t, v_t)$ was defined in (v). Then
    \begin{equation}
        d\|\bar x_t\|_{\beta,2}^2 = [-2\|\bar x_t\|_{\beta+1/2,2}^2 +2\ip{\bar x_t}{F(\bar x_t)}_\beta + \|G(\bar x_t)\|_{\beta,2}^2]dt + 2\ip{\bar x_t}{G(\bar x_t)dW_t}_\beta \,,
    \end{equation}
    where $\beta$ is as in (SPDE-G-lem of the paper). 
    \end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:17:42.313916+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d37fc60f77755dea72988016e87bfae2f0f87f74de5fb551eb9c9d783e3c2f6c","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 41 of arXiv:2608.05931, 'Effective Lagrangian regularity and the uniqueness threshold for random H\"older velocity '] Suppose $U=\sum_{j=0}^\infty u^j$ satisfies Assumption~\ref{asmp:main for autonomous}. Additionally, suppose that for some $\alpha\in(0,1)$ and $p>1$, we have a constant $K\geq 1$ such that for $n\in\{0,1,2\}$ and all $j\in\N$,
\begin{equation}
\|\nabla^n u^j\|_{L^p_\omega \mathcal{C}^0_{x}}\leq K 2^{(n-\alpha)j}.
\end{equation}
    Then for all $\eps>\frac{d}{p}$ and $A\geq 1$ there exist vector fields $\{u^{j,A}\}_{j \geq 0}$ (on possibly a larger probability space, though coupled to the original $u^j$) also satisfying Assumption~\ref{asmp:main for autonomous} such that for all $j\geq 0$, $n\in\{0,1,2\}$,
\[\|\nabla^n u^{j,A}\|_{\mathcal{C}^0_{x}} \leq A K 2^{(n-\alpha+\eps)j},
\]
and, letting $U^A:=\sum_{j=0}^\infty u^{j,A}$, there exists $C(d,p,\eps)>0$ such that
\[\P(U^A\neq U)\leq C A^{1-p}.\] (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:02:28.585342+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d3838f7c32b24b90b9d0c8e14b9ac7ddbebc324971593a3eab0cf860173deb71","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 14 of arXiv:2609.01333, '2609.01333'] Let $\gamma\in (0,\gamma_*)$ and $\lambda_{*}=\lambda_*(\gamma)$ be as in Theorem \ref{thm:Lambda-star}.  For every $f\in Y^\perp$ there exists a unique $w\in X^\perp$ such that
