{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:09:34.480659+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d7c2a33653b0f3147b5eda504eb6a875b68de5c267fb9351e1bf0316de361779","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn Capacity in $\mathbb{H}$ of arXiv:2505.14762, 'Multiple radial SLE($\kappa$) and quantum Calogero-Sutherland system'] For any compact subset $K \subset \overline{\mathbb{H}}$ such that $\mathbb{H} \backslash K$ is a simply connected domain. The half-plane capacity of a hull $K$ is the quantity
$$
\operatorname{hcap}(K):=\lim _{z \rightarrow \infty} z\left[g_K(z)-z\right] \text {, }
$$
where $g_K: \mathbb{H} \backslash K \rightarrow \mathbb{H}$ is the unique conformal map satisfying the hydrodynamic normalization $g(z)=z+O\left(\frac{1}{z}\right)$ as $z \rightarrow \infty$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:57:44.958859+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d7c34d1115a6a329c28e08fdeaf814697a4c9edcc3758f17d9dae008a7efc986","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 16 ($L^p$ centered operator) of arXiv:2606.26065, 'Localized Centered Second-Chaos Operators'] Fix $p\ge2$ and $\eps>0$.  If
\begin{equation}
 \mathfrak S_H^{(\eps)}(\beta,\sigma)
 +\mathfrak S_B^{(\eps)}(\zeta,\sigma)<\infty,
\end{equation}
then $\cB^\circ_\Lambda$ is a well-defined random bounded operator
\[
 \cB^\circ_\Lambda:\Xspace_T^\sigma\to\Yspace_T^{\beta,\zeta},
\]
uniformly in $\Lambda$, and
\begin{equation}
 \left\|\norm{\cB^\circ_\Lambda}_{\Xspace_T^\sigma\to\Yspace_T^{\beta,\zeta}}
 \right\|_{L^p(\Omega)}
 \lesssim_{p,T,\eps}
 \mathfrak S_H^{(\eps)}(\beta,\sigma)
 +\mathfrak S_B^{(\eps)}(\zeta,\sigma).
\end{equation}
Moreover, as $\Lambda\to\infty$,
\begin{equation}
 \cB^\circ_\Lambda\to\cB^\circ
 \quad\text{in }L^p\bigl(\Omega;\cL(\Xspace_T^\sigma,\Yspace_T^{\beta,\zeta})\bigr).
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:57:49.855062+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d7c5c1fbc655e8fa89d4d3dbc79d7448dad4c6976a53c365ca15820b15166d28","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem th:main of arXiv:2410.23974, 'Polynomial rate of relaxation for the Glauber dynamics of infinite-volume critical Ising m'] Fix any $d\geq2$, let $P_{t}$ denote the infinite-volume Markov semigroup for the Ising Glauber dynamics at critical temperature $\beta_{c}$. Under Assumptions (equation polynomial of the paper) and (equation arm of the paper), there exists a constant $C=C(\beta_{c},\eta,\delta,d,c_{M})$ such that the polynomial bound
    \begin{equation}
        \langle\sigma_{0},P_{t}\sigma_{0}\rangle_{\mathbb{Z}^{d}}\leq\frac{C}{t^{\alpha}},\quad \forall t\geq0
    \end{equation}
    holds with the exponent $\alpha$ given by 
    \begin{equation}
        \alpha=\frac{2\delta\wedge1}{2\eta}.
    \end{equation}
    In the above, $c_{M}>0$ is an upper bound for the flipping rates of the dynamics, see (equation eq:bound-rates of the paper). $\langle\cdot\rangle_{\Z^{d}}$ denotes the unique infinite volume Ising expectation (equation eq:infinite-expectation of the paper) on $\Z^{d}$ at critical temperature $\beta_{c}$. $\langle f,g\rangle_{\Z^{d}}:=\langle fg\rangle_{\Z^{d}}$ is the $L^{2}$ inner product for any mean zero functions $f$ and $g$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:43:53.644648+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d7cd38af6762a15db3f7e2d64546482f8c435965481f5d7a34c9c68d418520c6","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:disc_loc_times of arXiv:2512.17747, 'On exponentially height-penalized random trees'] For any path $(p(t);0\leq t\leq n)$ of length $n$, define the local time at a particular level and the maximum local time as follows. For an integer $k\in [-n,n]$,
\begin{align*}
    L_{k,n}(p):=|\{t:p(t)=k\}|;\;\;L_n(p):=\max_{-n \leq k \leq n}L_{k,n}(p).
\end{align*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:51:26.726554+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d7ce7f4806348caa00263b030fad3daf4855b77cd09b17d6264b5b9fb8f15b3b","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop thm:polynomial of arXiv:2606.08325, 'Digit Mixing under Polynomial Maps'] Let \(P\in\mathbb R[x]\) have degree \(d\ge2\).  Then there is a constant \(c_P>0\), depending only on \(P\), with the following property.  If
			\begin{equation}
				\sum_{s\ge1}s \exp(-c_PR'_d(s))<\infty,
			\end{equation}
			then \(P(X)\) is almost surely absolutely normal. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:57:59.894226+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d7d2604ae44127792915adbf75f0f1e3523528b465f0747e6797bf9447c88a2f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:hoermplaw of arXiv:2607.09946, 'Statistics on Yau's conjecture: Variance asymptotics'] {Keeler}}{}]
For any $M$ compact Riemannian manifold, 
\begin{equation} 
E^M_{[0,\ell]}(x,y) = \ell^n\left( E_{[0,1]}^{n}(\ell\dist xy)+R_{[0,\ell]}^M(x,y)\right),
\end{equation}
with remainder of order $R_{[0,\ell]}^M(x,y)=\mathrm{O}(\ell^{-1})$. Precisely, the estimate continues to hold for the derivatives in the following sense: for $\rho(M)$ small enough, for any $k\in \mathbb{N}$,
\begin{equation}
\sup_{\mathrm{dist}(x,y)\le \rho(M)}\max_{|a|,|b|\le k} \frac{\| \nabla^a \nabla^b R_{[0,\ell]}^{M}(x,y)\|}{\ell^{|a|+|b|}}=\mathrm{O}(\ell^{-1}). 
\end{equation}
In particular, it follows that: 
\begin{equation} 
\sigma(\phi_{[0,\ell]}^M)^2=\ell^{n}\left( \frac{b_n}{(2\pi)^n}+\mathrm{O}(\ell^{-1})\right),\quad \lambda(\phi_{[0,\ell]}^M)^2=\ell^2\left(\frac{n}{n+2}+\mathrm{O}(\ell^{-1})\right).
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:28:49.114469+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d7da6c5e77013c235d646742ebc2f3ae4ba3e81a9af6e4b237e7ff854dac0e29","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop PRO26 of arXiv:2608.16170, '\bf On hitting time distributions of Markov processes with sub-Gaussian heat kernel bounds'] Assume that every semipolar subset of $E$ is polar. Then, for any $x\in E$ and $B\in \mathscr{B}^n(E)$,
