As a result, either $\rmh_{\bspi}^0(\phi)$ equals $-\infty$ and so does $\rmh_{\bspi}^\ann(\phi_t)$ for every $t$, or these quantities are all finite and
\[\rmh_{\bspi}^\ann(\phi_t) \to \rmh_{\bspi}^0(\phi) \qquad \hbox{as}\ t \downarrow 0.\]

The same conclusions hold if $\rmh^\ann_{\bspi}$ and $\rmh_{\bspi}^0$ are replaced with $\ul{\rmh}^\ann_{\bspi}$ and $\ul{\rmh}_{\bspi}^0$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:58:38.071996+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d83fdd283103fd84e5cee3dca760bc429e91ab5c555c26d29954d2a16879965e","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition eq:unlabeled-delta-BL-p of arXiv:2606.21556, 'Mean field limit of non-exchangeable interacting diffusions on co-evolutionary networks'] For any two fibered probability measures \(\mu,\bar{\mu} \in \mathcal{P}_{\nu}(\mathcal{X}\times [0,1])\), we define
	the \emph{unlabeled} version of the distance \(d_{\text{BL},p}\) as 
	\begin{equation} 
		\delta_{\text{BL},p}(\mu,\bar{\mu}):= \inf_{\varphi \in S_{[0,1]}} d_{\text{BL},p}(\mu,\bar{\mu}^{\varphi}),
	\end{equation}
	where \(S_{[0,1]}\) is the set of bijective measure-preserving maps from \([0,1]\) to itself, and \(\bar{\mu}^{\varphi}\)
	is the fibered probability measure rearranged by \(\varphi\), that is, \(\bar{\mu}^{\varphi,\xi}=\bar{\mu}^{\varphi(\xi)}\).","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:48:47.287901+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d8419e48118af6a2cef446e961146805bc749d1d19f19506ab099a3394c53a86","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma Hopf Lax of arXiv:2604.20660, 'The Legendre structure of the TAP complexity\\ for the Ising spin glass'] Let $\mu$ be a probability measure on $\mathbb{R}$ with support bounded from below and let $t>0$.
		\begin{enumerate}
			\item Let $x\in\mathbb{R}$ and set $\omega := \omega_{\mu,t}(x)\in\mathbb{C}^+\cup\mathbb{R}$. Define the characteristic path $(z_s)_{s\in [0,t]}$ by
			\begin{equation}
				z_s = \omega + s\, G_\mu(\omega).
			\end{equation}
			If $x\leq \ell(\mu\boxplus\sigma_t)$, then for every $s\in [0,t]$,
			\begin{equation}
				x \leq z_s \leq \ell(\mu\boxplus\sigma_s).
			\end{equation}
			If instead $x$ lies in the interior of $\mathrm{supp}(\mu\boxplus\sigma_t)$, then for every $s\in [0,t)$,
			\begin{equation*}
				\Im\bigl(\omega + s\,G_\mu(\omega)\bigr) > 0.
			\end{equation*}

			\item For every $x\leq \ell(\mu\boxplus\sigma_t)$,
			\begin{multline}
				\int \log|\lambda-x|\,\mathrm{d} (\mu\boxplus \sigma_t)(\lambda) = \int \log |\lambda-\omega_{\mu,t}(x)|\,\mathrm{d} \mu(\lambda) + \frac{\Re\bigl(\omega_{\mu,t}(x)-x\bigr)^2 - \Im\bigl(\omega_{\mu,t}(x)\bigr)^2}{2t}\\
				= \inf_{u\leq \ell(\mu)}\;\sup_{\substack{v\geq 0 \\ u+iv\in \overline{\Omega_{\mu,t}}}} \Bigl(\int \log |\lambda-(u+iv)|\,\mathrm{d} \mu(\lambda) + \frac{(x-u)^2-v^2}{2t}\Bigr).
			\end{multline}
			Moreover, the infimum in (equation eq:ch of the paper) is uniquely attained at $(u,v)=\bigl(\Re(\omega_{\mu,t}(x)),\,\Im(\omega_{\mu,t}(x))\bigr)$.
		\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:48:27.282100+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d8441a98570fd5b5982bad10fa28834e85d52e853327b6afd9cca4c9603ef134","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 3 of arXiv:2604.25336, 'Central limit theorem for slow-fast system under mixed fractional Brownian motion'] Under assumptions \textbf{H1}-\textbf{H3}. Then for any function \(h\in C^{2,2+\gamma}\) with \(\gamma\in(0,1]\) which is ``centering" that is \(\bar h = \int_{\mathbb{R}^{m}} h(x,y)\mu^x(dy)=0\), there exists a unique solution $u$ satisfying the following Poisson equation in \(\mathbb{R}^m\) which depends on the parameter \(x\), 
