\end{equation}
and $\hdd R^{\mathsf s}=H$ almost surely.  Again, for individual defects one may replace $\norm{H}_{1,\alpha}$ by $\norm{H}_{1,\alpha}+C_H(\omega)$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:27:25.782770+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d8b70b52b137c20014fde7e5bd973877be79f4db3a0070f75699e18e14eeb856","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:initialization-coupling of arXiv:2605.08052, 'Rapid phase ordering of Ising dynamics on $\mathbb Z^2$'] [Time-zero monotone coupling] Assign each vertex in $\mathbb Z^d$ at time $0$ two $\text{Unif}[0,1]$ random variables $(\mathcal U_{v,0}^\pi)_{v\in \mathbb Z^d}$ and $(\mathcal U^Q_{v,0})_{v\in \mathbb Z^d}$ independently. Start with an arbitrary enumeration $v_1,v_2,...$ of the vertices of $\mathbb Z^d$. 

    For any distribution $\pi_{\Lambda}^{\eta}$ where $\Lambda \subset \mathbb Z^d$ finite, and $\eta \in \{\pm 1\}^{\Lambda^c}$ is a boundary condition, draw a sample $\sigma \sim \pi_{\Lambda}^{\eta}$  and $Q\sim \bigotimes_{v\in \Lambda} \text{Rad}(p)$ as follows: iteratively, for $i\ge 1$, 
    \begin{itemize}
        \item let $\sigma_{v_i}$ be $+1$ if $$\mathcal U_{v_i,0}^\pi \le \pi_{\Lambda}^{\eta} ( \sigma_{v_i} =+1 \mid (\sigma_{v_j})_{j<i:v_j\in \Lambda})$$
        and $-1$ else. 
        \item let $Q_{v_i}$ be $+1$ if $\mathcal U_{v_i,0}^Q \le p$ and $-1$ else. . 
    \end{itemize}
    Once all vertices in $\Lambda$ have been processed, terminate.","status":"unverified"}

