In particular, one has $\boldsymbol{\tau}^{L,M}(\beta)\geq 0$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:25:05.622310+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d9afa386667f3cae99af319aa5ad2c113911ab855e79c51ee1b6273a7821c98e","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 3 of arXiv:2608.22112, '2608.22112'] \textbf{(Control of $\ell^2$ trajectory error via norm equivalence)}
Define $\tilde \f_H$ as in \cref{fH}, and let $\tilde \S_H$ be the corresponding learned stroboscopic map. Assume that the true and predicted trajectories remain uniformly bounded over the training horizon, i.e., there exists constants $M > 0$ and $\tilde{M} > 0$ such that
\begin{equation}
\|\S^h(\x[0])\|_2 \le M,
\qquad
\|\tilde \S_H^h(\x[0])\|_2 \le \tilde{M}
\end{equation}
for all $\x[0] \in \X_0$ and $h = 1,\dots,H$. Such bounds are natural when trajectories remain in a compact region of state space over the training horizon. Then there exists a constant $C = (M + \tilde{M}) > 0$, such that
\begin{equation}
\frac{1}{mH}
\sum_{h=1}^H
\sum_{\x[0] \in \X_0}
\|\tilde \S_H^h(\x[0]) - \S^h(\x[0])\|_2^2
\;\le\;
C \cdot \mathrm{Loss}_H(\tilde \f_H).
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:46:55.645583+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d9b6eb3e04d8fe9ee21337117e786e8593eba8d38ea5b5ace070cdb2f64da859","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 14 (KL telescoping bound) of arXiv:2605.24937, '2605.24937'] Let $(Y_n')_{n=0}^N$ and $(\widetilde Y_n)_{n=0}^{N-1}$ be defined
