{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:55:37.352979+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_da706ca09c374db49f0aee1650d4a3efb5e5c40862911c637e92f217ccddabab","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop 19 (Theorem 3.1 of \cite{T2}) of arXiv:2606.17472, '2606.17472'] For all $a\in \R$ and $t\ge 0$, it holds that
\begin{align}

  h(X_t-a)=h(x-a)+M_t^a+L_t^a,
\end{align}
where
\begin{align*}
  M_t^a:=\int_0^t \int_{\R\setminus \{0\}}\Big(h(X_{s-}-a+y)-h(X_{s-}-a)\Big)\tilde{N}(ds,dy)
\end{align*}
is an $L^2$-martingale. Here, $\tilde{N}$ denotes the compensated Poisson random measure. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:21:52.417240+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_da721a7eaf9a5c636cc95305cb6712a660ad2a04e8e1d14b04e9f52792945b22","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 102 of arXiv:2601.16377, '2601.16377'] Consider a variational datum satisfying Assumptions~\ref{Assumption_A}, \ref{Assumption_B} and \ref{Assumption_C} and having a single band per segment. For each $h \in [H]$, take a function $\Delta V_h$ which is holomorphic in a complex neighborhood of $[\hat{a}'_h,\hat{b}_h']$. Then consider the family of variational data parameterized by a real $\epsilon$ in a small neighborhood of $0$, differing from the original variational datum only by the potentials $V_h^\epsilon = V_h + \epsilon \Delta V_h$ for any $h \in [H]$. Then, denoting with an exponent ${}^{\epsilon}$ all the associated quantities, we have the second-order Taylor expansion
\begin{equation}

\I^{\epsilon}[\mu^\epsilon]\mathop{=}_{\epsilon \rightarrow 0} \I^0[\mu^0]+ \I'[\mu^0] \epsilon + \I''[\mu^0] \epsilon^2 +o(\epsilon^2),
 \end{equation}
The error $o(\epsilon^2)$ is uniform as long as $|\Delta V_h(z)|$ is uniformly bounded for any $h \in [H]$ and the constants in Assumptions~\ref{Assumption_A}, \ref{Assumption_B}, and \ref{Assumption_C} remain the same. The factors $\I'[\mu^0]$ and $\I''[\mu^0]$ do not depend on $\epsilon$ and are uniformly bounded under the same assumptions.

Furthermore, if we vary the original variational datum as in Proposition~\ref{Proposition_differentiability_filling_fraction} and Theorem~\ref{Theorem_differentiability_full}, then the factors $\I'[\mu^0]$ and $\I''[\mu^0]$ depend smoothly on the variation parameters and the remainder $o(\epsilon^2)$ remains uniform. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:57:49.873713+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_da7392a75a4ce322dd9077f13b299e1dd891071608145e5013505e2d9cd8ecd3","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:standard subadditive of arXiv:2411.00663, 'Ergodicity and Mixing of invariant capacities and applications'] Let $(\Omega,\mathcal{F},P,T)$ be a measure preserving system. Suppose that $\{f_n\}_{n\in\mathbb{N}}$ is a sequence of $\mathcal{F}$-measurable functions satisfying the following conditions:
	\begin{longlist}
		\item $\int |f_1|dP<\infty$;
		\medskip
		\item for each $k,n\in\mathbb{N}$, $f_{n+k}\le f_n+f_k\circ T^n$, $P$-a.s.
	\end{longlist} 
	Then there exists a $T$-invariant function $\phi:\Omega\to\mathbb{R}$ such that 
	\[\lim_{n\to\infty}\frac{1}{n}f_n=\phi\text{,   }P\text{-a.s. }\]
	Moreover, the function $\phi$ is given by
	\[\phi(\omega)=\inf_{n\in\mathbb{N}}\frac{1}{n}\mathbb{E}_P(f_n\mid \mathcal{I})(\omega)=\lim_{n\to\infty}\frac{1}{n}\mathbb{E}_P(f_n\mid \mathcal{I})(\omega)\text{ for each }\omega\in\Omega.\] (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:40:26.974975+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_da77f0ec2b9eac2b30b8bd4bb6c57143228f5c6453d46ac024b362e2466ac8da","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop sobolev of arXiv:2604.10602, 'Fractional Navier-Stokes Equations with Caputo Derivative Driven by Hermite Noise'] In dimension $d=2$, we have:
\begin{itemize}
    \item If $\nu > 1$, then $\dot{H}^\nu \hookrightarrow L^\infty(D)^2$ continuously.
    \item If $\nu > 0$, then $\dot{H}^{\nu+1} \hookrightarrow W^{1,\infty}(D)^2$ continuously.
    \item The embedding $\dot{H}^{\nu} \hookrightarrow \dot{H}^{\mu}$ is compact for $\nu > \mu$.
\end{itemize} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:59:24.054977+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_da78729f4a930e2e34bbde6028404337044c60c46c585145c68fbc5a3c5bbb7f","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:projected-background of arXiv:2608.30904, 'Entire monotone solutions of the anisotropic Allen--Cahn equation in dimension 5'] We have
\begin{equation}
\Pi_{\varepsilon,f}N_\varepsilon(U_{\varepsilon,f})
=
-\varepsilon\left[J_\Gamma f+\mathcal Q_\Gamma(f)\right]
+\varepsilon^3G_{\varepsilon,f},
\end{equation}
where
\begin{align}
\|G_{\varepsilon,f}\|_{C^{0,\alpha}_{\gamma+2}}
&\le C,
\\
\|G_{\varepsilon,f_1}-G_{\varepsilon,f_2}\|_{C^{0,\alpha}_{\gamma+2}}
&\le
C\|f_1-f_2\|_{C^{2,\alpha}_\gamma}.

\end{align}
Set
\begin{equation}
E^\perp_{\varepsilon,f}
:=
N_\varepsilon(U_{\varepsilon,f})
-
Z_{\varepsilon,f}\Pi_{\varepsilon,f}N_\varepsilon(U_{\varepsilon,f}).
\end{equation}
In the unclipped region,
\begin{equation}
\left|
E^\perp_{\varepsilon,f}
\bigl(\Theta_f(Y,\varepsilon s)\bigr)
\right|
\le
C\varepsilon^2(1+|s|)q'(s)\rho(Y)^{-2}
+
O_N(\varepsilon^N\rho(Y)^{-N}),
\end{equation}
and $E^\perp_{\varepsilon,f}$ is super-algebraically small outside this region.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-09-07T12:18:08.279009+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_da7898b5d45c53f7dad4798ef7383b01ef1cbb755a5cdaa8dc0f67b6c57ac3a7","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 6 of arXiv:2505.20799, 'A note on the improved sparse Hanson-Wright inequalities'] Assume that $\{\delta_{i}, 1\le i\le n \}$ and $\{\zeta_{i}, 1\le i\le n \}$ are two independent sequences of random variables, where $\delta_{i}\sim\textnormal{Bernoulli}(p)$ are independent and $\zeta_{i}$ are independent $\alpha$-sub-exponential random variables with mean $0$ and variance $1$.
    Consider a random vector $\xi=(\xi_{1}, \cdots, \xi_{n})$  with $\xi_{i}=\delta_{i}\cdot\zeta_{i}$. Let $A$ be an $m\times n$ fixed matrix. Then for $0<\alpha\le 2$ and $t\ge 0$,
    \begin{align}
        \mathbb{P}\Big\{ \big\vert \Vert A\xi\Vert_{2}-\sqrt{p}\Vert A\Vert_{F} \big\vert>L^{2}(\alpha)t \Big\}\le 2\exp\Big\{-c(\alpha)\min \big\{\big(\frac{t}{\Vert A\Vert_{l_2\to l_2}}\big)^{2}, \big(\frac{t}{\Vert A\Vert_{l_2\to l_2}} \big)^{\alpha} \big\}    \Big\},
    \end{align}
    where $L(\alpha)=\max_{i}\Vert \zeta_i\Vert_{\Psi_{\alpha}}$ and $c(\alpha)>0$ is a constant depending only on $\alpha$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:43:43.390823+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_da79012828b32ec77bc6de591a5a7ebfd4d56d57817faa0738ad5061bce097cd","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem l.sum of arXiv:2507.16056, 'Conditional GMC within the stochastic heat flow'] For $m\in\mathbb{N}$ and $i\in[\![m]\!]$, let $\mathcal{T}_{i}(t):L^2(\mathbb{R}^{d_i},\mathrm{d} x) \to L^2(\mathbb{R}^{d_{i-1}},\mathrm{d} x)$ be a $t$-parameterized operator with a nonnegative kernel.
Assume that, for $c', c_1, \ldots, c_{m}\in[0,\infty)$, $b_i\in(0,1)$,  and for a partition $A_1,A_2,A_3$ of $[\![m]\!]$, the following bounds hold for all $t>0$.
\begin{subequations}

\begin{align}

	&\text{When } i \in A_1,
	\quad
	\Normop{ \mathcal{T}_{i}(t) }
	\leq
	c_{i} \, e^{c't} \, t^{-b_i}\ .
\\

	&\text{When } i \in A_2,
	\quad
	\Normop{ \mathcal{T}_{i}(t) }
	\leq
	c_{i} \, e^{c't} \,t^{-1} \, \big|\log(\tfrac{1}{2}\wedge t)\big|^{-2} \ .
\\

	&\text{When } i \in A_3,
	\quad
	\Normop{ \mathcal{T}_{i}(t) }
	\leq
	c_{i} \, e^{c't} \,t^{-1}
	\text{ and }
	\NOrmop{\int_{0}^\infty \mathrm{d} t\ e^{-c' t} \mathcal{T}_{i}(t) } \leq c_i\ .
\end{align}
\end{subequations}
There exists a universal $c>0$ such that for all $m\in\mathbb{N}$, $\ell=0,\dots,|A_2|-1$, $t>0$, and $\lambda \geq c'+2$
\begin{align}

\begin{split}
	\NOrmop{ \int_{\Sigma(t)} &\mathrm{d} \vec{u} \, \prod_{i\in[\![m]\!]} \mathcal{T}_{i}(u_{i}) }
	\leq
	c^m\, m^{2} \, t^{|A_1|-1} \,  e^{\lambda t} \,
	\prod_{i\in[\![m]\!]} c_i 
	\cdot 
	\prod_{j\in A_1}\frac{t^{-b_j}}{1-b_j}
\\
	&\cdot
	\begin{cases}
	\displaystyle
	\frac{1}{ |\log(\lambda-c'-1)|^{|A_2|-1-\ell} \, |\log(t\wedge\frac{1}{2})|^{\ell+1} } & \text{ when } |A_2|>0\ ,
	\\
	\ 1 &\text{ when } |A_2|=0\ .
	\end{cases}
\end{split}
\end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:54:34.576308+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_da7b3d1412c1c6634e85648022882cd1acb73f6dadd3f0e51a2e253844245026","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 3 of arXiv:2606.14812, 'Joint convergence in Wiener chaos via transport hierarchy and Malliavin covariances'] Let $p\geq 2$ and $q\geq 1$ two integer numbers. Let $ (X_{N}= I_{p} (f_{N}), N\geq 1)$ with $f_{N} \in H ^{\odot p}$ for all $N\geq 1$. Assume (\ref{c1}). Then

