{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:21:56.724465+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_da9af1e6a8ca4c18590e047e20ad97d682a209e48c4db0e0c5fce34fdcf380af","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:interior_intersection_right of arXiv:2507.15589, 'Tightness of approximations to metrics on non-simple conformal loop ensemble gaskets'] Fix $a_1 >0$, $p \in (0,1)$. Suppose that we have the setup of Section~(se:bubble_setup of the paper) with $(D,x_0,y_0) = (\delta\mathbf{D},-i\delta,i\delta)$. Let $z_1,w_1 \in \partial B(0,\delta/2)$. Let $\eta_{z_1}^{\theta_2}$ be the angle $\theta_2$ flow line starting from $z_1$, stopped upon exiting $B(0,3\delta/4)$. Let $\eta_{w_1}^R$ be the angle $-\pi/2$ flow line starting from $w_1$, stopped upon exiting $B(0,3\delta/4)$. In case $\eta_{w_1}^R$ and $\eta_{z_1}^{\theta_2}$ intersect, sample the internal metric in the regions bounded between them. 
Let $E^p$ be the event that the following hold.
\begin{itemize}
\item The right side of $\eta_{w_1}^R$ and the left side of $\eta_{z_1}^{\theta_2}$ intersect with an angle difference of $-\pi/2 - \theta_2$, and they do not intersect in any other way.
\item There exist $x',x,y,y' \in \eta_{z_1}^{\theta_2} \cap \eta_{w_1}^R$ such that $(x,y) \in \intptsapproxbubble{U_{x',y'}}{\epsilon}$ as in~(equation eq:intpts_bubble of the paper) and $U_{x',y'} \subseteq B(0,\delta^{1+a_1})$ and $\metapproxacres{\epsilon}{U_{x',y'}}{x}{y}{\Gamma} \geq \median{\epsilon}+\epsilon^{-a_0}\mathbf{a}_{\epsilon}$.
\item The following event has conditional probability at least $p$ given $\eta_{z_1}^{\theta_2}$, $\eta_{w_1}^R$, $X_\delta^{\theta_2}$, $X_\delta^{-\pi/2}$, and the values of $h$ on these sets:
\begin{itemize}
\item The flow line $\eta_2$ merges into $\eta_{z_1}^{\theta_2}$ before entering $B(0,\delta/4)$.
\item The angle $-\pi/2$ flow line of $h$ from $-i\delta$ to $i\delta$ merges into $\eta_{w_1}^R$ before entering $B(0,\delta/4)$.
\end{itemize}
\end{itemize}
Then $\mathbf{P}[E^p] = O(\delta^{{\alpha_{\mathrm{apr}}}})$ as $\delta \to 0$.","status":"unverified"}

