{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:41:28.179692+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_db8b323b84fa364d3ba4e3cf289b3ca00f18a1339b2e0fbdcd35a5fb59cc39a7","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:balanced of arXiv:2511.10630, 'Cutoff for generalised Bernoulli--Laplace urn models'] Let $d \geqslant 2$ be a fixed integer. Consider a sequence $\{\mathbf{X}^{(n)}\}_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$ of balanced generalised Bernoulli--Laplace chains where the $n^{\mathrm{th}}$ chain has parameters $(d, n, \mu_n)$. Suppose that the Cheeger constant of the single ball transition matrix satisfies $\Phi_{\ast}^{(n)} = \Omega(1)$. Then, for any $\varepsilon \in (0,1)$, we have
    \begin{equation}

        \lim_{n \rightarrow \infty} \frac{t^{(n)}_{\textup{mix}}(\varepsilon)}{n t_{\textup{mix,\,single}}^{(n)}(1/\sqrt{n})} = 1,
    \end{equation}
    where $t_{\textup{mix,\,single}}^{(n)}(1/\sqrt{n})$ denotes the $(1/\sqrt{n})$-mixing time of the rate $1$ single ball chain. In particular, the sequence $\{\mathbf{X}^{(n)}\}_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$ exhibits cutoff. Moreover, the cutoff window is of size $O(n)$. Further, if $\mu_n$ is symmetric for each $n \in \mathbb{N}$, then the equality in (equation eqn:mix2 of the paper) continues to hold under the assumption that $\Phi_{\ast}^{(n)} = \Omega(n^{\alpha-1})$ for some $\alpha > 0$, with a cutoff window of size $O(n/\gamma_n)$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:33:01.774566+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_db8d1ab16338c1bd83bbcccf2b747566a768d661bc7cdfb2141c350ca69a9176","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 30 (Careless logarithmic scale) of arXiv:2605.14511, 'Clumsy and Careless:\\ Stationary-Entry Flux in Non-monotone Coupon Collectors'] For fixed $p\in(0,1)$ and $q=1-p$,
\[
        \log T_{n,p}
        =\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\log\frac1q
          +\log\frac{n^n}{n!}
          +\OO_{\mathbb P}(1).
\]
Equivalently, by Stirling's formula,
\[
        \log T_{n,p}
        =\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\log\frac1{1-p}
          +n-\frac12\log(2\pi n)+\OO_{\mathbb P}(1).
\]","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:57:59.897492+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_db8d860359783d1b9a9bfdd37158922de127942290862d29c2a4b418bdb960dc","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma of arXiv:2607.09946, 'Statistics on Yau's conjecture: Variance asymptotics'] If $f\randin \mathcal{C}^r(M)$ is a Gaussian field, then its covariance function $C$ is in $\mathcal{C}^{r,r}(M\times M)$ and for any $l,k\le r$, we have
