{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:48:43.562911+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_de9d38d32031308774972404394fe027cc0a2b32320a440ffd6e9f228058e431","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 10 (Iterative edge-colored bipartite graph process) of arXiv:2608.07239, 'Unified framework for asymptotically uniform iterative construction of generalised random '] Consider the bipartite vertex set $V=A\cup B$ with $A=\{v_1,\ldots,v_n\}$ and $B=\{v_1',\ldots,v_{n'}'\}$, and for $g=1,\ldots,\chi$, consider degree sequences $\d^{(g)}=(d_1^{(g)},\ldots,d_n^{(g)})$ and $\d'^{(g)}=(d_1'^{(g)},\ldots,d_{n'}'^{(g)})$ of non-negative integers with $m^{(g)}:=d_1^{(g)}+\ldots+d_n^{(g)}=d_1'^{(g)}+\ldots+d_{n'}'^{(g)}>0$, and consider a set $X^{(g)}$ of forbidden edges. For $g=1,\ldots,\chi$, define $$\b^{(g)}_{i,j}:=\frac{d_i^{(g)}d_j'^{(g)}}{2m^{(g)}}+\sum_{\substack{h=g+1,\ldots,\chi\\(v_i,v_j')\not\in X^{(h)}}}\frac{d_i^{(h)}d_j'^{(h)}}{m^{(h)}}.$$ For $g=1,\ldots,\chi$, sample $E^{(g)}$ with the following steps:
		\begin{itemize}
			\item Start with no edges $E_0^{(g)}=\emptyset$.
			\item For $r=0,\ldots,m^{(g)}-1$, let $d_{i,r}^{(g)}$ and $d_{j,r}'^{(g)}$ denote the degrees of vertices $v_i$ and $v_j'$ in $(V,E_r^{(g)})$, and consider those pairs of vertices $(v_i,v_j')\in A\times B$ with $d_{i,r}^{(g)}<d_i^{(g)}$, $d_{j,r}'^{(g)}<d_j'^{(g)}$, and $(v_i,v_j')\not\in E_r^{(g)}\cup X^{(g)}\cup E^{(1)}\cup\ldots\cup E^{(g-1)}$.
			\begin{itemize}
				\item If no such pairs exist, let $E_{r+1}^{(g)}=E_r^{(g)}$.
				\item Otherwise, choose such a pair $(v_i,v_j')$ at random with probability proportional to $(d_i^{(g)}-d_{i,r}^{(g)})(d_j'^{(g)}-d_{j,r}'^{(g)})(1-\b^{(g)}_{i,j})$, and let $E_{r+1}^{(g)}=E_r^{(g)}\cup\{(v_i,v_j')\}$.
			\end{itemize}
			\item Finally, apply rejection sampling to sample $E^{(g)}=E_{m^{(g)}}^{(g)}$ with $m^{(g)}$ edges.
		\end{itemize}
		This guarantees that $(V,E^{(g)})$ for $g=1,\ldots,\chi$ are edge-disjoint simple bipartite graphs with degree sequences $\d^{(g)}$ and $\d'^{(g)}$ on their parts and with no edges from $X^{(g)}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:31:49.836673+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_de9e6f313a6c13bd0c82628f8f978c1af7f749c4aaafd230bf492a0a39b14492","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 12 of arXiv:2506.13347, 'Ergodic theorem for the differential equations with interaction'] Let $a: \mathbb{R}^{2} \times \mathcal{M} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}^{2}$ be a Lipschitz function on both variables and  for every $\mu\in\mathcal{M},$ $a(\cdot, \mu) \in C'_b(\mathbb R^2)$.  Let $\varepsilon>0$ and $\mu_0\in \mathcal{M}_{D}. $   A closed domain $D \subset \mathbb{R}^{2}$ is flow invariant with respect to differential equation with interactions
$$
\left\{\begin{array}{l}
d x(u, t)=a\left(x(u, t), \mu_{t}\right) d t \\
x(u, 0)=u, u \in D \\
\mu_{t}=\mu_0 \circ x(\cdot, t)^{-1}
\end{array}\right.
$$
if for   any $\mu \in \mathcal{M}_{D^\varepsilon},$   $\langle a(x, \mu), \xi\rangle \leq 0$ for every $x \in D$ and every $\xi \in \hat{N}_{D}(x)$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:36:44.548695+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_de9fb4d96276a59f05082dcfc1bfa55787cf57079a89f21f08e5ddb616133c23","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 40 of arXiv:2604.01852, 'The on/off Brownian snake'] In the setting of Proposition \ref{proposition identification oobmm h erased tree}, the process $(\Tilde{\mathcal{Z}}_y^{(\varepsilon)})_{y \geq 0}$, defined to be, for $y \geq 0$,
    \begin{equation*}
        \Tilde{\mathcal{Z}}_y^{(\varepsilon)} \coloneqq \sum_{i \in I} \sum_{\substack{p: A^i_{\gamma^{(h)}_{p, i}}(s)=1 \hspace{0.1cm} \forall 0 \leq s \leq y, \\ y \in [Y_{p, i}^{(h)}, Z_{p, i}^{(h)})}} \delta_{w^i_{\gamma^{(h)}_{p, i}}(y)}, 
    \end{equation*}
    is a $\text{BBM}(2/\varepsilon, 2/\varepsilon + c)$ starting from a Poisson random measure with intensity $\mu_{\rm a}/\varepsilon$.
    \begin{proof}
        This follows in the same way as for the $\text{BBM}(4/\varepsilon)$ (which is the same as $\text{BBM}(2/\varepsilon, 2/\varepsilon)$) given in Proposition \ref{proposition identification oobmm h erased tree}. The difference here is that once an active particle moves to the dormant state it is no longer included in the sum, and this is what increases the death rate by $c$.
    \end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:47:21.610699+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_dea4b8413d2bd58f60519e5b6d5de92fbc4a9e9c3d19df2950a82844af412d62","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:admiss-ST of arXiv:2412.09087, 'General Markovian randomized equilibrium existence and construction in zero-sum Dynkin gam'] A stopping time for Player $i=1,2$ is said to be admissible if it is on the form
\begin{align}
\tau_i = \tau_{\Psi^{(i)}} = \inf \{t \geq 0: \Psi_t^{(i)} \geq E_i \}
\end{align}
where (i)
$\Psi^{(i)} = (\Psi_t^{(i)})_{t \geq 0}$,  with $\Psi_{0-}^{(i)}= 0$, is a 
non-decreasing, right-continuous and ${\cal F}^X$-adapted process that takes values in $[0,\infty]$, and 
(ii) $E_i$ is an $Exp(1)$-distributed random variable (assumed to live on our probability space) that is independent of all other random sources.  

The spaces of admissible stopping times are denoted by $\mathbb{T}_i,i=1,2$.","status":"unverified"}

