{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:23:03.036292+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_df1f3ccf8003ba4587952c98b3072c6e3301a1a93ec2b6e0dcd5e59f9e762f5b","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 8 of arXiv:2605.03182, 'Exponential integrability of the solution to the stochastic Burgers equation driven by whi'] For any $\gamma < \frac{1}{4}$, for any $T>0$, there are $\delta_1 > 0$,  $0<\varepsilon_1<\frac{1}{4}-\gamma$, $c_{T, \varepsilon_1} > 0$ and $\theta := 2(\frac{1}{4}-\varepsilon_1-\gamma)^{-1} > 0$ such that,
	$\P$-a.s., 
	for any $t\in[0,T],$ we have   
	\begin{equation}
		 \| X^{n,x_n,u}_t \|_{L^2_{\xi}}^2 \leqslant e^{-\delta_1 t} \| x_n\|^2_{L^2_{\xi}}+c_{T, \varepsilon_1} \left( 1 + \left(K^{}_{\varepsilon_1,  4}\right)^{\theta} + \| u \|^2_{L^2_t L^2_{\xi}} \right).	
	\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:33:21.478202+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_df25b55ffafa1c2ca05017fb558de76a2d160178ff490772c5a16ea706b5e0f8","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:spec-Borel of arXiv:2606.21391, 'Extreme points and faces in the moment problem'] Let $\mu$ be a Borel measure on a topological space $X$. Then $\mu$ is a 
\emph{Radon measure}

\footnote{Radon measures are also known as \emph{tight} measures [topsoe70].}

[schwartz73] if $\mu$ is inner regular on compact sets, that is to say, the 
measure $\mu(A)$ of any $A\in\mathcal{B}(X)$ is the supremum of $\mu(B)$ over all compact 
subsets $B\subset A$. The measure $\mu$ is \emph{$\tau$-smooth}

\footnote{It is well known that every Radon measure is $\tau$-smooth
[bogachev07-vol2]. Also, for every $\tau$-smooth 
measure $\mu$ there exists a smallest closed set $A$ of full measure 
$\mu(A)=\mu(X)$, called the \emph{support} of $\mu$.}

if $\mu(\bigcup_{i\in I}A_i)=\sup_{i\in I}\mu(A_i)$ for every increasing net 
$(A_i)_{i\in I}$ of open sets [topsoe70,winkler85]. Let $R$ and $P\!_\tau$
be the sets of Radon and $\tau$-smooth probability measures on $X$, respectively.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:51:26.730399+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_df2b04e33422791d2fd96b7c46d4a7e1ba7fac4d615eea472d163ae6e32d4572","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:Poisson of arXiv:2606.08325, 'Digit Mixing under Polynomial Maps'] For every fixed \(K\ge1\), the following convergence in distribution holds:
			\begin{equation}
			(A_{N+1},\ldots,A_{N+K})
			\xrightarrow{D}
			(Z_1,\ldots,Z_K),
			\end{equation}
			where \(Z_1,\ldots,Z_K\) are independent Poisson random variables of mean $\lambda := \frac{C^d}{d!}\Gamma(1/d)^d$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:05:37.777869+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_df2d3447e6f076d64e5ee9ca5ddb9de462ffeee324358f20f95dfd32fd086771","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Cor 3.1 of arXiv:2608.00420, '{Weighted $L^p$ solutions of scalar BSDEs with general unbounded stochastic coefficients}\'] Let the generator $g$ satisfy assumption (A:H1 of the paper) plus $\mu_\cdot\in {\mathbb R}_+$ (or assumption (A:H1 of the paper)(i)-(ii) plus (equation eq:2.3* of the paper) or assumption (A:H1' of the paper)) with the nonnegative process $f_\cdot$ satisfying $\mathbb{E}\left[(\int_0^\tau f_t{\rm d}t)^{p}\right]<+\infty$ instead of (equation eq:2.2 of the paper). Assume further that for some constant $M>0$,
\begin{align}
\int_0^\tau\left(\mu_t+\frac{\theta}{2(p-1)}\nu_t^2\right)^+{\rm d}t\leq M.
\end{align}
Then, for each $\xi\in L_\tau^p(0;{\mathbb R})$, BSDE (equation BSDE1.1 of the paper) admits an adapted solution $(y_t,z_t)_{t\in[0,\tau]}$ such that $y_\cdot\in S_\tau^p(0;{\mathbb R})$. Furthermore, if
\begin{align}
\int_0^\tau\left(\mu_t+\frac{\theta}{2[1\wedge(p-1)]}\nu_t^2\right)^+{\rm d}t\leq M,
\end{align}
then $z_\cdot\in M_\tau^p(0;{\mathbb R}^{d})$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:35:19.871702+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_df2fbceee39ccdc4edcc30d353f0a85875e3e7b2cab43acd3a27aa262b228d6b","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition eq:fully_linear_val_sec2 of arXiv:2608.24963, 'Why and When Neural Networks Improve Local Approximation in Optimization'] A model $m_k$ is said to be \emph{fully linear} on $\Ball{x_k}{\Delta_k}$ if there exist constants $\kappa_f,\kappa_g>0$, independent of $k$, such that for all $x\in\Ball{x_k}{\Delta_k}$,
\begin{equation}
    |f(x)-m_k(x)| \le \kappa_f\Delta_k^2,
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
    \|\nabla f(x)-\nabla m_k(x)\| \le \kappa_g\Delta_k.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:50:27.006811+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_df2fe780eb65d95327aef1525b8dc7e983bb58428a3f8d08f02af938db0b5ce4","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm thm7a of arXiv:2505.11700, 'Distribution of the cokernels of determinantal row-sparse matrices'] Let $k \ge 3$ be an odd integer, $k_n=k$ for all $n$ and $r$ be a positive integer. Then for all sufficiently large $n$, 
\begin{equation} 
\mathbb{P}(\dim_{\mathbb{F}_2} \ker \overline{A_n} \ge r) 
\ge \frac{1}{4r!} \left ( \frac{2(k-1)}{e^{k-1}} \right )^r.
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:18:27.577868+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_df319b8b4e48a8631a48e2d23d7274944eb12f9f5886d8020995edbf652df15f","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Def 41 of arXiv:2510.23423, 'One-arm exponents of the high-dimensional Ising model'] Let $\n\in \Omega_{G_\fg}$ and $x,y\in V_\fg$.
\begin{enumerate}
    \item[$(i)$] We say that $x$ is \emph{connected} to $y$ in $\n$ and write $x\overset{\n}{\longleftrightarrow} y$, if there is a sequence of points $x_0=x,x_1,\ldots, x_\ell=y$ such that $\n_{x_ix_{i+1}}>0$ for $0\leq i \leq \ell-1$.
    \item[$(ii)$] The \emph{cluster} of $x$ in $\n$, denoted by $\mathbf{C}_\n(x)$, is the set of points connected to $x$ in $\n$.
\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:32:11.122679+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_df33388b51db93395362b4652b66a32c41abe1886666b5421c5fc4b92f2dfe68","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem th:mainSPECTRUMgraph of arXiv:2510.00748, '\bf On irreducible central limit theorems'] Let $Z_n$ be as in (equation eq:bilformsubsec of the paper), and let $G_n=(V_n,\mathcal{E}_n)$ be its associated graph. Suppose that there exists $k\ge 2$ such that, as $n\to \infty$,
\begin{equation}
\liminf_n \mu_k^{(n)} >0.
\end{equation}
Then $\{Z_n\}$ is irreducible, in the sense of Definition (def:irreducibility of the paper). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:12:19.528235+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_df339cec12beb066d7f13f1cb5798378455d14f5c6aaff876ef17b08bbf18957","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 9 of arXiv:2608.18557, 'Randomly Permuted Orthogonal Products and Fast Dimension Reduction'] Let \(X\) be a random variable and let \(\sigma,M>0\). Suppose that
	\[
	\log \E e^{\lambda X}
	\le
	\lambda^2\sigma^2
	\qquad
	\text{for }|\lambda|\le\frac1M.
	\]
	Then, for every \(t\ge0\),
	\[
	\Pp\{|X|\ge t\}
	\le
	2\exp\left[
	-\min\left\{
	\frac{t^2}{4\sigma^2},
	\frac{t}{2M}
	\right\}
	\right].
	\]","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:30:24.617912+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_df3857f993be4c3fed94826e3be21eae14cc2af5e28b5fb50c9b6aa3027feacc","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 33 of arXiv:2506.09592, 'Extremal process of the local time of simple\\random walk on a regular tree'] For all~$\lambda>0$, $\sigma\in(0,1/10)$ and~$\delta\in(0,1)$ there exist~$c>0$ and~$a_2\ge0$ such that for all $s\ge 1$, all~$u$ satisfying $s\ge u\ge s-\lambda$ and all~$n$ with~$s\le n^{1-\delta}$,
\begin{equation}

\begin{aligned}
Q_{n,s,u}\Biggl(\biggl(\,\bigcap_{k=1}^n\Bigl\{h(x_k)\le&\frac {n-k}n\wt m_n+s+\gamma_{n,a}(k)\Bigr\}\biggr)^\cc\,\Biggr)
\\
&\le
c\frac{(1+s-u)s}n\sum_{1\le k\le n/2}\bigl[k+\gamma_{n,a}(k)^2\bigr]\texte^{-\frac18 c_2\gamma_{n,a}(k)}
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
holds for all~$a\ge a_2$ with
\begin{equation}

\gamma_{n,a}(k):=a+\bigl(\min\{k,n-k\}\bigr)^\sigma.
\end{equation}
Here~$c_2$ is as in \eqref{E:4.73}. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:52:17.370365+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_df3d94caa37073cd58b9fb320eab669ca866d2a2c25e65c947df68871a719292","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem 2 of arXiv:2606.03661, '2606.03661'] Let $0<\alpha<1$ and $\nu>-1$. Suppose that $f$ is such that the Mellin transforms exist, the boundary term in the Mellin transform of $f^\prime$ vanishes, and the Mellin convolution theorem applies. Then
\begin{equation}

\mathcal{M}[\mathscr{D}_t^{(\alpha,\nu)} f;s]
=
 \frac{(1-s)\Gamma(1+\nu-s)}{\Gamma(2+\nu-\alpha-s)}F(s-1). 
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:51:52.057156+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_df425a7616b0361b707e0b3db514589fc3e648dfb192467c330efabae9f9870d","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:R<p/q of arXiv:2607.14062, 'Gilbert's disc model conditioned on the square lattice'] Let $a,b\in \mathbb{Z}_{>0} = \{1,2,\dots\}$, let $R \in (0, a/b)$, and let $u_0,\ldots,u_n\in\mathbb{Z}^2$. If there exists $P\in\mathcal P$ such that $u=(u_0,\ldots,u_n)$ is a path of $\Gamma_{p,R}^{\texttt{CN}}(P)$ (resp.\ of $\Gamma^{\texttt{NN}}_{p,R}(P)$), then 
    \begin{equation}
    \forall k\in \{0,\ldots, n-b\}, \quad \|u_{k+b}-u_k\|_\infty \leq a.
    \end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:46:00.393359+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_df45b11f81c7571f74a0591116316126d7b4419dd0d96385f4c2b35686c3b7b6","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:F of arXiv:2607.21443, 'The critical KPZ scale for the averaging process'] There exists $C>0$ such that, for all $1/2<\lambda \le 7/8$, $T>0$ and $N$ large enough,
	\begin{equation}
		\int_0^T f_{\lambda,N}(t)\,{\rm d} t \le C e^{CT}\set{N^{-1}+N^{2\lambda-5/2}\,T^{1/2}}\; \text{.}
	\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:07:17.257040+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_df472699c3f914b5c15b3a4dee3e611d98850ee4d1e42d4bc5b898fa8ed8f161","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition AUIASLc of arXiv:2603.12774, 'Synchronization by noise for stochastic differential equations driven by fractional Browni'] Consider the setting of Proposition~(POINT of the paper). Let \( (x_i)_{i \geq 1} \) be an arbitrary sequence in \( \mathbb{R}^d \). Then there exists an increasing sequence \( (s_n)_{n \geq 1} \) with \( s_n \to \infty \) as \( n \to \infty \), such that
	\begin{align*}
