{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:04:40.950910+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e10c4a29d8f070db4e8ca8b81db10c95a604be746e9f3faebf2105809d096dc0","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition P:crt-asy-Feller of arXiv:2412.03521, 'On ergodic properties of stochastic PDEs'] Let $\left\{t_{n}; n \ge 1\right\}$ and $\left\{\delta_n; n \ge 1\right\}$ be
  two positive sequences with $\{t_n\}$ nondecreasing and $\{\delta_n\}$
  converging to zero. A semigroup $P_t$ on a Hilbert space $\mathcal{H}$ is
  asymptotic strong Feller if, for all $\varphi: \mathcal{H} \to \mathbb{R}$
  with $\|\varphi\|_\infty$ and $\|\nabla\varphi\|_\infty$ finite, we have
  \begin{align}
    \left\lvert\nabla{P}_{t_n}\varphi(x)\right\rvert
    \le C(\|x\|)\left(\|\varphi\|_\infty+\delta_n\|\nabla\varphi\|_\infty\right),
  \end{align}
  where $C:\mathbb{R}_+\to\mathbb{R}$ is a fixed nondecreasing function. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:26:52.661011+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e10ea10f678b393be95be427f3090683e1c9f70a3ce189808355c3d03d23dcdc","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 3 (homogeneous bundle and induced representation) of arXiv:2609.03736, '2609.03736'] The homogeneous bundle associated with $E$ is
\begin{equation}
 \cE_E=\mathsf G\times_{\mathsf H}E\longrightarrow X

\end{equation}
with $(gh,e)\sim(g,he)$.  Its fiber over $x=g\mathsf H$ is denoted $E_x$.
The Hilbert space of sections is the induced representation
\begin{equation}
 \mathcal I_E=L^2(X,\cE_E)=\Ind_{\mathsf H}^{\mathsf G}E.

\end{equation}
Equivalently, $\mathcal I_E$ consists of functions $f:\mathsf G\to E$
satisfying $f(gh)=h^{-1}f(g)$, with inner product
\begin{equation}
 \langle f_1,f_2\rangle
 =\frac1{|X|}\sum_{g\mathsf H\in X}
   \langle f_1(g),f_2(g)\rangle_E.

\end{equation}
The group $\mathsf G$ acts by left translation.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:33:23.330524+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e1130e704295674e27d9a4ff5a45ac8ba8a0fa3dc1a123720a7311dbb596f70a","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 8 of arXiv:2506.22020, 'Norm-dependent Lamperti-type MAP representations of stable processes and Brownian motions '] We say that a function $f\in C^2_b(\mathbb{R}\times\mathcal{S}^{d,+}_{1})$ is in \textit{class $\mathcal{D}$} if it satisfies for each $1\leq i\leq d$ and $\boldsymbol{w}\in[0,\infty)^d\setminus\{\boldsymbol{0}_d\}\text{ with } w_i=0$, the condition
	\begin{equation}

	\Big(\boldsymbol{e}_0+\boldsymbol{e}_i-\|\boldsymbol{w}\|^{-1}_1\sum_{j\neq i} w_j\boldsymbol{e}_j\Big)\cdot\gradient f\big(\log{\|\boldsymbol{w}\|_1},\arg(\boldsymbol{w})\big)=0,
\end{equation}
where the set of vectors $\{\boldsymbol{e}_0,\boldsymbol{e}_1,\ldots,\boldsymbol{e}_d\}$ is just the standard orthonormal basis of $\mathbb{R}^{d+1}.$","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:13:31.950527+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e11f1da31e279efd02487dcd322ae1a2bf3bdeca6236fef51f54006343ef2eb3","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop maximum_principle of arXiv:2506.12171, 'The multinomial dimer model'] Suppose that $q, q_+, q_-$ are a solution, supersolution, and subsolution respectively to the critical gauge equation $\mathcal{C}_n(\cdot)= 1$ on a region $U\subset B\cap \varepsilon\Lambda$. Then 
	\begin{enumerate}[(i)]
		\item the minimum value of $q/q_-$ over $U$ is realized on $\partial U$. 
		\item the maximum value of $q/q_+$ over $U$ is realized on $\partial U$;
	\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:20:15.484470+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e120b94fe8fade79175a27cab1af629939ed0fe3874dc5fe999d996a1a7bf5c9","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Cor 56 of arXiv:2509.01797, 'Relation between Wick powers and excursion clusters of the 2D GFF'] Let $\eta\in (0,1)$ and $k\geq 0$.
