    where $d_{TV}$ is the total variation distance. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:32:11.128518+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e1f12f953eaf5d048e33b0527115e90d69f087d6748f2b33e2195681b7b5ece0","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 2q:moment of arXiv:2510.09818, 'On the Fourier dimension of fractional Brownian graphs'] [LL25].
Let $B$ be a fractional Brownian motion with Hurst parameter $H \in (0,1)$, and recall that the Fourier transform of $\mu_{G}$ is given by~(equation b1 of the paper). Then for any $q\in \mathbb N_+$ and $\xi_1, \xi_2 \in \mathbb R \setminus\{0\}$, we have
\begin{align}
	\mathbb E\left[ |\hat{\mu}_G(\xi_1,\xi_2)|^{2q} \right]
	= \frac{(q!)^2}{|\xi_2|^{2q/H}} \sum_{\varepsilon\in \mathcal{A}_{2q}} \mathcal{I}_{T}^{\lambda}[\varepsilon, G_\varepsilon],
\end{align}
where the parameters $\lambda, T$ and the function $G_{\varepsilon}$ are chosen as
\begin{align}
	\lambda = \frac{\xi_1}{|\xi_2|^{1/H}}, \qquad
	T = |\xi_2|^{1/H} ,\quad \text{ and }\quad
	G_\varepsilon(u) = e^{-\pi\mathrm{Var}(\sum_{i=1}^{2q} \varepsilon_i B_{u_i})}.
\end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:11:02.381046+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e20c1e8563462971a1aba3d28ef18bb23fb5392a9409a408165f94a3e755a438","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:global_clt of arXiv:2507.10531, 'Quantitative central limit theorems for exponential random graphs'] As in (equation eq:sigmadef_global_intro of the paper), set
\begin{equation}

    \sigma_n^2 = 
    \mleft( 1 - 2 p (1-p) \sum_{j=1}^K \beta_j \mathsf{e}_j (\mathsf{e}_j-1) p^{\mathsf{e}_j-2} \mright)^{-1}
    \times p(1-p) \binom{n}{2},
\end{equation}
Then for any $\varepsilon > 0$, if $X$ is distributed as (equation eq:tilt of the paper) with $Y \sim \mathcal{G}(n,p)$ and $\mathsf{g}$ given in
(equation eq:smallgdef of the paper), under the ``phase-uniqueness or forest'' assumption, we have
\begin{equation}
    \mathbf{d} \mleft( \frac{\mathsf{E}(X) - \mathbb{E}[\mathsf{E}(X)]}{\sigma_n}, Z \mright) \lesssim n^{-\frac{1}{2} + \varepsilon},
