where the infimum on the second line runs over all q-neighbourhoods of $\pi$.  The analogous formulas hold if `$\rmh^0$' is replaced with `$\ul{\rmh}^0$' and `$\limsup$' is replaced with `$\liminf$'.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:01:39.422264+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e90216ea71175462a3093081b0e3ec8890f74b30ddf0524fdab6852712c93ade","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition equation proposition of arXiv:2607.22156, 'A random matrix approach to lamplighter groups'] Let $I,P$ be defined as above and $B\subset P$. We denote $\mathfrak{C}\mathfrak{C}(T)$ the set of connected components of $T$, not to be confused with $\mathcal{C}\mathcal{C}(T)$ the set of colored component with respect to some families. Let $\sigma\in P(\sqcup_{i\in I}V_{i})$, then 
        \begin{multline}
