    More precisely, for $f \in H^{1,1}_{0}(0,\infty):= \{u \in L^2(0,\infty): u',xu \in L^2(0,\infty), u(0)=0\}$, define $\mathcal{H}_{\beta}(t)f$ as the distribution such that for any $g \in C_0^{\infty}(0,\infty)$,
    \begin{equation}
        \langle \mathcal{H}_{\beta}(t) f,  g \rangle := \int_{0}^{\infty} (f'(x) g'(x)+xf(x)g(x))dx+\frac{2}{\sqrt{\beta}}\int_{0}^{\infty} f(x)g(x) \xi_1(t)[dx],
    \end{equation}
    with $\int_{0}^{\infty} f(x)g(x) \xi_1(t)[dx] = \langle \xi_1(t), f(x)g(x) \rangle$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:48:35.816337+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_eb2b644cd94fbb04994d22be1e9956d6f5c37cbbbf9e625de119fe5d08ea5e42","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:informal of arXiv:2411.09516, 'Sharp Matrix Empirical Bernstein Inequalities\footnote{NeurIPS 2025 Poster}'] Let $ \mathbf{X} _1,\dots ,  \mathbf{X} _n$ be symmetric matrices with eigenvalues in $[0,1]$ and common conditional mean $ \mathbf{M}  =  \mathbb E [  \mathbf{X} _i |  \mathbf{X} _1,\dots,  \mathbf{X} _{i-1} ]$. 
    For an appropriate weighted average $\hat  \mathbf{M} _n$ of $ \mathbf{X} _1,\dots,  \mathbf{X} _n$ and an appropriate sample variance proxy $v_{n,\alpha} =v(\alpha,  \mathbf{X} _1,\dots,  \mathbf{X} _n) > 0$,
    \begin{equation}
     \mathbb P \left( \lambda_{\max}(\hat \mathbf{M} _n -   \mathbf{M} ) \geqslant \sqrt{\frac{2 \log(d/\alpha) v_{n,\alpha}} {n}}   \right) \leqslant \alpha.
\end{equation}

    Further, if $\{ \mathbf{X} _i\}$ have a common conditional variance $ \mathbf{V} $, $v_{n,\alpha}$ converges almost surely to 
    $\| \mathbf{V}  \|$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:51:52.062366+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_eb2cd1d6094b9fc90858661a259a137d2299149da811ada9e9fd3458c5683878","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition of arXiv:2607.14993, 'Sharp phase transition for percolation with short-range dependencies'] Let $\Omega = \{0, 1\}^E$ with $E$ at most countable. For $\omega \in \Omega$ and $F, G \subset E$ such that $F \cap G = \emptyset$, we denote
  \begin{equation}
    \omega^F_G : e \in E \mapsto \begin{cases}
      1 & \text{ if } e \in F \\
      0 & \text{ if } e \in G \\
      \omega(e) & \text{ otherwise}
    \end{cases}
  \end{equation}
  If $F$ or $G$ is empty, we omit it. If $F$ or $G$ is a singleton $\{e\}$, we write $e$ instead of $\{e\}$\footnote{We always write $\omega(e)$ for the actual value of the $e$-component of $\omega$ in order to avoid confusion.}.
  Consider an increasing event $A \subset \Omega$. We define the event that $e \in E$ is pivotal for $A$ to be
  \begin{equation}
    \{\omega \in \Omega: (\omega_e \notin A) \wedge (\omega^e \in A)\}.
  \end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:01:54.384582+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_eb3d565ccdeae0a93a818cb8ff981063033d1d406e814a3e3ee66b46c2cc3e0b","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lm:T.bds of arXiv:2411.02046, 'Speed of Convergence and Moderate Deviations of FPP on Random Geometric Graphs'] Let $d \geq 2$ and $r>r_c$. Then, there exist $C,c>0$ and $\beta^\dagger >1$ such that, for all $x \in \R^d$ and every $t> \beta^\dagger \|x\|$,
    \begin{equation} 
        \mu\big(D(o,x) \geq t\big)  \leq Ce^{-c t}. 
    \end{equation}

    Additionally, if (equation A2 of the paper) holds, then there exist $c_{1}, c_{2}>0$ and $\beta>1$ such that, for all $x \in \R^d$ and every $t> \beta \|x\|$,
    \begin{equation} 
        \p(T(x)\geq t) \leq c_{1} e^{-c_{2} t}, 
    \end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:16:53.597504+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_eb3e06c0f161941d04e8dab169d506b6a6ca6d40c2233d45bcf535fc28ab1df7","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:diffusionType of arXiv:2412.05030, 'Characterization of Subordinate Symmetric Markov Processes'] ([BKKL])

  Assume $(\mathcal{E},\mathcal{F})$ satisfies the diffusion type heat kernel estimate for a scale function $\phi_c$ and 
  let $S$~ be an independent subordinator whose Bernstein function is given in ((eq:Bernstein function of the paper))
  with $\phi := \phi_c$.
  Then, the jump kernel $J(x,y)$ of the subordinate process satisfies 
  the following estimate: 
  \begin{equation}
    J(x,y) \simeq \frac{1}{V(x,r)} \frac{1}{\psi(r)},
  \end{equation}
  where $r := d(x,y)$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:06:42.724644+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_eb42b47e280a22b8340587201a6314a0f19225dca4c0806e6e627dfc013204da","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem new of arXiv:2411.04238, 'Holomorphic jump-diffusions'] Let $X=(X_t)_{t\in [0,T}$ be an $S$-valued jump-diffusion with characteristics $(b,a,K)$ and extended generator $\mathcal A$.  Assume that $b_j,a_{ij}\in\mathcal O(S)$, $K$ is a kernel with holomorphic jump size, and let $F$ be the corresponding non-negative measure on the measurable space $E$. 
Let $G$ satisfy (equation eqnG of the paper) and
for each $h\in H(P_G^d(0))$, $z\in P^d_{R(S)+\varepsilon}(0)$, and $y\in E$ set $\mathcal D(J)$ and $Jh$ as in (equation eqnDJ of the paper).
Set 
 \begin{align*}
     \mathcal{V}&\subseteq\{h\in H(P^d_G(0))\colon e^h\in \mathcal D(J)
     \text{ and } P^d_{R(S)+\varepsilon}\ni z\mapsto Je^h(z,\cdot)\in L^1(E,F) \text{ is continuous}\},
\\  \mathcal{V}^*&:=\{\mathbf{u}\in \mathcal{S}^* \colon h_\mathbf{u}=h|_{{P}^d_{R(S)}(0)},  \ h\in \mathcal{V}\}.
 \end{align*}
Then $X$ is an $S$-valued $\mathcal{V}$-affine-holomorphic process and for all $\mathbf{u}\in\mathcal{V}^*$, $$\mathcal{A}\exp(h_\mathbf{u})=\exp(h_{\mathbf{u}})R(h_\mathbf{u})|_S,$$ where $R:\mathcal{V}^*\rightarrow \mathcal{S}^*$ is given by
\begin{align}
    R(\mathbf{u})&:= \sum_{|\beta|=1}\mathbf{u}^{(\beta)}\ast \underline{\mathbf{b}}^{\beta} 
    +\sum_{|\beta_1|,|\beta_2|=1}\frac{1}{(\beta_1+\beta_2)!}\underline{\underline{\mathbf{a}}}^{\beta_1+\beta_2}\ast ({\mathbf{u}^{(\beta_1)}}\ast\mathbf{u}^{(\beta_2)}+\mathbf{u}^{(\beta_1+\beta_2)})\\
    &+\bm{\lambda}\ast \int_{E}\bm{\exp}^*\big(\mathbf{u}\circ^s\underline\mathbf{j}( y)-\mathbf{u}\big)-\mathbf{1}-\sum_{|\beta|=1}\mathbf{u}^{(\beta)}\ast \underline{\bm{j}}( y)^{\ast\beta}\ F(d y) .\nonumber 
\end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:28:34.932113+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_eb42c2cc1e31b465ec552eccff2f29d417420cb0c3113f8158bd44aead444466","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lemma_prepare_loop_decompose of arXiv:2412.05706, 'Quasi-multiplicativity and regularity for metric graph Gaussian free fields'] For any $d\ge 3$, there exists $
	\refstepcounter{newcnstcnt}
	\ensuremath{C_{\thenewcnstcnt}}>0$ such that the following holds. Let $\mathbf{B}_1:=[\widetilde{B}(10\ensuremath{C_{\ref*{const_prepare_loop_decompose}}}M)]^c$, $\mathscr{B}_1:= \partial B(\ensuremath{C_{\ref*{const_prepare_loop_decompose}}}M)$, $\mathbf{B}_2:=\widetilde{B}(\frac{N}{10\ensuremath{C_{\ref*{const_prepare_loop_decompose}}}})$ and $\mathscr{B}_2:= \partial B(\frac{M}{\ensuremath{C_{\ref*{const_prepare_loop_decompose}}}})$. Then for any $M> N\ge 1$, $j\in \{1,2\}$, $k\in \mathbb{N}^+$, $A_1,...,A_k,D\subset \mathbf{B}_j$ and $\omega_l\in \Omega_l[N,M]$, 
