{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:33:06.539315+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ec03a4e4d1d3920c6c629bcb5e97423c471a408e0ba440be409b8efd0f1e049f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 12 of arXiv:2608.25829, 'Topology of low-dimensional generalized Ricci solitons and string backgrounds'] Let $(M^{2n},g, J)$ be a compact BHE with $\pi_1(M)$ free abelian.  Then $M$ is spin.

\begin{proof}
    Since $\pi_1(M)$ is free abelian it follows that $H_1(M)$ is torsion-free, thus the universal coefficient theorem gives $H^2(M) \cong
\operatorname{Hom}\!\left(H_2(M),\mathbb Z\right)$, hence $H^2(M)$ is torsion-free.  Since $M$ is BHE we have $c_1(M; \mathbb R) = 0$, hence $c_1(M; \mathbb Z)=0$.  Since $M$ is complex, $w_2(M)\equiv c_1(M)$ mod $2$, thus $w_2(M) = 0$.
\end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:55:18.064431+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ec051ac0341b3f52b8dc0bb8231c26cee519e999f127fa8fd19bc0d3017e97cf","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 21 (Dubins' inequality for finite time, $T < \infty$) of arXiv:2606.17452, 'Optional Stopping for Superhedging Supermartingales'] Let $f_0, f_1, \ldots, f_T,$ be a given real valued supermartingale sequence with $f_n \geq c $ where $c$ is a constant. Assume the definitions of $\rho_k, \tau_k$ given by Definition \ref{upcrossing_stopping_times}  with $T < \infty$ (i.e. the finite time case). Furthermore, assume
that $(L)-a.e.$ holds; then,
for any $k\ge 0$ and $c \leq a < b$:
\begin{equation} \nonumber 
\overline{\sigma}({\bf 1}_{\{\tau_{k+1} < T+1\}}) \leq \left(\frac{a-c}{b-c} \right)~~ \overline{\sigma}({\bf 1}_{\{\rho_{k}  < T+1\}}) \leq
\left(\frac{a-c}{b-c}\right)~~ \overline{\sigma}({\bf 1}_{\{\tau_{k}  < T+1\}}),
\end{equation}
and so
\begin{equation} 
\overline{\sigma}({\bf 1}_{\{\tau_{k+1}  < T+1\}}) \leq
\left(\frac{a-c}{b-c} \right)^{k+1} ~\overline{\sigma}({\bf 1}_{\{\rho_{0}  < T+1\}}) \leq
\left(\frac{a-c}{b-c}\right)^{k+1}.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:23:03.044796+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ec071ba98ea0bf443ed14bfff671ad0bf4e2eb1a4c5ee395adc3f1f6448f8739","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 89 of arXiv:2605.03236, 'Stochastic It\^o Equations and Parabolic Second-Order Equations with singular Drift'] We say that $X$ is a {\em regular
diffusion process 
\index{$D$@Processes!regular
diffusion process}
corresponding to
$\cL$ or to $a,b$\/} if



(i)  for any $(t,x)\in\bR^{d+1}$
there exists a $d$-dimensional Wiener process $w_{t}$, $t\geq0$,
which is a Wiener process relative to $\bar \cN_{t}$,
where $\bar \cN_{t}$ is the completion of $\cN_{t}$
with respect to all $P_{s,y}$, and such that with    
$P_{t,x}$-probability one, for
all $s\geq 0$, $\sft_{s}=t+s$ and
\begin{equation} 

x_{s}=x+\int_{0}^{s}\sigma(t+u,x_{u})\,dw_{u}
+\int_{0}^{s}b(t+u,x_{u})\,du.
\end{equation}

(ii) there exists $\rho_{b}\in(0,\infty)$ such that
\begin{equation}

  \bar b_{\rho_{b}}=\sup_{r\leq\rho_{b}}r^{-1}
\sup_{(t,x)\in\bR^{d+1}}\sup_{C\in\bC_{r}}
E_{t,x}\int_{0}^{\tau_{C}}|b(\sft_{s},x_{s})|\,ds \leq \sfb_{0},
\end{equation}
where $\tau_{C}$ is the first exit time of 
$(\sft_{s},x_{s})$ from $C$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:05:57.786560+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ec08f0cfbdf031fb1a510e1cf8e08dfa060bb3fae2bbddafad144d1d9c126513","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 45 of arXiv:2608.30549, '\vspace{-1cm}Maximally Spread Out Measures and Implications\\{} for Phase Transitions in A'] Let $(\XX,\mathcal{F})$ be a measurable space and let $\mu\colon \mathcal{F}\to [0,1]$
    be a probability measure on $\XX$.
    Let $\SC \subset \XX$ be such that $\om{\mu}(\SC)=1$.
    Then the measure $\nu$ defined via
    \begin{equation}

       \nu(A \cap \SC) := \mu(A)
       \quad \text{ for $A\in \mathcal{F}$}
    \end{equation}
    is a well-defined probability measure on $\mathcal{F}\sigmacap\SC$.
    Furthermore,
    \begin{equation}

        \om{\nu}(B) = \om{\mu}(B)
        \quad \text{ for all $B\subset \SC$}.
    \end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:56:37.666091+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ec090f7e7441a5c1b81a9d9554a2ea034819faa810dc64093f52d8e9ac7a1a75","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn eq:tau-col of arXiv:2609.00110, 'Phase Transition and Fluctuation Results for First-Passage Percolation on Spread-Out Cycle'] For $v\in\mathds{T}_n$ and $t\geqslant0$, define the one-source passage times and
passage-time balls by
\begin{align*}
 \mathsf{T}_1(v)&:=T_{\mathds{T}_n}^{(\ell)}(0,v),
 &
 \mathcal{A}_1(t)&:=\{v\in\mathds{T}_n:\mathsf{T}_1(v)\leqslant t\},\\
 \mathsf{T}_2(v)&:=T_{\mathds{T}_n}^{(\ell)}(U_n,v),
 &
 \mathcal{A}_2(t)&:=\{v\in\mathds{T}_n:\mathsf{T}_2(v)\leqslant t\}.
\end{align*}
The \emph{original-time collision time} is
\begin{align}
\tau_{\mathrm{col}}:=\inf\Big\{t\geqslant0:
&\text{ there exist }u\in\mathcal{A}_1(t),\ v\in\mathcal{A}_2(t)
\text{ with }\{u,v\}\in\mathcal{E}_n^{(\ell)}
\text{ and} \notag\\
&\go_{u,v}
\leqslant (t-\mathsf{T}_1(u))+(t-\mathsf{T}_2(v))
\Big\}.
\end{align}
Its rescaled version is
\begin{align*}
 \mathsf{S}_{\mathrm{col}}:=\mathfrak{a}_\ell\tau_{\mathrm{col}}.
\end{align*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:10:07.392058+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ec0c8bc088638630961c152f00f276b518829871f12c3592bac47244b1e26d45","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem t:hg of arXiv:2606.11503, 'Percolation on hierarchical lattices'] Let $U, \tilde{U} \subset \mathbb{R}^{n}$ be open sets and consider a diffeomorphism $f:U \to \tilde{U}$. Assume there exists $u_{*} \in U$ such that $f(u_{*}) = u_{*}$ and that the Jacobian matrix $Df(u^{*})$ has no eigenvalue with absolute value one. Then there exist an open neighborhood $V \subset U$ of $u_{*}$, an open neighborhood $\tilde{V} \subset \mathbb{R}^{n}$ of the origin, and a homeomorphism $h: V \to W$ such that $h(u^{*}) = 0$ and
\begin{equation}
f(x) = h^{-1}\Big( Df(u_{*}) h(x) \Big), \qquad \text{for all } x \in V.
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:39:41.405591+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ec10dced39543db8aab25b2a9c7c578e13438566eef9909615582da714fc31d1","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop 7 of arXiv:2604.06516, 'From stochastic individual-based models to free-boundary Hamilton-Jacobi equations'] Let $x\in\R$. We consider the birth-death-mutation process $(Z^K_t,t\ge 0)$ defined as before but started from a unique individual with trait $x$, i.e.\ $Z^K_0=\delta_{x}$, and we denote the corresponding expectation by $\mathbb{E}_{\delta_x}$.\\ 
(i) Let $\varphi:\R\to\R$ be bounded and measurable. Then, for any $t>0$, we have
		\begin{equation}

			\mathbb{E}_{\delta_{x}}\left[\langle {Z}^K_{t}, \varphi\rangle \right]=\mathbb{E}_{x}\left[\exp\left(\int_{0}^{t} R(X^K_s)\ ds \right)\varphi(X^K_{t}) \right],
		\end{equation}
		where $X^K$ is the  process defined in  \eqref{marchealeatoire}.\\
(ii) For $t>0$, $x\in \R$ and for a bounded measurable function $\Phi:\mathbb{D}[0,t]\to\mathbb{R}$,
		\begin{equation}

			\mathbb{E}_{\delta_x}\left[\langle {\Theta}^K_t,\Phi\rangle\right]=\mathbb{E}_{\delta_{x}}\left[\sum_{u\in {V}^K_{t}}\Phi({X}^{K,u}_{s},\ s\leq t) \right]
            =\mathbb{E}_{x}\left[\exp\left(\int_{0}^{t} R(X^K_{s})\ ds \right)\Phi(X^K_{s},\ s\leq t) \right].   
		\end{equation}
(iii) For all $t>0$ and $A\subset\mathbb{D}[0,t]$,
\begin{align*}
        \E (N^{K,A}_{t})  & =\int_\R K^{\beta^K_0(x)}\E_{\mu^K_{x,T}}\Big[\exp\Big(\log K\int_0^{t} R(Y_s^K) ds \Big)\mathbbm{1}_{(Y^K_{s})_{s\in[0,t]}\in A}\Big]dx.
\end{align*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:53:54.936007+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ec12f9ea36b373909b228dd31784b56d2663e9637ab9bf5089522e3d173e2c0e","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 1 of arXiv:2607.17396, '2607.17396'] Let $\left\{X_{k}, k \geq 0 \right\}$ be a critical branching process with immigration with $\lambda \in \left(0, \infty\right)$, $b^{2}\in \left(0, \infty\right)$ and $\sigma^{2}\in \left(0,\infty\right)$.
Assume that the immigration sequence $\left\{{{\varepsilon_k},k\geq 1} \right\}$ is a strictly stationary and ergodic sequence adapted to a stationary non-decreasing filtration $\left\{\mathcal{H}_{i}, i \in \mathbb{Z}\right\}$ such that $\delta_{n,2}=o\left(\sqrt{n}\right)$ as $n \to \infty$.

Then
