{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:48:24.305098+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ec2928d24b35bd0b021a4e91234988aa5f02c97b0ee802841977c23a61c438fe","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem_Gt_asymp of arXiv:2605.27949, 'Yaglom limits of continuous-state branching processes in Brownian random environment \than'] In the weakly subcritical regime $({\bf m}\in(-\sigma^2,0))$, let the time-dependent function $G_t(y, v)$ be defined as:
\begin{eqnarray}
G_t(y, v) := 2^{\frac{\eta}{2}-1} y^{\frac{\eta}{2}-1} v^{\frac{\eta}{2}-1} \mathrm{e}^{- \frac{{\bf m}^2}{2\sigma^2}t} \mathrm{e}^{-\frac{1}{2y} - v} \theta\left( \sqrt{\frac{2v}{y}}, \frac{\sigma^2\beta^2}{4}t \right),
\end{eqnarray}
where $\theta(r, t)$ is defined in Proposition (jtpdf of the paper). Then, for any $y>0$ and $v>0$,
\begin{eqnarray*}
\lim _{t \rightarrow \infty} t^{3/2} \mathrm{e}^{\frac{{\bf m}^2}{2 \sigma^2} t} G_t(y, v) = \frac{4\sqrt 2}{\sigma^3\beta^3\sqrt{ \pi }} G(y, v),
\end{eqnarray*}
where $G(y,v)$ is the time-independent kernel defined in (equation G_def of the paper). Furthermore, there exists a constant $C' > 0$ such that for all $t \ge 1$, the scaled function satisfies the uniform bound:
\begin{eqnarray*}
\left| t^{3/2} \mathrm{e}^{\frac{{\bf m}^2}{2 \sigma^2} t} G_t(y, v) \right| \le C' G(y,v).
\end{eqnarray*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:10:07.422837+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ec2a39cf4671e8b351d691aff7a7df542401ee9c94b1cf159891fe628cf95812","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem eq:array-assumptions of arXiv:2607.27613, 'Regularized Bulk Universality versus Bounded-Disorder Nonuniversality\\for Annealed Comple'] \label[theorem]{thm:bulk-universality}
Fix \(p\ge3\), \(m\ge2\), \(L>0\), \(\varepsilon>0\),
\(V\in\mathcal V_m\), and either \(f\in\mathcal F_m\) or
\(f=\ell_\eta\) for fixed \(\eta>0\).  For each \(N\), let
\(J=(J_{I,N})_{I\in[N]^p}\) have independent, not necessarily
identically distributed coordinates satisfying
\begin{equation}
 \sup_{N,I}\mathbb E e^{\abs{J_{I,N}}/K}\le2,
 \qquad
 \mathbb E J_{I,N}^r=\mathbb E G^r,\quad 1\le r\le m,
\end{equation}
where \(G\sim\mathcal N(0,1)\).  Let \((G_I)\) be independent standard Gaussians.  Then there is a constant
\[
 C=C(p,m,L,K,\varepsilon,V,f)<\infty
\]
such that, for all sufficiently large \(N\),
\begin{equation}
 \left|\log\frac{\mathbb E\mathcal Z_{N,L}^J(V,\varepsilon,f)}
 {\mathbb E\mathcal Z_{N,L}^G(V,\varepsilon,f)}\right|
 \le C Nq_N^{m-1}.
\end{equation}
Consequently, for every \(p\ge3\) and every \(m\ge2\),
\begin{equation}
 \Phi_{N,L}^J(V,\varepsilon,f)-\Phi_{N,L}^G(V,\varepsilon,f)\longrightarrow0.
\end{equation}
Assume additionally that
\begin{equation}
 (m-1)(p-2)>2.
\end{equation}
Then
\begin{equation}
 \frac{\mathbb E\mathcal Z_{N,L}^J(V,\varepsilon,f)}
 {\mathbb E\mathcal Z_{N,L}^G(V,\varepsilon,f)}\longrightarrow1,
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
 \Phi_{N,L}^J(V,\varepsilon,f)-\Phi_{N,L}^G(V,\varepsilon,f)=o(N^{-1}).
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:52:32.333609+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ec2e4b849037a6390f19c27c15ba96da87b5db06cdacf4810c148b6ccec0783f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:patching of arXiv:2609.03980, 'Decay Rates and Domain Dependence of a Coupled Wave-Heat System with Spatially Dependant H'] Let $(\Omega,\Omega_w,\Omega_h)$ be a Lipschitz interface triple with interface $\gamma$. Let $\pi\in L^2(\Omega)$ be a patching of $w,h$, i.e., $\pi|_{\Omega_w} = w, \pi|_{\Omega_h }=h$.
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item Assume $w\in H^1(\Omega_w), h\in H^1(\Omega_h)$, then $w,h$ have matching trace $${\operatorname{tr}}^{\gamma}(w) ={\operatorname{tr}}^{\gamma}(h)$$ if and only if $\pi$ is an element of $ H^1(\Omega)$.
        \item Assume $w\in H^{\operatorname{div}}(\Omega_w), h\in H^{\operatorname{div}}(\Omega_h)$, then $w,h$ have matching normal trace $${\operatorname{tr}}^{\gamma}_N(w) = -{\operatorname{tr}}^{\gamma}_N(h)$$ if and only if $\pi$ is an element of $ H^{\operatorname{div}}(\Omega)$.

        \item Assume $w\in H^\Delta(\Omega_w)$, $h\in H^\Delta(\Omega_h)$, then $w,h$ have matching boundary trace and normal derivative, i.e., $${\operatorname{tr}}^{\gamma}(w) ={\operatorname{tr}}^{\gamma}(h)~~\textnormal{and} ~~ {\operatorname{tr}}^{\gamma}_N(\nabla w) = -{\operatorname{tr}}^{\gamma}_N(\nabla h),$$ if and only if $\pi$ is an element of $ H^\Delta(\Omega)$.
    \end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:28:34.924496+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ec2f3ea82bf4f8198bf335da9d9d5cf237900575b076e1a1b95f1e1390906b17","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lemma_revise_2.3 of arXiv:2412.05706, 'Quasi-multiplicativity and regularity for metric graph Gaussian free fields'] {cai2024high}}]
For any $d\ge 3$, there exists $C>0$ such that for any $x_1,x_2,x_3\in \mathbb{Z}^d$ with $\max\{|x_1-x_2|,|x_2-x_3|,|x_3-x_1|\}\ge C$,
