{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:17:04.313201+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f3fd8909c9c24a904b30efcb7b68cce2838c5ee656ef59696a4df08f4a63083d","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem WP_H^1 of arXiv:2606.00325, 'Ergodicity for a class of stochastic Hasegawa-Mima equations with dissipation'] For every $p>2$, $T>0$ and $\phi_0\in L_{\mathcal{F}_0}^{2p}(\Omega, V)$ there exists a pathwise unique solution of (eq:main of the paper) in the sense of (def-sol-H1 of the paper). Moreover $\phi$ is a Markov process in $V$ and it satisfies,  
    \begin{align}
        &\mathbb{E}\left[ \left\| \phi(t)\right\|_V^2 + 2\int_0^t|A^{1/2 }\phi(s)|^2ds\right] =  \mathbb{E}\left[ \left\| \phi_0\right\|_V^2 \right] + \kappa t \\
        &\mathbb{E}\left[ \sup_{t\in [0, T]} \|\phi(t)\|_V^p + \left(\int_0^T |A^{1/2} \phi(t)|^2 dt\right)^{p/2}\right] \lesssim 1
    \end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:14:46.376322+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f4015838b6153fc00f6836c29bf82558acd4f9d51cab98f3482a22d62e9e42ea","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition of arXiv:2608.10415, 'Continuous random variables'] The geometric law $c_p$ has the following properties:
