{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:23:25.634483+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f63fa3986d834f586ea64e80463981a1ca95638c8de2b9b9c4e6402216855c43","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm Inverse3 of arXiv:2609.01350, 'New classes of trace-form permutation polynomials and their compositional inverses'] Let $q=2^m$, $f_2(x)=x+\gamma {\rm Tr}_{q}^{q^2} (c_1 x+ c_2 x^2+ c_3x^3+c_3x^{q+2}+x^{2q-1}),$ where $c_1 \in \mathbb F_{q^2}$, $c_2, c_3  \in \mathbb F_{q}$ and $\gamma \in \mathbb F_{q}^*$ such that $1+\gamma{\rm Tr}_q^{q^2}(c_1)=\gamma$. Then the compositional inverse of $f_2(x)$ is
\begin{equation}
  f_2^{-1}(x)=
  \begin{cases}
    \gamma^{-1}x
      & \text{if } x\in\mathbb F_q,\\[0.8em]
    x+h_2(x)+\dfrac{x^3+x^{3q}}{x^{q+1}}
      & \text{if } x\notin\mathbb F_q,\ \beta=0,\\[0.8em]
    x+h_2(x)+\dfrac{x^3+x^{3q}}{x^{q+1}}+R+\dfrac{\beta}{R}
      & \text{if } x\notin\mathbb F_q,\ \beta\neq0,\ m \text{ odd},\\[0.8em]
    x+h_2(x)+\dfrac{x^3+x^{3q}}{x^{q+1}}
      +\dfrac{\theta^{(q+2)/3}+\theta^{(2q+1)/3}}{\beta}
      & \text{if } x\notin\mathbb F_q,\ m \text{ even},
  \end{cases}
\end{equation}
where
\[
  \beta=h_2(x)^2+(x+x^q)\,h_2(x)
        +\frac{(x+x^q)^6}{x^{2q+2}}
        +\frac{(x+x^q)^4}{x^{q+1}}
        +(x+x^q)^2+x^{q+1},
\]
\[
  R=\bigg(\beta\,(x+x^q)\sum_{i=0}^{(m-1)/2}
      \Big(\frac{\beta}{(x+x^q)^2}\Big)^{4^i}\bigg)^{1/3},
\]
and, in the last case, $\theta\in\mathbb F_{q^2}\setminus\mathbb F_q$ is either
root of $\theta^2+\beta(x+x^q)\theta+\beta^3=0$ (both roots give
the same value). For $m$ even one always has $\beta\neq0$, so the
four cases are exhaustive.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:25:55.316881+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f6413da8b468c2c105856838b40aaae92e29a1726bfe51c0c6ec77b5b8af9114","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 3 ({\cite[Eq.~(3.7, 3.8)) of arXiv:2607.27786, 'Gaussian free field in annulus: \\BPZ equations and crossing probabilities for level lines'] {PeltolaWuGlobalMultipleSLEs}}]

Fix $\kappa\in (0,4]$ and $2N$-polygon $(\Omega; x_1, \ldots, x_{2N})$. 
For $1\le j\le N$, let $\gamma^j$ be chordal $\SLE_{\kappa}$ in $(\Omega; x_j, x_{2N+1-j})$. Let $\PP_N^{(\kappa)}$ be the probability measure on $\bs{\gamma}=(\gamma^1, \ldots, \gamma^N)$ under which the curves are independent. Then the law of rainbow $\SLE_{\kappa}$ in $(\Omega; x_1, \ldots, x_{2N})$ has the same law as $\PP_N^{(\kappa)}$ weighted by the Radon-Nikodym derivative 
\begin{align}
\frac{\prod_{j=1}^{N}\LZtwo(\Omega;x_j,x_{2N+1-j})}{\LZ_{\rainbow}^{(\kappa)}(\Omega;x_1,\ldots,x_{2N})}\one_{\LE_{\emptyset}(\bs{\gamma})}
		\exp\left(\frac{\mathfrak{c}}{2}\blm(\Omega;\gamma^1,\ldots,\gamma^N)\right), 
\end{align}
where $\LE_{\emptyset}(\bs{\gamma}) = \{ \gamma^{j} \cap \gamma^{i}=\emptyset, \, \forall i\neq j\}$ is the event that different curves are disjoint, and $\blm$ is the Brownian loop measure defined in~\eqref{eqn::blm_def}.
In particular, we have 
\begin{align}
	\begin{split}
		\LZ_{\rainbow}^{(\kappa)}(\Omega;x_1,\ldots,x_{2N})
		= \prod_{j=1}^{N}\LZtwo(\Omega;x_j,x_{2N+1-j}) \times
		\E_N^{(\kappa)} \left[
		\one_{\LE_{\emptyset}(\bs{\gamma})}
		\exp\left(\frac{\mathfrak{c}}{2}\blm(\Omega;\gamma^1,\ldots,\gamma^N)\right)
		\right].
	\end{split}
\end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:58:09.039432+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f64579fee7de12a86f06beae3ed9e409357ac7be29a7818d79dc80e87e1973a1","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 21 of arXiv:2606.26496, 'Homeomorphism of the Revuz correspondence under Dynkin class assumptions'] Let ${\sf A}^n,{\sf A}\in\mathbf{A}_c^+$. Denote by $\mu_n$, $\mu$ their Revuz measures in ${\sf S}_0$ for each $n$. Assume that $\mu \in {\sf S}_{D}$ and $\{\mu_n\}$ is of uniformly Dynkin. If
