{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:41:29.041240+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f6fffd6d5817acdbf0d2e59adcd401afd4eb4122fa8f35c207a7b97072de5f1e","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 4 of arXiv:2608.26598, 'Pinching cones for positive isotropic curvature in dimensions seven and eight'] Let $n\ge6$ and $S\in\CPIC\cap\CB(\mathbb R^n)$. Then, for every orthonormal frame
    $\{e_1,\dots,e_n\}$:
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item $S_{ijij}+S_{klkl}\ge0$ whenever $\{i,j\}\cap\{k,l\}=\emptyset$;
        \item $\Scal(S)-2\Ric_{kk}=\sum_{i\ne k}\sum_{j\ne k}S_{ijij}\ge0$ for every $k$;
        \item $\sum_{p\ge3}(S_{1p1p}+S_{2p2p})\ge0$; equivalently
        $\Ric_{11}+\Ric_{22}\ge2S_{1212}$;
        \item $\Ric_{11}+\Ric_{22}+\Ric_{33}+\Ric_{44}\ge2(S_{1212}+S_{3434})\ge0$;
        \item $(n^2-7n+14)S_{1212}+(n-5)(\Ric_{11}+\Ric_{22})+\Scal(S)\ge0$; for $n=8$ this reads
        $22S_{1212}+3(\Ric_{11}+\Ric_{22})+\Scal(S)\ge0$, and together with (3) it gives
        $\Ric_{11}+\Ric_{22}\ge-\frac1{14}\Scal(S)$;
        \item for every $\lambda\in[0,1]$ and every $p\ge5$,
        $S_{1313}+\lambda^2S_{1414}+S_{2323}+\lambda^2S_{2424}-2\lambda S_{1234}
        +(1-\lambda^2)(S_{1p1p}+S_{2p2p})\ge0$;
        \item if $\Scal(S)=0$ then $S=0$; equivalently every nonzero
        $S\in\CPIC\cap\CB(\mathbb R^n)$ has positive scalar curvature.
    \end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:01:15.335450+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f70088c9421e000c944d4b940777298fee1de9a18753e6c54bf955da42cb21e4","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 13 of arXiv:2608.30409, 'Gradient estimates and volume doubling for locally finite weighted graphs with $CD\psi(n,-'] Let $G=\left(V,E\right)$ be a locally finite weighted graph satisfying the $CD\psi\left(n,-K\right)$ condition with $K\geq0$, where $\psi\in C^{1}\left(0,+\infty\right)$ is a concave function with $\psi^{\prime}(1)>0$ and $H_{\psi}<\infty$. Then for any $0<t<s$ and any $x,y,z\in V$, 
\begin{equation*}
    p\left(t,x,y\right)\leq p\left(s,x,z\right)\left(\frac{s}{t}\right)^{\frac{n}{2\psi^\prime \left(1\right)}}\exp\left(\frac{Kn(s-t)}{4\psi^{\prime}(1)}+\frac{\psi^\prime \left(1\right)H_{\psi}\mu_{\max}\left(3+2Ks\right)d^2\left(y,z\right)}{3w_{\min}\left(s-t\right)} \right).
\end{equation*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:44:28.086263+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f700aefee2412c32f1bb8d842c2e2b3c19245157b74cf94f8b2f7c1ee4004e0b","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop 16 of arXiv:2607.13849, 'Rearranged Stochastic Heat Equation with an Entropy Gradient Structure'] For $\delta >0$, 
let $(X_t, \mathscr{J}_t)_{0 \le t \le T}$ be a weak limit in $\mathcal{C}([0,T], \mathcal{H}^{-1}_{\mathrm{Sym}}(\mathbb{S})\times \mathcal{H}^{-(3+\delta)}_{\mathrm{Sym}}(\mathbb{S}))$  of the couple of processes $\{(X_t^{(h)},\mathscr{J}_t(X^{(h)}))_{0 \le t \le T}\}_{ h \in T/{\mathbb N}^*}$ when $h\searrow 0$. 
Let $\phi\in \mathcal{H}^{(3+\delta)}_{\mathrm{Sym}}(\mathbb{S})\cap \mathcal{U}^2(\mathbb{S})$

and $\varepsilon >0$. 
Then, 
with probability one, 
for all $0\leq s<t\leq T$, 
\begin{align}
	\langle \phi, \mathscr{J}_{t}- \mathscr{J}_{s}\rangle \geq  \frac{1}{2}\int_s^t\int_{\mathbb{S}}\frac{\phi'(\mathbf{x})}{E_{\varepsilon}(\mathbf{x})+ D_{\mathbf{x}}{X}_{r}(\mathbf{x})}\mathrm{d}\mathbf{x}\mathrm{d}r,
\end{align}
where $E_\varepsilon(\mathbf{x})$ is given by \eqref{eq:varepsilon}. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:32:19.939452+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f705b44085fb3027b3432ed7ed37fb8891bfc35c66ce27a92b247129f90fe561","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 27 of arXiv:2608.02927, 'Thinning Operation via the Poisson-F\"ollmer Process'] [Variational formula for entropy] 

      Let $(\Omega, \mathcal{F},\mu)$ be a probability space, and let $f : \Omega \rightarrow (0,\infty) $ be a $\mu$--integrable function such that $\int_{\Omega} f \dd \mu = 1.$ Then 
      \begin{displaymath}
          \Ent_{ \mu } (f) = \sup_{g} \Big\{ \int f g \dd \mu - \log \Big( \int e^{g} \dd \mu \Big) \Big\}
      \end{displaymath}
      where the supremum is taken over measurable and bounded functions $g : \Omega \rightarrow \R.$ (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:43:04.795005+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f70654b63c190c0f5e78a0f9a5ddff6f0c429a87fa23d44a505f2d82b314e766","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 17 of arXiv:2605.18198, 'Large deviations of crowding in finite $\beta$-ensembles'] Let $Q\in C^{1,1}_{loc}(\R^2)$ and $\mu_Q$ be the corresponding weighted equllibrium measure. Then there exists a positive constant $C_2$ such that  
  	\[
  	\mu_Q(A)\le C_2m(A), \mbox{ for all $A\subset \mathbb C$}
  	\]
  	where $m$ is the Lebesgue measure on the complex plane.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:04:39.793738+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f708778545930e1031f7025206ddf43f933618b6ceba3e8dc33db73a0e3af60d","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:harmonic-grad of arXiv:2609.02987, 'Tail Likelihood Reinforcement Learning'] Under (lem:tailrl-regularity of the paper), for every $T\geq1$,
\begin{equation}
\nabla_\theta J_{\mathrm{TailRL{}}}^{(T)}(\theta;x)
=
\sum_{k=1}^{T}
\frac{1}{k}
\nabla_\theta\text{Best-of-}k(\theta;x).

\end{equation}
Equivalently,
\begin{equation}
\nabla_\theta J_{\mathrm{TailRL{}}}^{(T)}(\theta;x)
=
\int_0^1
\frac{1-\left(1-p_\theta(x,\tau)\right)^T}
{p_\theta(x,\tau)}
\nabla_\theta p_\theta(x,\tau)
\,d\tau,

\end{equation}
where the ratio is interpreted by continuity as $T$ when $p_\theta(x,\tau)=0$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:17:04.328642+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f70b3853ecc924d03a28a9fed8e8d22ebe42284a3e1953ef66022d2edcbde331","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition eq:kipnisvaradhan of arXiv:2606.01937, 'Stationary fluctuations for an exclusion process with mass and energy conservation'] Let $(X_t)_{t\ge 0}$ be a Markov process evolving on a state-space $\mathscr{E}$, with generator $\mathscr{L}$ and invariant measure $\pi$. Then, there exists a universal constant $C_4>0$ such that for any smooth mean-zero function $f:[0,T]\times\mathscr{E}\longrightarrow\mathbb{R}$, we have 
    \begin{equation*}
        \mathbb{E}_\pi\left[ \sup_{0\le t\le T}\left(\int_0^t f(s,X_s)\,\mathrm{d} s\right)^2\right] \le C_4\int_0^T \|f(t,\cdot )\|_{-1,\pi}^2\,\mathrm{d} t
    \end{equation*}
    where the variational norm $\|\cdot\|_{-1,\pi}$ is defined by
    \begin{equation*}
        \| f\|_{-1,\pi}^2 = \sup_g\left\lbrace 2 \langle f,g\rangle_\pi -\langle g,(-\mathscr{L})g\rangle_\pi \right\rbrace.
    \end{equation*}
    Above, the supremum is taken over all local functions $g:\mathscr{E}\longrightarrow\mathbb{R}$, and $\langle\cdot ,\cdot\rangle_\pi$ stands for the scalar product in $L^2(\pi )$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:49:21.122691+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f70c8752fc283a433a9ce4a013de9c39c5c4afab6fd32f657ddc6bbf8985eec8","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop 24 of arXiv:2608.26642, '2608.26642'] Assume that the pair $(C,f)$ satisfies the Fredholm equation (\ref{main-Fredeq}) with the normalization condition (\ref{main-Frednorm}). Then the function $f$ satisfies the growth condition (\ref{main-SLgrowth}). More precisely, we have
\begin{align}

f(x)= h(x)+C\mp \frac{1}{\P_0[X_1^2]}\int_K yf (y)\,\mu(dy)+o(1)\qquad \text{as}\ x\to \pm \infty.
\end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:05:09.629495+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f70dd960efba586de3a8982fd21e0e6897d4b22c059f4fc1f2c610da991198e9","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem_drift_como_conv of arXiv:2411.03609, 'Asymptotically optimal Wasserstein couplings for the small-time stable domain of attractio'] \noindent\normalfont{(a)} Let $\alpha \in (1,2)$, and $p,\delta>0$ where $p\ne\alpha-1$ and $\delta\ne(\alpha-1)/\alpha$. Then, under Assumptions~(\nameref{asm:S}) \& (\nameref{asm:C}), we have, as $t \downarrow 0$,
    \begin{align*}
        |\bm{\varpi}_{\bm{S}^t}-\bm{\varpi}_{\bm{Z}}|&=\mathcal{O}\big(G_2(t)+t^{1-1/\alpha}G(t)^{-1} 
    +(1+G_1(t))t^{\min\{1-1/\alpha,\delta\}}
    \\
    &\qquad + G(t)^{p}(1+G_1(t))^{1+p}t^{\min\{1-1/\alpha,p/\alpha\}}\big).
    \end{align*}
\noindent\normalfont{(b)} Let $\alpha \in (0,1)$. Then, under Assumptions~(\nameref{asm:S}) \& (\nameref{asm:C}) we have, as $t \downarrow 0$, 
\begin{equation*}
|\bm{\varpi}_{\bm{S}^t}-\bm{\varpi}_{\bm{Z}}|
=\mathcal{O}\big(G_2(t)+t^{p/\alpha}G(t)^{p} +t^{\delta}\big).
\end{equation*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:52:23.576788+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f70e8d9346274738a0dba08cc7e64728eb558861fef8f57615b0745632a38d1e","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma L:balan of arXiv:2602.05317, 'Strong local nondeterminism for stochastic time-fractional\\ slow and fast diffusion equat'] For all $t > 0$, $a \in [b, \infty) \subseteq (0, \infty)$, and $H \in (0,1)$, there exist strictly
  positive constants $C^{(1)}$ and $C^{(2)}$ depending on $t$, $b$, and $\eta$, such that
  \begin{align}
    \frac{C^{(2)}}{t \sqrt{a^2 + t^2}} \int_{\mathbb{R}} \frac{|\tau/t|^{1 - 2H}}{\tau^2 + a^2 + t^2} \;\ensuremath{\mathrm{d} } \tau
    \leq A_{\eta}(t,a) \leq
    \frac{C^{(1)}}{t \sqrt{a^2 + t^2}} \int_{\mathbb{R}} \frac{|\tau/t|^{1 - 2H}}{\tau^2 + a^2 + t^2} \; \ensuremath{\mathrm{d} } \tau.
  \end{align}
  In particular, when $\eta = 0$, inequality~(equation E:balan of the paper) holds for all $a \in (0, \infty)$, with constants $C^{(1)}$ and $C^{(2)}$ depending only on $t$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:36:31.223215+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f70fbe435a238cf294e14edea7a7bd1b4612de892f6efaea69a8664800350892","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 12 (Parametrization of the unique toroidal limit) of arXiv:2609.00501, 'Uniqueness of Finite-Time Varifold Limits for the M\"obius-Invariant Willmore Flow'] Assume the hypotheses of Corollary~\ref{cor:classification} and suppose that
$\Var_T\neq0$ and $\spt\Var_T$ has genus one.  Then there exists a
uniformly conformal bi-Lipschitz homeomorphism
\[
 f\in W^{2,2}(\Sigma,\R^4)\cap W^{1,\infty}(\Sigma,\R^4),
 \qquad
 f\colon\Sigma\longrightarrow M:=\spt\Var_T,
\]
and there are a smooth zero-scalar-curvature, unit-volume metric
$g_{\mathrm{poin}}$ on $\Sigma$ and a function $u\in L^\infty(\Sigma)$
such that
\begin{equation}

 f^*g_{\mathrm{euc}}=e^{2u}g_{\mathrm{poin}}.
\end{equation}
Moreover,
\begin{equation}

 \Var_T=v(M,1),
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}

 \lVert\Var_T\rVert
 =f_\#\mu_{f^*g_{\mathrm{euc}}}
 =\mathcal H^2\llcorner M.
\end{equation}
In particular, formula \eqref{eq:limit-measure} is independent of the sequence
used to construct the parametrization $f$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:52:47.271439+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f710f255b13ebcc1e84354b775629b966bc938d2b6ec605ac61e272e694ac87b","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 8 (Resolution invariance) of arXiv:2609.01116, 'Vector-Carleson, Calder\'on, and Poisson--Atomic Criteria for\\ Generalized Hilbert Operat'] Let $2<p<\infty$ and let $\sigma$ be an $(L,M,p)$-admissible analytic
resolution. Then
\[
 \boxed{
 \|\mathscr S_{\phi,p}^{\sigma}:\ell^p\to H^p\|
 \asymp_{p,L,M}
 \|\mathscr S_{\phi,p}:\ell^p\to H^p\|.}
 \tag{3.10}
\]
For the nonconstant coordinate tails,
\[
\begin{split}
 \|\mathscr S_{\phi,p}^{\sigma,>N}\|
 &\lesssim_{p,L,M}
 \|\mathscr S_{\phi,p}^{>N-L}\|,\\
 \|\mathscr S_{\phi,p}^{>N}\|
 &\lesssim_{p,L}
 \|\mathscr S_{\phi,p}^{\sigma,>N-L}\|,
\end{split}
 \tag{3.11}
\]
where a negative cutoff means the full nonconstant synthesis.
Consequently,
\[
 \|T_\phi\|_{\mathrm e}
