{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T01:47:04.924230+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f85a28d5dfbf99058892e7b8e6925c01ce86b8dbe06f7953969b1ac3afc0e94d","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem of arXiv:2412.02160, 'A functional central limit theorem for weighted occupancy processes of the Karlin model'] For functions $f:[0,1)\to {\mathbb R}$ such that $(a_j)_{j\in{\mathbb N}}\equiv (a_j(f))_{j\in{\mathbb N}}$ satisfy the assumptions in Theorem (thm:2 of the paper), 
we have
\[
\pp{\frac{S_{\floor {nt}}(f) - {\mathbb E} \pp{S_{\floor{nt}}(f)\mid{\mathcal P}}}{n^{\alpha/2}}}_{t\in[0,1]}\stackrel{a.s.w.}\to \pp{\frac{\mathsf s_{\alpha,\theta}}{\Gamma(1-\alpha)}}^{1/2}\pp{\mathcal{Z}_{\alpha}(t)}_{t\in[0,1]}
\]
with respect to ${\mathcal P}$ in $D([0,1])$, where on the right-hand side the process $\mathcal{Z}_{\alpha}$ is as in (equation eq:calZ of the paper) and independent from ${\mathcal P}$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:01:15.318309+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f85a7796d91b143964d724ecf56cba4c92387d1eecb95b42c1f872ee49007bb1","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 2 (Full-path tube closure) of arXiv:2608.30261, 'Estimating Population-Risk Curves Along Nonconvex Gradient Flows from the Training Sample'] \label[lemma]{lem:full-path-tube}
Under \cref{ass:tube}, for every $i\in[n]$ and $\lambda\in\Lambda$ the deleted flow
$\theta_{-i,t}^\lambda$ and the response $d_{i,t}^\lambda$ exist uniquely on
$[0,T]$.
Moreover, for $0\leq t\leq T$,
\begin{equation}
 \max\{\|\theta_{-i,t}^\lambda-\theta_t^\lambda\|,\|d_{i,t}^\lambda\|\}
 \leq(n-1)^{-1}M\phi_\kappa(t)<r_{\rm tube}.
\end{equation}
Consequently, at each time $t$, the line segments joining any two of
$\theta_t^\lambda$, $\theta_{-i,t}^\lambda$, and
$\theta_t^\lambda+d_{i,t}^\lambda$ are contained in
$B(\theta_t^\lambda,r_{\rm tube})$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:34:20.780238+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f85b80cfe3a00c8203982e01080c8652d5e6f828971bb9211b4cca249869bf80","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theo 5 of arXiv:2609.02261, 'Quantization and process'] i)  Let $F\in C^{\infty }(\R^{2n})$
   satisfying for all    multi-indices  $(\alpha , \beta)\in(\N^n)^2$,

\be \Vert \partial _x ^{\alpha }  \partial _{\xi } ^{\beta } F 
\Vert _{L^{\infty } (\R^{2n})} \leq K 
 C^{|\alpha +\beta  |} \sqrt { \alpha ! \beta ! },\ee

  with $K>0$ and $C>0$. If $\lambda < 1/(2C^2)$ then $F\in D_{\lambda } (  \R^{2n})$. 

 ii)  Conversely, if  $F\in D_{\lambda } (  \R^{2n})$ (with $\lambda>0$)
then, for all multi-indices $\alpha\in\N^{2n}$,

   $$  \Vert \partial ^{\alpha} F \Vert _{L^{\infty}( \R^{2n})} 
  \leq K   \sqrt {\alpha ! }
   \Big (\frac  { C }  {\lambda } \Big )^{| \alpha| /2} 
   \Vert  F \Vert _{D_{\lambda}( \R^{2n}) }.$$ (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:04:39.787858+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f85c89663099d713135504d67e02f1ee85b4476e0cc3ca1e322019afe2a5c364","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:tailrl-threshold-reparameterization of arXiv:2609.02987, 'Tail Likelihood Reinforcement Learning'] Define
\begin{equation}
F_w(t)
:=
\int_0^t w(s)\,ds,
\qquad
\widetilde r(x,z):=F_w\!\left(r(x,z)\right).

\end{equation}
Then $F_w$ is a strictly increasing map from $[0,1]$ to $[0,1]$, and
\begin{equation}
J_w(\theta;x)
=
\int_0^1
\log
\Pr_{z\sim\pi_\theta(\cdot\mid x)}
\!\left(\widetilde r(x,z)>u\right)
\,du.

\end{equation}
Thus, weighted TailRL on $r$ is exactly uniform TailRL on the monotone rescaling $\widetilde r=F_w(r)$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:25:39.116533+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f862bdeaa0cf54fd2853b2c0f73183d7c9286d44b3193f1e59a7dea17d955d9c","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def: partition procedure in sle of arXiv:2606.13147, 'Scaling limits of the single-curve interface and outermost loops in the planar random fiel'] Fix a sufficiently small constant $\iota>0$, and set
$M=\iota r^{3\kappa}N.$
We introduce the following partition procedure. 
    We first partite $\Lambda_N$ into $M$-boxes. For each box $\mathsf{B}$, we have two possible \begin{itemize}
        \item If $\sum_{x\in \mathsf{B}}{\operatorname{dist}}(x,\gamma)^{-\frac{1}{4}}\ge M^{\frac{7}{4}}$, then we divide $\mathsf{B}$ into two small boxes $\mathsf{C}_1$ and $\mathsf{C}_2$ such that \begin{equation}
            \max\Big\{\sum_{x\in \mathsf{C}_1}{\operatorname{dist}}(x,\gamma)^{-\frac{1}{4}},\sum_{x\in \mathsf{C}_2}{\operatorname{dist}}(x,\gamma)^{-\frac{1}{4}}\Big\}\le (\frac{1}{2}+\kappa)\cdot\sum_{x\in \mathsf{B}}{\operatorname{dist}}(x,\gamma)^{-\frac{1}{4}}.
        \end{equation}
        \item If $\sum_{x\in \mathsf{B}}{\operatorname{dist}}(x,\gamma)^{-\frac{1}{4}}< M^{\frac{7}{4}}$, then we turn to the next box.
    \end{itemize}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:20:15.481545+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f8630b40c6ce241a5dff402fb60ff19ff2e2c7fe0d9e9739091197feb0162a23","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 18 of arXiv:2509.01797, 'Relation between Wick powers and excursion clusters of the 2D GFF'] For every $\eta>0$,
\begin{displaymath}
\lim_{\varepsilon\to 0}
\E\Big[
\big\Vert
\psi_{n,A}
-
