{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:29:35.091103+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f99a148d44d3b06b13807b63a0f26eb76939e196380f7e3c7d0bd2a72897f894","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn 22 (Test Function $\mathbb{W}_{x}$) of arXiv:2609.03821, 'Condensation and Metastability in the Supercritical Two-Species Zero-Range Process via Res'] Fix $x\in S_{\star}$. For $u,w\ge0$ and $y\in S_{\star}\setminus\{x\}$,
define
\begin{equation}
W_{N}(u,w):=\int^{u+w}_{0}U_{\epsilon}(s)\,{\rm d}s+\omega_{N}(x,y)U_{\epsilon}(u+w)(u-(u+w)\rho_{N}).
\end{equation}
Then, define a function $\mathcal{W}_{x,y}:\Omega_{N}\to\mathbb{R}$
for each $y\in S_{\star}\setminus\{x\}$ as 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{W}_{x,y}(\bm{\eta}):=W_{N}(\mathcal{X}^{1}_{x,y}(\bm{\eta}),\mathcal{X}^{2}_{x,y}(\bm{\eta})).
\end{equation}
Finally, via the partition of unity \eqref{eq:PoU}, the global test
function $\mathbb{W}_{x}:\Omega_{N}\to\mathbb{R}$ is defined as
\begin{equation}
\mathbb{W}_{x}(\bm{\eta}):=\sum_{y\in S_{\star}\setminus\{x\}}\Theta^{x,y}_{\epsilon}\left(\frac{\bm{\eta}}{N}\right)\mathcal{W}_{x,y}(\bm{\eta}).
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:18:54.361766+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f99ba56b17eb51bb568429d0675c7817515779718ba8f12296098eb0989840f8","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem of arXiv:2608.10415, 'Continuous random variables'] The gamma function is given at half-integers by
$$\Gamma(n)\simeq\left(\frac{n}{e}\right)^n\sqrt{\frac{2\pi}{n}}
\quad,\quad\Gamma\left(n+\frac{1}{2}\right)\simeq\left(\frac{n}{e}\right)^n\sqrt{2\pi}$$
and we have the following uniform approximation formula for it,
$$\Gamma\left(\frac{N}{2}\right)\simeq\left(\frac{N}{2e}\right)^{N/2}\sqrt{\frac{4\pi}{N}}$$
valid for $N\in\mathbb N$, in the $N\to\infty$ limit.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:54:34.579116+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f99beccb17c02f49544127f8e49a2b21072d70e69522df72c991c7a4bb145976","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 16 of arXiv:2606.14876, 'High-Order Talagrand and Eldan--Gross Inequalities via Besov-Type Variance Functionals'] For all $J\subseteq [n]$, $f:\cube^n\to \Rbb$, and $t > 0$,
\begin{equation}
    \Dop_J \Pop_t f(x) = \left(\upe^{2t}-1\right)^{-|J|/2} \Exp_{\ybold\sim p_{t,x}} \left[\phi_J^{t,x}(\ybold)f(\ybold)\right],
\end{equation}
where $\phi_J^{t,x}(y) := \prod_{i\in J} \frac{y_i-\upe^{-t} x_i}{\sqrt{1-\upe^{-2t}}}$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:40:59.378943+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f99c88d1aafff91174cb0b2872a15ad68d0d5a14c00cf0f4240bc8a532f9d1b6","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 6 (Nguyen--Squassina \cite{NM18}) of arXiv:2609.03962, '$L^p$ isomorphisms for the fractional Laplacian on $\mathbb{R}^n$ and their application'] Let $s\in(0,1)$, $sp=n$, and $R>0$. Then there exists $C_R>0$ such that
