{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:56:49.286174+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f9cb4b3f7cb2b2023727f30cac281ac6bf6e776fdfae189b7ab595a48b3ff4e5","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 8 (Homogeneous $\alpha$-H\"{o}lder norm) of arXiv:2606.22964, 'Signature McKean--Vlasov stochastic differential equations'] Let $\alpha\in(0,1]$. For any path $\mathbf{x}:[0,T]\rightarrow G^{\lfloor p\rfloor}(\mathbb{R}^k)$, the homogeneous $\alpha$-H\"{o}lder norm of $\mathbf{x}$ is defined as
    \begin{equation*}
        \lVert \mathbf{x}\rVert_{\textup{hom $\alpha$-H\"{o}l};[0,T]} := \sup_{0\leq s<t\leq T}\frac{d_{cc}(\mathbf{x}_s,\mathbf{x}_t)}{|t-s|^\alpha}.
    \end{equation*}
    Then, for any $\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y}:[0,T]\rightarrow G^{\lfloor p\rfloor}(\mathbb{R}^k)$, the homogenous $\alpha$-H\"{o}lder distance between $\mathbf{x}$ and $\mathbf{y}$ is defined as
    \begin{equation*}
        d_{\textup{hom $\alpha$-H\"{o}l};[0,T]}(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y}) := \sup_{0\leq s<t\leq T}\frac{d_{cc}(\mathbf{x}_{s,t},\mathbf{y}_{s,t})}{|t-s|^\alpha},
    \end{equation*}
    where $\mathbf{x}_{s,t}:=\mathbf{x}_s^{-1}\bullet \mathbf{x}_t$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:49:05.413688+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f9ce7556b316d3259fd892dc2d7530ad988389aa8c24bf8bae72e626fbe77e5a","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma of arXiv:2601.07808, 'Supercritical long-range percolation on graphs of polynomial growth: the truncated one-arm'] \label[lemma]{lem:lattice_animal}
    Let $G$ be a transitive graph of polynomial growth with $d \geq 1$, and let $J : G \times G \to \mathbf{R}_+$ be transitive, and suppose that $J(x,y) = \Theta(d_G(x,y))^{-d \alpha}$ with $\alpha > 1$.

    The finite cluster of the origin $K$ has a unique block decomposition $\mathcal{B}(K)$, the block graph $\mathcal{H}(\mathcal{B}(K))$ is connected, and the block decomposition is $\mathcal{B}(K)$-isolated. 

    In particular,

    \begin{equation}
        \mathbf{P}_{\beta}
        \left(
            \#K <\infty 
        \right)
        \leq
        \sum_{\substack{
            b \in \mathbf{N},
            \mathbf{m} \in \mathbf{N}^{b}
            \\
            \mathbf{B} \in \mathcal{A}(b,\mathbf{m})
        }}
        \mathbf{P}_{\beta}
        \left(
            \mathcal{B}(K)
            =
            \mathbf{B},
            \mathcal{H}(\mathbf{B})
            \text{ is connected},
            \mathbf{B}
            \text{ is }
            \mathcal{B}(K)
            \text{-isolated}
        \right).
    \end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:48:25.143147+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f9cef7f65ebbfaba43e66d35ef5cfd0a7d33be621da4950ef7e86057ae3b13cb","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:constant-overshoot of arXiv:2607.04620, 'The Exact Ville Identity\\ \large From the Absorbing Case to the General Law\\ with an App'] Suppose there is a constant $\omega\ge0$ such that, on $\{T_b<\infty\}$, the conditional landing value obeys
\begin{equation}
  \mathbb{E}[M_{T_b}\mid \mathcal{F}_{T_b-1},T_b=n]\le b+\omega
  \quad\text{a.s., for every }n.

\end{equation}
Then $O_b\le\omega$ and $\mathbb{P}(T_b<\infty)\ge(1-D_b-R_b)/(b+\omega)$, with equality throughout when (equation eq:landing-bound of the paper) holds with equality. In particular, if the conditional overshoot is a level-independent constant $\omega$, then $O_b=\omega$ and $\mathbb{P}(T_b<\infty)=(1-D_b-R_b)/(b+\omega)$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:30:52.738939+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f9d15ccbb8d5afd6969ebdf59d41d93587eaaf9d3802f42f8cd4239588d5de73","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 5 of arXiv:2506.10494, 'Geometric Jensen-Shannon Divergence Between Gaussian Measures On Hilbert Space'] [\textbf{Geometric Jensen-Shannon divergence between equivalent Gaussian measures}]

		Let $\mu_i = \Ncal(m_i, C_i) \in \Gauss(\Hcal, \mu_{*})$, $i=0,1$, with $C_i= C_{*}^{1/2}(I-S_i)C_{*}^{1/2}$, $S_i \in \SymHS(\Hcal)_{< I}$. Let $m_{\alpha} \in \Hcal, S_{\alpha} \in \SymHS(\Hcal)_{<I}, C_{\alpha} \in \Sym^{+}(\Hcal) \cap \Tr(\Hcal)$ be as in Theorem \ref{theorem:geometric-interpolation-equivalent-Gaussian-Hilbert-space}. Then
		\begin{align}
			&\JS_{G_{\alpha}}(\mu_0||\mu_1) = (1-\alpha)\KL(\mu_0||\mu_{\alpha}) + \alpha \KL(\mu_1||\mu_{\alpha})
			\\
			& = \frac{(1-\alpha)}{2}||C_{\alpha}^{-1/2}(m_0- m_{\alpha})||^2 - \frac{1-\alpha}{2}\log\dettwo[(I -S_{\alpha})^{-1}
			(I-S_0)]
			\nonumber
			\\
			&\quad +\frac{\alpha}{2}||C_{\alpha}^{-1/2}(m_1- m_{\alpha})||^2 -\frac{\alpha}{2}\log\dettwo[(I -S_{\alpha})^{-1}
			(I-S_1)].
		\end{align}
		In particular, for $S_0, S_1 \in \SymTr(\Hcal)_{< I}$,
		\begin{align}
			\JS_{G_{\alpha}}(\mu_0||\mu_1) &=\frac{(1-\alpha)}{2}||C_{\alpha}^{-1/2}(m_0- m_{\alpha})||^2 +\frac{\alpha}{2}||C_{\alpha}^{-1/2}(m_1- m_{\alpha})||^2
			\nonumber
			\\
			&\quad - \frac{1}{2}\log\frac{\det(I-S_0)^{1-\alpha}\det(I-S_1)^{\alpha}}{\det(I-S_{\alpha})}
			\nonumber
			\\
			&\quad  +\frac{1}{2}\trace[(I-S_{\alpha})^{-1}(S_{\alpha}-(1-\alpha)S_0- \alpha S_1)].

		\end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:35:10.793596+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f9d1f38beef83aff10519c3fa19aa53b55fdb4f4136d17e6e293a7807c907bc8","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop 27 of arXiv:2604.00635, 'Large deviations of the periodic Toda chain'] Let $V$ satisfy \ref{potentialh1} and \ref{potentialh2} and fix $\gamma > 0$.

Then there is a constant $C >0$ (which may depend on $\gamma$) such that, for $N$ sufficiently large, the upper bound holds

\begin{align}

\mc{I}(\bs{\la}^+_N ; \veps_N) \leq \mathrm{e}^{CN^{1- \frac{1}{4}}} \sqrt{\frac{ \mc{T}(\boldsymbol{\lambda}^+_N) \mc{T}(\boldsymbol{\lambda}^-_N)}{\Delta(\boldsymbol{\lambda}_N^+) \Delta(\boldsymbol{\lambda}_N^-)} } \cdot  \prod_{j=1}^N \max\{ \ln |\lambda_j^+ - \lambda_j^-|^{-1}   , \gamma N \} \;.

