{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:27:11.446248+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fab3bef912e1be9016a67608a590a415ccf5a49d8910d50b6e3b891f0646ab58","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma l: fourier smooth multiplication of arXiv:2605.22803, 'Persistence of asymptotic variance under transport:\\ from hyperfluctuation to stealthy hy'] Let $f\in L^1(\R, \C)$ be Fourier-smooth with exponent $p\in[-1, \infty)$, and define $g:\R^d\to\C$ by
    \begin{equation}
        g(x) := \prod_{j=1}^d f(x_j), \quad x\in\R^d.
    \end{equation}
    Then $g$ is Fourier-smooth with exponent $p-(d-1)$.
    \begin{proof}
        Since $f$ is Fourier-smooth with exponent $p$, there is a $c>0$ such that
        \begin{equation*}
            |\hat{f}(k)| \leq c \frac{1}{(1+ |k|)^\frac{1+p}{2}}, \quad k\in\R.
        \end{equation*}
        Therefore,
        \begin{equation*}
            |\hat{g}(k)| \leq c^d \prod_{j=1}^d \frac{1}{(1+|k_j|)^\frac{1+p}{2}} \leq c^d\frac{1}{(1+\|k\|_\infty)^\frac{1+p}{2}} \leq c^d\frac{1}{(1+\|k\|)^\frac{d+p-(d-1)}{2}}, \quad k\in\R^d,
        \end{equation*}
        which shows the first part of the asserted Fourier-smoothness of $g$.

        For the second part, we can calculate that, for $r>0$,
        \begin{align*}
            \int_{B_2^c} \sup_{s\in B_1} |\hat{g}(r(k+s))|^2\, dk &= \int_{B_2^c} \sup_{s\in B_1} \prod_{j=1}^d|\hat{f}(r(k_j+s_j))|^2\, dk\nonumber\\
            &\leq d \int_{B_2^c} \I\{k_d\leq 2\} \sup_{s\in B_1} \prod_{j=1}^d|\hat{f}(r(k_j+s_j))|^2\, dk\nonumber\\
            &\quad \ \ + \bigg(\int_{(-2,2)^c}\sup_{s\in(-1,1)}|\hat{f}(r(k+s))|^2\, dk\bigg)^d\nonumber\\
            &\leq 4cd \int_{B_2^c}  \sup_{s\in B_1} \prod_{j=1}^{d-1}|\hat{f}(r(k_j+s_j))|^2\, dk\nonumber\\
            &\quad \ \ + \bigg(\int_{(-2,2)^c}\sup_{s\in(-1,1)}|\hat{f}(r(k+s))|^2\, dk\bigg)^d.
        \end{align*}
        Note that $B_2$ in the second-to-last integral does not refer to the ball in $\R^d$ but in $\R^{d-1}$. By the assumption that $f$ is Fourier-smooth with exponent $p$, the second summand of the bound is $O(r^{-(d+dp)})$ as $r\to\infty$, and since $p\geq-1$, this fact also implies that it is $O(r^{-(d+p-(d-1))})$. For the first summand of the bound, we can use induction to see that it also is \linebreak $O(r^{-(1+p)})=O(r^{-(d+p-(d-1))})$, which concludes the proof.
    \end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:37:25.925602+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fab64729d7a9aaf6d84d7bd77f8084dd56d5762245bb2d4cf9f3904ca99a628a","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 29 of arXiv:2608.29585, '2608.29585'] The interpolation-basis filtration on $\cZ_{n,\mathrm{fin}}$ is
separated, and for every $m\ge0$ there is a canonical isomorphism
\begin{equation}
 \cZ_{n,\mathrm{fin}}/\cZ_{n,\mathrm{fin}}^{(m)}
 \cong
 \bigoplus_{\substack{\ell(\lambda)\le n\\|\lambda|\le m}}
 (R_{\mathrm{ev}}/I_m)\sigma_\lambda.
\end{equation}
Consequently, taking inverse limits identifies
\begin{equation}
 \widehat\cZ_n^{\mathrm{BG}}
 \cong
 \prod_{\ell(\lambda)\le n}
 \widehat R_{\mathrm{ev}}^{\mathrm{BG}}\sigma_\lambda
\end{equation}
as topological modules.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:33:21.470979+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fabb3efa4c1f76b0953d2b4b9b86850df88f8542407627ac26a53a6738462f01","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn def: TruncatedPageRank of arXiv:2606.19583, 'Power-law hypothesis and (un)fairness of PageRank on undirected multi-type PAMs'] {\normalfont \rmfamily} 
We define the \textbf{truncated undirected PageRank on the directed multi-type PAM} $\vec{G}_n$ by
\begin{equation*}
R^{1,\mathrm{dirTr}}_v(\vec{G}_n):=c(1-c)\sum_{\substack{u \in V(\vec{G}_n)\\u \rightarrow v}}\frac{1}{m_{X_u}+d_u^-},    \quad v \in \vec{G}_n,
\end{equation*}
where $u \rightarrow v$ indicates that the sum runs over all vertices $u$ that have an edge directed to $v$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:32:34.793787+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fabda735d3b2a9d86651a5d324676c45947e77d3f87c0c9a8dddc7feb5734f77","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:criticalh of arXiv:2411.08295, 'Improving the convergence of Markov chains via permutations and projections'] Let $P_\beta$ be the transition matrix of the MH chain with ergodic proposal $N$ and target distribution $\pi_\beta$, and $Q \in \mathcal{I}(\pi_\beta) \cap \mathcal{L}$. We have
	\begin{itemize}
		\item(Similarity preserves critical height) \begin{align*}
			h(P_\beta) &= h(Q P_\beta Q).
		\end{align*}

		\item(Optimality of $\alpha = 1/2$) For $\alpha \in [0,1]$, 
		\begin{align*}
			h(\overline{(P_\beta)}_\alpha(Q)) \leq h(P_\beta).
		\end{align*}
		In particular, 
		\begin{align*}
			\min_{\alpha \in [0,1]} h(\overline{(P_\beta)}_\alpha(Q)) = h(\overline{(P_\beta)}_{1/2}(Q)).
		\end{align*}
	\end{itemize} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:32:29.000721+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fabf555d4620cea3e5905936abbf926ecffa5e56c7e3de442c31f94447f4ebf4","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem CM mom LB of arXiv:2608.25471, 'Exact Hausdorff measures and fine geometry of collisions of independent Markov processes'] Fix $x_0 \in M$, $r \in (0, 1 \wedge r_*)$, and $n, N \in \mathbb{N}$ with $2n \leq N$ and $c_1 Nr^{d^{\mathrm{min}}_\mathrm{w}} < 1 \wedge t_*$.
  It then holds that 
  \begin{equation} 
    \sup_{\bm{x} \in M^k}
    \hat{E}_{\bm{x}}\!\left[\Pi_{c_1 N r^{d^{\mathrm{min}}_\mathrm{w}}}(B(x_0,r))^n\right]
    \leq
    \begin{cases}
      n! \bigl( \tilde{c}_1 r^{d_{\mathrm{cp}}} \bigr)^n,& \text{if}\quad \hat{d}_{\mathrm{s}} > 2,\\
      n! \bigl( \tilde{c}_1 r^{d_{\mathrm{f}}} \log(N/n) \bigr)^n,& \text{if}\quad \hat{d}_{\mathrm{s}} = 2,
    \end{cases}
  \end{equation}
  and 
  \begin{equation} 
    \inf_{\bm{x} \in \hat{B}_*(x_0, r)}
    \hat{E}_{\bm{x}}\!\left[\Pi_{c_1 N r^{d^{\mathrm{min}}_\mathrm{w}}}(B(x_0,r))^n\right]
    \geq
    \begin{cases}
      n! \bigl( \tilde{c}_2 r^{d_{\mathrm{cp}}} \bigr)^n,& \text{if}\quad \hat{d}_{\mathrm{s}} > 2,\\
      n! \bigl( \tilde{c}_2 r^{d_{\mathrm{f}}} \log(N/n) \bigr)^n,& \text{if}\quad \hat{d}_{\mathrm{s}} = 2.
    \end{cases}
  \end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:48:45.304505+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fabf8a5593a90b972953f6c1bdb570d2720dcb30a4511ba6579f819c52157025","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem \cite{FNRW} of arXiv:2411.14818, 'Scaling limits of solitons in the Box-Ball system'] Let $X_{k}(n)$ be the position of the leftmost component of a tagged $k$-soliton at time $n$ and ${\mu}$ be a probability measure on $\{0,1\}^{{\mathbb{Z}}}$ satisfying the following. 
                \begin{itemize}
                    \item ${\mu}(\Omega) = 1$. 
                    \item ${\mu}$ is an invariant measure of the BBS, {namely $T{\mu}={\mu}$.}
                    \item ${\mu}$ is a shift-ergodic measure. 
                \end{itemize}
            Assume that for some $k \in {\mathbb{N}}$, $k$-solitons exist with positive probability under ${\mu}$. Then, there exists some $v^{\mathrm{eff}}_{k} =v^{\mathrm{eff}}_k({\mu}) > 0$ such that 
                \begin{align}
                    \lim_{n \to \infty} \frac{X_{k}(n)}{n} = v^{\mathrm{eff}}_{k} \quad {\mu}\text{-a.s.}
                \end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:51:30.070034+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fac100e6b7efab4e85b5e081c3af0c04b1974e9cb8d08fe06ce33fda96f9ed72","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 19 of arXiv:2606.01742, 'Large deviation principles for SPDEs with locally Lipschitz coefficients'] (\cite[Proposition 3.2]{BEM10}) Assume that $\sup_{0\le t\le T}\sup_{x\in\mathbb R}\mathbb E(|V(t,x)|^p)$ is finite for some $p$ large enough. Then,  we have
\begin{itemize}
  \item[(i)] For each $x\in\mathbb R$ a.s., the mapping $t\mapsto U(t,x)$ is  $\beta_1$-H\"older continuous for $0<\beta_1<1/4$.
  \item[(ii)] For each $t\in[0,T]$ a.s., the mapping $x\mapsto  U(t,x)$ is $\beta_2$-H\"older continuous for $0<\beta_2< 1/2$.
\end{itemize} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:33:46.768207+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fac6a86fd8f29e67f5468ffd299014d6b3e017121216bb2659ef7a06833f9169","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 19 of arXiv:2603.24616, 'Pinning in non-critical half-space geometric last passage percolation'] Fix a set $\Sigma = \llbracket 1, N \rrbracket$ with $N \in \mathbb{N} \cup \{\infty\}$ and $T_0, T_1\in \mathbb{Z}$ with $T_0 \leq T_1$. A $\Sigma$-indexed geometric line ensemble $\mathfrak{L} : \Sigma \times \llbracket T_0, T_1 \rrbracket \rightarrow \mathbb{Z}$ is said to satisfy the {\em interlacing Gibbs property} if it is interlacing, meaning that $L_i \succeq L_{i+1}$ for all $i \in \llbracket 1, N-1 \rrbracket$, and for any finite $K = \llbracket k_1, k_2 \rrbracket \subset \llbracket 1, N - 1 \rrbracket$ and $a,b \in \llbracket T_0, T_1 \rrbracket$ with $a < b$ the following holds.  Suppose that $f, g$ are two increasing paths drawn in $\{ (r,z) \in \mathbb{Z}^2 : a \leq r \leq b\}$ and $\vec{x}, \vec{y} \in \mathfrak{W}_k$ with $k=k_2-k_1+1$ are such that $\mathbb{P}(A) > 0$, where $A$ denotes the event $$A =\{ \vec{x} = ({L}_{k_1}(a), \dots, {L}_{k_2}(a)), \vec{y} = ({L}_{k_1}(b), \dots, {L}_{k_2}(b)), L_{k_1-1} \llbracket a,b \rrbracket = f, L_{k_2+1} \llbracket a,b \rrbracket = g \},$$
where if $k_1 = 1$ we adopt the convention $f = \infty = L_0$. Then, writing $k = k_2 - k_1 + 1$, we have for any $\{ B_i \in \Omega(a, b, x_i , y_i) \}_{i = 1}^k$ that
\begin{equation}
\mathbb{P}\left( L_{i + k_1-1}\llbracket a,b \rrbracket = B_{i} \mbox{ for $i \in \llbracket 1, k \rrbracket$} \, \vert  A \, \right) = \mathbb{P}_{\ice, \operatorname{Geom}}^{a,b, \vec{x}, \vec{y}, f, g} \left( \cap_{i = 1}^k\{ Q_i = B_i \} \right),
\end{equation}
where $\mathfrak{Q} = \{Q_i\}_{i = 1}^k$ has law $\mathbb{P}_{\ice, \operatorname{Geom}}^{a,b, \vec{x}, \vec{y}, f, g}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:48:25.155354+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_facfb490385a4669c278c56f7f1150756286de340bb5421d92de1685584d0187","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lm:interp of arXiv:2607.05345, 'The local product condition implies cutoff'] Fix two measures $\mu,\pi\in\mathcal {P}({\mathbb{X}})$ with $\mathrm{d}_{\textsc{tv}}(\mu,\pi)<1$. Then, there is a   measure $\nu\in\mathcal {P}({\mathbb{X}})$ which is absolutely continuous w.r.t. both $\mu$ and $\pi$, such that
		\begin{eqnarray*}
			\left\|\frac{\mathrm{d} \nu}{\mathrm{d} \mu}\right\|_\infty\vee\ \ \left\|\frac{\mathrm{d}\nu}{\mathrm{d}\pi}\right\|_\infty	 & \leq & \frac{1}{1-\mathrm{d}_{\textsc{tv}}(\mu,\pi)}.
		\end{eqnarray*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:38:17.800119+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fad4524abdd4f366e50387c4198d06595439fb4f83a62b93f1536cc816116eb9","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm: main of arXiv:2510.05850, 'Three-point connectivity constant for $q$-state Potts spin clusters'] For the continuum fuzzy Potts model with $q \in (1,4)$, $\kappa = 4\arccos(-\sqrt{q}/2)/\pi\in(\frac{8}{3},4)$, and parameter $r=1/q$, we have
\begin{align}
    R(q)=C(q) \cdot {C_{\beta=\frac{2}{\sqrt{\kappa}}}^{\rm ImDOZZ}\mathopen{}\mathclose\bgroup\originalleft(\frac{1}{4\beta} - \frac{\beta}{2},\frac{1}{4\beta} - \frac{\beta}{2},\frac{1}{4\beta} - \frac{\beta}{2}\aftergroup\egroup\originalright)},\quad \text{where }C(q)=\sqrt{\kappa/2} \cdot\frac{\sin(\kappa\pi/2)}{\sin(4\pi/\kappa)}.
\end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:30:14.221793+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fadb9efbdb90777788491936aa75a76ab244901abbfbcba21ebf6397897ccd35","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:der-mart-lower of arXiv:2608.20537, 'Persistent anisotropy in multi-dimensional\\ branching Brownian motion'] For all $\underline{c} \in (0, 1]$, there exists $t_{\textnormal{max}} \leq -1$ such that the following holds.
  For all $\mathfrak{t} \leq t_{\textnormal{max}}$, there exists $\underline{T}(\mathfrak{t}) > \abs{\mathfrak{t}}$ and a function $\underline{u}(t,x; \mathfrak{t}) \geq 0$ defined where $t \in [-\underline{T}(\mathfrak{t}), \mathfrak{t}]$ and $x \geq 2 \log \left |t \right|$ satisfying the following properties:
  \begin{enumerate}[label = \textnormal{(\roman*)},itemsep = 2pt]
  \item $\underline{u} = 0$ where $x \geq \left |t \right|$;

  \item

    $\underline{u} \leq \underline{c} \mathrm{e}^{-x}$ where $t = -\underline{T}$ or $x = 2 \log \left |t \right|$;   

  \item $\underline{u}$ is a subsolution of (equation eq:ancient of the paper);

  \item $\partial_x \underline{u} \leq 0$; and

  \item for all $x \in [4 \log \abs{\mathfrak{t}}, \abs{\mathfrak{t}}/4]$,
    \begin{equation}

      \underline{u}(\mathfrak{t}, x; \mathfrak{t}) \geq 2^{-8} \underline{c} x \mathrm{e}^{-x}.
    \end{equation}
  \end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:33:01.758744+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fae166bf50a3272f99228836b4f177f739dfee9a73f9eca5d56e2f8efae855f5","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 6 of arXiv:2605.14254, '2605.14254'] The law of the supremum of $\xi^{\lambda,z}$ up to time $\sigma_z^{\lambda}$ is the same as the law of the supremum of $z+B^{-\lambda}$, i.e.,
		\begin{equation}
			\sup\limits_{t\leq \sigma_z^{\lambda}} \xi_t^{\lambda,z} \overset{\text{law}}{=} \sup\limits_{t\geq 0} (z+B_t^{-\lambda}).
		\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:33:46.772018+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fae4ccd0f76806ed9cb79dcc1226bb990bf8abba29dfc932245a6e3ba844447c","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 51 of arXiv:2603.24616, 'Pinning in non-critical half-space geometric last passage percolation'] Assume the same notation as in Lemma \ref{Lem.PrelimitKernelBot} with $\mathcal{T} \subset (0, \infty)$, and recall the contours $\mathcal{C}_z^{\varphi}$ from Definition \ref{Def.S1Contours}. Fix $x_{\infty},y_{\infty} \in \mathbb{R}$, $s,t \in \mathcal{T}$ and sequences $x_N \in \Lambda_s(N)$, $y_N \in \Lambda_{t}(N)$ such that $\lim_{N \rightarrow \infty} x_N = x_{\infty}$ and $\lim_{N \rightarrow \infty} y_N = y_{\infty}$. Then, we have the following limits.
\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
&\lim_{N \rightarrow \infty}  (1-c)^{-2}\sigma_1^{-2}N^{-2/3} I^N_{11}(s,x_N;t,y_N) = I^{\infty}_{11}(s,x_{\infty};t,y_{\infty}) \mbox{, where } I^{\infty}_{11}(s,x;t,y) \\
& = \frac{1}{(2\pi \im)^{2}}\int_{\mathcal{C}^{\pi/3}_{\sigma_1}} dz\int_{\mathcal{C}^{\pi/3}_{\sigma_1}} dw e^{z^3/3 + w^3/3 - f_1 s z^2 - f_1 t w^2 - xz - yw  } \frac{z-w  }{zw (z + w)},
\end{split}
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
&\lim_{N\rightarrow \infty} I^N_{12}(s,x_N;t,y_N) = I^{\infty}_{12}(s,x_{\infty};t,y_{\infty}) , \mbox{ where }  I^{\infty}_{12}(s,x;t,y) \\
&= \frac{1}{(2\pi \im)^{2}}\int_{\mathcal{C}^{\pi/3}_{\sigma_1}} dz\int_{\mathcal{C}^{2\pi/3}_{-\sigma_1}} dw e^{z^3/3 - w^3/3 - f_1 s z^2 + f_1 t w^2 - xz + yw  } \frac{z+w }{2z(z-w) },
\end{split}
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
&\lim_{N \rightarrow \infty} (1-c)^{2}\sigma_1^{2}N^{2/3} I^N_{22}(s,x_N;t,y_N) =  I^{\infty}_{22}(s,x_{\infty};t,y_{\infty}) , \mbox{ where }  I^{\infty}_{22}(s,x;t,y)  \\
& = \frac{1}{(2\pi \im)^{2}}\int_{\mathcal{C}^{2\pi/3}_{-\sigma_1}} dz\int_{\mathcal{C}^{2\pi/3}_{-\sigma_1}} dw e^{-z^3/3 - w^3/3 + f_1 s z^2 + f_1 t w^2 + xz + y w  } \frac{z-w}{4(z+w) },
\end{split}
\end{equation}
\begin{equation} 
\lim_{N \rightarrow \infty} R^N_{12}(s,x_N;t,y_N) = R^{\infty}_{12}(s,x_{\infty};t,y_{\infty}) ,\mbox{ where } R^{\infty}_{12}(s,x;t,y) = \frac{- {\bf 1}\{s < t\}}{\sqrt{4\pi f_1 (t-s)}} \cdot e^{-\frac{(y - x)^2}{4 f_1 (t-s)}}. 
\end{equation}
In addition,   we have 
\begin{equation}
\lim_{N \rightarrow \infty} (1-c)^{2}\sigma_1^{2}N^{2/3} R^N_{22}(s,x_N;t,y_N) = R^{\infty}_{22}(s,x_{\infty};t,y_{\infty}),
\end{equation} 
where
\begin{equation}
R^{\infty}_{22}(s,x;t,y)=-\frac{1}{4\pi\im}\int_{\mathcal{C}^{2\pi/3}_{-\sigma_1}} dw \cdot w e^{f_1 (s + t)w^2+w(y - x )  } . 
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:09:49.780988+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fae5f196a8d40e382a7a10dc1a917283a6e80a4bccc5e1deb7fd8167ef0f6836","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm 2 of arXiv:2609.03054, 'The classes of bivariate Schur and Herglotz matrix-valued rational functions: realizations'] Let $F\in\Cmats{m}{n}(z_1,z_2)$ be a rational  matrix valued function. Then the following conditions are equivalent:
\begin{enumerate}[\rm (i)]
\item $F$ has no poles in $\RHPtwo$, and $F(z_1,z_2)$ is a contraction for any $z_1,z_2\in\RHP$;
\item $F$ admits a realization of the form
\begin{equation}
F(z_1,z_2)= D+ C(z_1P_1+z_2P_2+A)^{-1}B,
\end{equation}
where the block matrix $\matr{A&B\\C&D} \in\Cmats{(k+m)}{(k+n)}$ is such that
\begin{equation}

\matr{
A+A^*- C^*C & C^*D+ B \\ D^* C+ B^*  & I - D^* D
} \geq 0,
\end{equation}
$P_1,P_2\in \sqmat{k}$ are positive semidefinite and $P_1+P_2=I_k$.
\end{enumerate}
Moreover, if any of the above conditions holds, then
the limit $\lim_{t\to\infty}F(t,t)$ exists.
Additionally, if $F\in\mathbb R^{m\times n }(z_1,z_2)$ then the matrices $A,B,C,D,P_1,P_2$ in {\rm (ii)} may be chosen as real matrices.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:59:24.049542+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fae6b439652d83836bd66478d4cfef7ad0302b528b618977285b762fdab250df","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:radial-neighbour of arXiv:2608.30831, 'Sharp discrete Hardy constants in dimensions three and four\\ and strict upper bounds from'] Let $H:\mathbb N_0\to\mathbb R$, let $n\in\mathbb Z^N$, and put $s=|n|^2$.  Define
\begin{equation}
 \mu_r(n):=\#\{j\in\{1,\ldots,N\}: |n_j|=r\},\qquad r\in\mathbb N_0.
\end{equation}
Then
\begin{equation}
 \sum_{m\sim n}H(|m|^2)
 =2\mu_0(n)H(s+1)
 +\sum_{r\ge1}\mu_r(n)
   \bigl[H(s+1-2r)+H(s+1+2r)\bigr].
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:28:53.655243+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fae6c0d8b3e3adaafd28ea3262c473db4e2298ae86495905c6eb07c66ed2c85a","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 7 of arXiv:2605.13518, 'The inertial It\^{o} drift and its applications to particle collision'] For every $p\geq 1$ and $T>0$, we have	
\begin{equation}
\sup_{\e\in\,(0,1)}\mathbb{E}\sup_{s \in\,[0,T]}\vert x_{\epsilon}(s)\vert^p \leq c_{p, T}.
\end{equation}
Moreover, the family $\{\mathcal{L}(x_{\e})\}_{\e \in\,(0,1)}$ is tight in $C([0,T];\mathbb{R}^d)$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:32:19.938045+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_faec2c7cead59253e2d4f62a4aa500caf87246b46cd97f4817987d426b200057","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 23 of arXiv:2608.02927, 'Thinning Operation via the Poisson-F\"ollmer Process'] (Equality cases of Thinning Lemma). Let $X$ be a random variable with density $f$ with respect to $\pi_{T}.$ Assume that $f : \N \rightarrow ( 0, \infty)$ satisfies $H( X | \pi_{T}) < \infty.$ Then, equality holds in the Thinning Lemma, i.e.,
    \begin{displaymath}
        H( T_{a}(X) | \pi_{aT}) = a H(X | \pi_{T} )
    \end{displaymath}
    for some $a \in (0,1)$ if and only if $f(k)=e^{\beta k + \gamma}$ for some $\beta ,\gamma \in \R$ (that is $X$ is Poisson distributed).","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:58:01.574380+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_faec3cd7a2d69cb3672036fb3a9c61f90ba9ab93c83335fd5d8264e0dd37b76d","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 48 of arXiv:2608.27307, '2608.27307'] Under Assumption~\ref{ass:basic}, suppose that $E_{+,0}$ is a regular right edge for $\Pi_0$ in the sense of Definition~\ref{def:reg_r_edge}. There is a unique critical point $\zeta_t$ satisfying $\zeta_t-E_{+,0}\sim t^2$. Moreover,
        \begin{align}
                \Phi_t'(\zeta_t)  & =0,\qquad m_0'(\zeta_t)\sim t^{-1},\qquad -m_0''(\zeta_t)\sim t^{-3}, \nonumber   \\
                \Phi_t''(\zeta_t) & \sim t^{-2},\qquad \Phi_t(\zeta_t)=E_+, \qquad c_{\rm edge}       =\frac1\pi\frac{m_0'(\zeta_t)}{a_t(\zeta_t)^2}\sqrt{\frac{2}{\Phi_t''(\zeta_t)}}.

        \end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:18:19.203327+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_faf0f84b3cc12a3ee9a566c84f442830699a5554dfe68e8618d20d4d6f220229","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 19 of arXiv:2608.28964, '2608.28964'] For every $T>0$, there exists a constant $C>0$ such that for any $f\in L^2(0,T;L^2(D;\R^2))$, the solution $v$ of \eqref{eq l2} satisfies
	\begin{align*}
	\|v\|_{L^\infty(0,T;\mathcal H^1)}\leq C\exp\left(C\left(\|g\|_{L^\infty((0,T)\times D)}^2+\|\partial_tg\|_{L^2(0,T;L^4(D))}\right)\right)\|f\|_{L^2(0,T;L^2(D))}.
	\end{align*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:28:33.706680+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_faf7368cac91d9270035e4b10770017e9879573d61630eadee4acef9e2afdf05","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem lA2 of arXiv:2504.07255, 'A Tail-Respecting Splitting Numerical Scheme\\ for L\'evy-Driven SDEs With Superlinear Dri'] Let $r\in[2,\infty)$. Then, for any $\gamma\in [0,1]$
\begin{equation}\begin{aligned}

