{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:51:33.322827+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fe2aca9ffa960b1bc048c6d95ff72c475ac9f7b115603509dbe63357cd4c31ce","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma eq:canonical-tensoring of arXiv:2608.28504, 'Global $\bm\infty$-categories and global Thom spectra'] Let $S\subset T$ be any cleft, and let ${\mathcal{C}}$ be any pointed $S$-cocomplete $T$-$\infty$-category. Then there exists a unique $S$-bilinear bifunctor 
    \begin{equation}
        {-}\otimes{-}\colon\ul\Spc_{S\triangleright T,*}\times{\mathcal{C}}\to{\mathcal{C}}
    \end{equation} 
    such that $1_+\otimes{-}$ is the identity of ${\mathcal{C}}$.
    \begin{proof}
        Passing to adjuncts, the claim translates to saying that there is a unique $S$-cocontinuous $T$-functor $\theta\colon{\mathcal{C}}\to\ul{\textup{Fun}}_T^\text{$S$-cc}(\ul\Spc_{S\triangleright T,*},{\mathcal{C}})$ such that postcomposing with $\smash{\ev_{1^+}\colon{\textup{Fun}}_T^\text{$S$-cc}(\ul\Spc_{S\triangleright T,*},{\mathcal{C}})\to{\mathcal{C}}}$ recovers the identity. But $\ev_{1^+}$ is an equivalence by Proposition~(prop:univ-prop-*-cc-param of the paper), so this is obvious.
    \end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:57:28.735208+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fe2eae310b460ba5415c80f4b3a3f56838d42587e24579f6f56311c1fd55f6ab","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 1 ({[\citealp[Theorem~5.2) of arXiv:2609.01268, 'Resultant multiplicity via projective degrees\\ and applications to tensor eigenvalues'] {Roy-13};
 \citealp[Theorem~0.1]{Ghidelli-19}]}]

    Let $\ff=(f_1,f_2,\dots,f_n)$ be a system of $n$ homogeneous forms in $n$ variables of degree $d$ whose projective zero scheme has degree $N$.
    Then, Macaulay's resultant vanishes at $\ff$ with multiplicity at least $N$:\begin{equation}

    \mu(\ff)\geq N \quad \text{if} \quad f_1,\dots,f_n \text{ have }N\text{ projective zeros, counted with multiplicity.}
    \end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

