{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:25:12.199334+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ff9e0332e0550f7420da859dfeacbb8ae8856f73fc0920bc57f57adafe4c81b8","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem 54 (\hspace{-0.05em}{{\cite[Proof of Proposition~2) of arXiv:2510.22783, 'Universality of Cutoff for Riffle Shuffling'] {lalley2000rate}}}]

    For all shuffle process with pile size $\vec\bn$, suppose that there exists $H(\vec\bn)\subset [N]$ such that (with $E(H)$ the subgraph of the path graph induced by vertex set $H$):
		\begin{equation}

		\begin{aligned}
			|H(\vec\bn)|&\geq N^{1/2} \\
			|\partial H(\vec\bn)|&\leq 2|H|^{1/2}\\
			\mathbb P\left[|E(G)\cap E(H(\vec\bn))|\geq |H(\vec\bn)|^{\frac{1}{2}+\delta}\right]&\to 1.
		\end{aligned}
		\end{equation}
	Then if $\nu \sim \bP(\vec\bn, K)$ and $K\leq (\widetilde C_{\mu}-\eps) \log N$, we have
	\begin{equation}

        \nu\Big(\#\{i\in H: \sigma(i)<\sigma (i+1)\}\leq |H(\vec\bn)|/2+|H(\vec\bn)|^{1/2+{\delta/2}}\Big)
        \to 0,
    \end{equation}  
        where the limit is uniform for all $\vec\bn$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:56:37.647820+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ffa1cf7c9ef5f13f4ded88d314d138d5dfffb7163e2f9c5981649b8c1c7d76b2","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Def Surface tension of arXiv:2608.18045, 'Supercritical sharpness for the random cluster representation of real-valued spin models'] Let $L,M\geq 1$ and $\beta>0$. The surface tension of the model on $\mathcal{R}(L,M)$ at inverse temperature $\beta$ is defined as
\begin{equation}
    \boldsymbol{\tau}^{L,M}(\beta):=-\frac{1}{L^{d-1}}\log  \frac{\mathbf{Z}^{+,-}_{\mathcal{R}(L,M),\beta}}{\mathbf{Z}^{+,+}_{\mathcal{R}(L,M),\beta}}.
\end{equation}
Moreover, we also define
\begin{equation}
    \boldsymbol{\tau}^L(\beta):=\liminf_{M\rightarrow \infty}\boldsymbol{\tau}^{L,M}(\beta), \qquad \boldsymbol{\tau}(\beta):=\liminf_{L\rightarrow \infty}\boldsymbol{\tau}^{L}(\beta).
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:32:58.363334+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ffa6df470fa637ffd81bb35ec6964babef6b7e7f7e55c86ba221050ce066d5ad","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 1 of arXiv:2506.19273, 'A large deviation view of \emph{stationarized} fully lifted blirp interpolation'] Let vectors $\m = [\m_1 ]$, $\p=[\p_0,\p_1,\p_2]$,  and $\q=[\q_0,\q_1,\q_2]$ be such that  $\p_0\geq \p_1\geq \p_2=0$ and $\q_0\geq \q_1\geq \q_2=0$. For $k\in\{1,2\}$  let the elements of $G\in\mR^{m \times n},u^{(4,k)}\in\mR^1,\u^{(2,k)}\in\mR^{m\times 1}$, and $\h^{(k)}\in\mR^{n\times 1}$ be independent standard normals. Set $a_k=\sqrt{\p_{k-1}\q_{k-1}-\p_k\q_k}$, $b_k=\sqrt{\p_{k-1}-\p_k}$, $c_k=\sqrt{\q_{k-1}-\q_k}$, and  ${\mathcal U}_k=[u^{(4,k)},\u^{(2,k)},\h^{(k)}]$. Let sets ${\mathcal X}=\{\x^{(1)},\x^{(2)},\dots,\x^{(l)}\}$, $\bar{{\mathcal X}}=\{\bar{\x}^{(1)},\bar{\x}^{(2)},\dots,\bar{\x}^{(l)}\}$, and ${\mathcal Y}=\{\y^{(1)},\y^{(2)},\dots,\y^{(l)}\}$  (with $\x^{(i)},\bar{\x}^{(i)}\in \mR^{n}$ and $\y^{(i)}\in \mR^{m}$), real scalars $\beta$, $p$, and $s$ (with $\beta,p\geq 0$), and function $f_{\bar{\x}^{(i_3)}} (\cdot): \mR^n\rightarrow \mR$ be given. One then has for function
\begin{equation}
